window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["bronco"]=[{"id":"bronco-armor-front-bumper","title":"Front Winch Bumper \u2014 Buying and Installing","vehicle":"bronco","category":"armor","subcategory":"bumper","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":300,"high":950},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","drill","another set of hands"],"skills_required":["measuring and fitment","torquing to spec","basic wiring (for lights)"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["armor","bumper","front bumper","winch mount","recovery","armor_bumper_front"],"parts":[{"name":"Early Bronco front winch bumper (frame-mounted)","vendor":"WFO Concepts / Tom's Offroad","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Grade 8 frame mounting hardware","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":40},{"name":"D-ring shackle tabs (if not integrated)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":30}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco front bumper options","url":"https://www.wfoconcepts.com/"},{"title":"Frame-mount winch bumper fitment","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-recovery-winch-install","bronco-recovery-points"],"summary":"A frame-mounted winch bumper is the foundation for both recovery and front-end protection \u2014 buy the bumper before the winch, because a winch is only as strong as what it's bolted to. The stock early Bronco bumper bolts to the frame horns and is fine for looks, useless for a 9,000-lb winch pulling against it.","description":"A winch bumper does three jobs: it protects the front sheet metal and grille, it gives you rated recovery points, and it provides a flat, frame-tied platform for a winch. The early Bronco's stock bumper does none of these well \u2014 it's light gauge and mounts to the frame horns with hardware that was never meant to take a recovery load.\n\nThe decision up front is whether you want a winch now or later. Buy a winch-ready bumper either way. Retrofitting winch capability into a non-winch bumper means cutting and re-welding, and you'll wish you'd bought the right one the first time.\n\nEarly Bronco winch bumpers mount to the frame horns, usually with a plate that wraps and through-bolts the frame rail for a load path that doesn't rely on the original bumper holes. Look for integrated D-ring tabs rated for recovery (not decorative tabs welded to thin plate) and a winch cradle sized for the winch class you plan to run \u2014 most EB owners run an 8,000\u20139,500 lb winch, which fits a standard bolt pattern.\n\nSteel thickness and frame tie-in matter more than styling. A 3/16\" plate bumper bolted through the frame rail will take a winch load. A tube bumper that looks aggressive but mounts only to the horns will flex and eventually tear.\n\nDon't confuse a true winch bumper with a bolt-on brush guard or stinger added to the stock bumper. A stinger dresses up the factory bumper and may deflect minor brush contact, but it still routes any load through the original frame-horn bolts -- it does not give you a rated recovery point or a platform strong enough for a winch pull. If a listing calls itself a \"bumper guard\" or \"brush guard\" rather than a winch bumper, assume it's cosmetic until the seller can point to a frame tie-in spec.\n\nSupport the truck and remove the stock bumper. Test-fit the new bumper to the frame horns, checking that the winch cradle clears the grille and radiator and that the bumper sits level. Mark and drill any added frame-rail through-holes, deburr and prime them, then bolt up with Grade 8 hardware torqued to spec (typically 75\u201390 ft-lb for 1/2\" bolts). Mount the winch to the cradle before final-torquing the bumper if access is tight.\n\nRecheck torque after the first trail run \u2014 frame-mount hardware settles, and a winch bumper that works loose under a recovery load is a serious hazard.\n\nRadiator and grille clearance is the common fitment problem, especially with an aftermarket radiator or a body lift. Mock it up fully before drilling.\n\nDon't trust decorative D-rings. If the tab isn't welded to plate that ties into the frame, it is not a recovery point \u2014 it will fail under a snatch load and become a projectile. Recovery loads are a safety issue: a tab pulling free under tension can kill someone standing in the line.\n\nCheck for a stated working load limit (WLL) on the tab itself or in the bumper's spec sheet. A common 3/4\" rated D-ring shackle carries roughly 4.75 tons WLL, and the bumper's mounting tab needs to be rated to match or exceed the shackle, not merely machined to fit the shackle pin. A tab that's sized right but not rated is the failure mode that catches people who assumed \"it fits\" means \"it's rated.\"\n\nWeight on the front end affects ride and steering. A heavy winch bumper plus a winch adds 100+ lb forward of the axle, which can require a stiffer front spring to hold ride height.\n\nA frame-mounted early Bronco winch bumper runs $300\u2013650. Premium tube-and-plate bumpers with integrated lights and tabs run $650\u2013950. Budget bolt-on bumpers under $250 typically mount to the horns only \u2014 fine for appearance, not for winching. Add $40 for Grade 8 hardware.\n\nEarly Bronco fab shops (WFO Concepts, Tom's Offroad, James Duff) build bumpers cut to the EB frame. You probably don't need the heaviest plate bumper sold \u2014 a mid-tier frame-tied bumper with rated tabs does everything a trail rig needs without burying the front springs."},{"id":"bronco-armor-rear-bumper","title":"Rear Bumper and Tire Carrier \u2014 Buying and Installing","vehicle":"bronco","category":"armor","subcategory":"bumper","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":1100},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","drill","another set of hands"],"skills_required":["measuring and fitment","torquing to spec","drilling the frame"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["armor","bumper","rear bumper","tire carrier","recovery","armor_bumper_rear"],"parts":[{"name":"Early Bronco rear frame-mount bumper","vendor":"Tom's Offroad / WFO Concepts","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Swing-out tire carrier (gate or frame-pivot)","vendor":"James Duff / Kentrol","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Grade 8 frame mounting hardware","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":40},{"name":"Rated D-ring shackle tabs","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":30}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco rear bumper and carrier options","url":"https://www.tomsoffroad.com/"},{"title":"Swing-out tire carrier fitment","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"},{"title":"Spare tire weight and tailgate limits","url":"https://www.jamesduff.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-armor-front-bumper","bronco-recovery-points","bronco-wt-tire-sizing"],"amazon_links":null,"summary":"Decide whether you need a tire carrier before you buy a rear bumper, because that one choice doubles the price and changes the whole install. If you've gone to 33s or larger, the stock tailgate-mounted spare is past its limit \u2014 the gate hinges and latch were never meant to swing a 70-lb tire, and they wear out and sag. A frame-mounted rear bumper with a swing-out carrier moves that load off the body and onto the frame where it belongs.","description":"A rear bumper on an early Bronco does less protection work than the front \u2014 there's no radiator back there \u2014 but it earns its place three ways: it carries the spare off the tailgate, it gives you a rated rear recovery point for backward pulls and tug straps, and it protects the rear sheet metal and fuel tank area on a departure-angle scrape. The stock rear bumper is light gauge bolted to the frame horns and does none of these jobs under load.\n\nThe honest take: if you're still on 31s with the spare on the tailgate, a plain frame-mount rear bumper with rated tabs is plenty and you can skip the carrier. The moment you run 33s or bigger, the carrier stops being optional \u2014 a sagging tailgate and a cracked hinge area are the predictable result of hanging a big tire on the body.\n\nA rear bumper for the early Bronco bolts to the frame horns, ideally with a plate that ties into the frame rail rather than relying on the original bumper holes. Look for rated D-ring tabs welded to that plate \u2014 same rule as the front, a decorative tab is not a recovery point.\n\nIf you're adding a carrier, the two common styles are a tailgate-pivot carrier (swings with the gate, lighter, simpler) and a frame-pivot swing-out (mounts to the bumper, pivots independently of the gate, carries the most weight). Frame-pivot carriers handle 35s and a jerry can or hi-lift mount; tailgate-pivot units are fine through 33s. Either way, the spare's weight should land on the frame through the bumper, not on the body.\n\nSteel thickness and frame tie-in decide whether the recovery tabs are real. A 3/16\" plate bumper through-bolted to the frame rail will take a rear recovery pull. A tube bumper mounted to the horns alone will flex.\n\nSupport the truck and remove the stock rear bumper. Test-fit the new bumper to the frame horns, checking that it sits level and clears the tailgate swing and the fuel filler. Mark and drill any added frame-rail through-holes, deburr, prime them, then bolt up with Grade 8 hardware torqued to spec (typically 75\u201390 ft-lb for 1/2\" bolts).\n\nIf you're installing a carrier, mount it after the bumper is final-torqued. Set the spindle or hinge so the carrier latches with light pressure and the spare sits centered behind the gate without contacting the body. Check that the carrier latch holds the tire firmly \u2014 a carrier that rattles will crack welds and wear the latch.\n\nRecheck all torque after the first trail run. A loaded carrier puts cantilever leverage on the bumper mounts, and frame-mount hardware settles.\n\nRecovery tabs are a safety item. If the D-ring tab isn't welded to plate that ties into the frame, it is not rated \u2014 under a snatch or tug load it can tear free and become a projectile. Do not run a strap to a decorative tab.\n\nA swing-out carrier that latches loosely is the most common failure. Play in the latch lets the tire bounce, which fatigues the pivot welds and eventually cracks them. Adjust the latch so the carrier seats with tension and check it periodically.\n\nDeparture angle and weight: a heavy bumper-and-carrier combo adds 80\u2013150 lb behind the rear axle, which squats the rear and can reduce departure angle if the bumper hangs low. A carrier also blocks tailgate access until you swing it \u2014 annoying but not dangerous.\n\nA frame-mount rear bumper with rated tabs runs $250\u2013500. Add a tailgate-pivot tire carrier for $150\u2013300, or a frame-pivot swing-out for $400\u2013650 \u2014 so a full bumper-plus-swing-out setup lands at $650\u20131,100. Grade 8 hardware adds about $40, and a pair of rated 3/4\" shackles is $30\u201350.\n\nEarly Bronco fab shops (Tom's Offroad, WFO Concepts, James Duff, Kentrol) build rear bumpers and carriers cut to the EB frame. You probably don't need the heaviest dual-swing setup sold unless you're carrying a 37, a hi-lift, and fuel \u2014 a mid-tier frame-tied bumper with a single swing-out covers a 33\u201335 build without burying the rear springs."},{"id":"bronco-armor-rock-rails","title":"Rocker Protection \u2014 Rock Rails and Sliders","vehicle":"bronco","category":"armor","subcategory":"sliders","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":900},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","drill","welder (for weld-on)","socket set","torque wrench","tape measure"],"skills_required":["measuring and fitment","drilling","welding (weld-on only)"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["armor","sliders","rock rails","rocker panel","body protection","armor_sliders"],"parts":[{"name":"Bolt-on rock sliders (DOM tube, early Bronco specific)","vendor":"WFO Concepts / Ballistic","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Grade 8 mounting hardware kit","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Weld-through primer","vendor":"SEM","price_estimate_usd":18}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco rock slider options","url":"https://www.wfoconcepts.com/"},{"title":"Slider design and frame mounting","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-armor-skid-plates"],"summary":"An early Bronco's rockers and rear quarters are thin, rust-prone, and expensive to replace \u2014 a set of frame-mounted sliders is the single best body-protection dollar you'll spend before you wheel it. Skip the nerf-bar \"steps\" sold as sliders; they bolt to sheet metal and fold the first time they take real weight.","description":"Rock sliders protect the rocker panel and lower body line when you slide off a ledge or set the truck down on a rock. On an early Bronco the rockers are structural-ish but the sheet metal is thin and patch panels run $80\u2013150 a side plus paint, so protecting them pays for itself the first time you bump a rock.\n\nThe distinction that matters: a real slider mounts to the frame and can carry the full weight of the truck so you can pivot on it. A \"nerf bar\" or \"step\" bolts to the body mounts or pinch seam and is decorative. If you can pick a Bronco up by it without bending the body, it's a slider.\n\nFrame-mounted sliders for the early Bronco come bolt-on or weld-on. Bolt-on kits use the existing body-mount holes plus added through-bolts into the frame rail \u2014 adequate for trail use up to moderate rock work. Weld-on sliders are stronger and let you delete the body-line gap, but require a welder and clean, rust-free frame to weld to. On a 50-year-old truck, inspect the frame first: pitted or rotted frame steel will not hold a weld or a bolt.\n\nDOM (drawn-over-mandrel) tubing is the standard material \u2014 1.75\" x .120\" wall is typical for a Bronco's weight. Avoid sliders built from ERW pipe or thin-wall tube; they dent and crush.\n\nSupport the truck on jack stands at the frame, not the axle, so the body sits at ride height. Test-fit the slider against the rocker and frame, marking the mounting points. For bolt-on kits, drill the frame for the through-bolts using the slider's plates as a template \u2014 deburr and prime every hole to stop rust starting at the bare metal. Torque the frame bolts to the kit spec (typically 75\u201390 ft-lb for 1/2\" Grade 8). For weld-on, tack first, check the slider is level and parallel to the rocker on both sides, then weld out and prime the welds.\n\nRun the slider tight to the body with a slight outward kick at the bottom so a rock rolls off rather than punching up into the rocker. Ground clearance matters: a slider hanging below the frame is the next thing to catch, so tuck it up.\n\nThe most common failure is mounting to rusty frame steel \u2014 a bolt or weld into pitted metal tears out under load and takes a chunk of frame with it. Inspect and, if needed, plate the frame before mounting.\n\nDon't bolt sliders only to the body mounts and call it done. Body mounts are rubber-isolated and will shear. The slider has to tie into the frame rail itself.\n\nWatch your step-in height. Sliders that kick out too far become a shin-barker and snag on off-camber rocks. Tuck them close to the body.\n\nA quality bolt-on early Bronco slider set runs $350\u2013550. Weld-on kits and custom fab run $600\u2013900. Budget \"step slider\" bars are $150\u2013250 but mount to sheet metal \u2014 they protect nothing. Add $35 for Grade 8 hardware and $18 for weld-through primer.\n\nReputable early Bronco-specific fab shops (WFO Concepts, Ballistic Fabrication, James Duff) sell sliders cut for the EB body line. A general-purpose slider will fight you on fitment. You probably don't need the most expensive set on the market \u2014 a mid-tier DOM bolt-on slider protects the body fine for everything short of dedicated rock crawling."},{"id":"bronco-armor-skid-plates","title":"Underbody Armor \u2014 Engine, Transmission, and Transfer Case Skids","vehicle":"bronco","category":"armor","subcategory":"skid_plates","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":7},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":800},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","drill","socket set","torque wrench","tape measure","angle grinder (for trimming)"],"skills_required":["measuring and fitment","drilling","working under a supported vehicle"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["armor","skid plates","underbody","transfer case","oil pan","armor_skid"],"parts":[{"name":"Transfer case / transmission skid plate (early Bronco, Dana 20)","vendor":"Tom's Offroad / WFO Concepts","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Engine oil pan / steering box skid","vendor":"Various EB fab","price_estimate_usd":160},{"name":"Grade 8 mounting hardware kit","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":35}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco skid plate options","url":"https://www.tomsoffroad.com/"},{"title":"Dana 20 transfer case protection","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-armor-rock-rails"],"summary":"The transfer case skid is the one to buy first \u2014 a hit to the bottom of a Dana 20 cracks the case and ends your day, and a $220 plate is far cheaper than a transfer case. Engine and steering-box skids come next; the gas tank skid matters most if you've lifted and exposed the tank.","description":"The early Bronco hangs its most expensive-to-replace parts low and unprotected: the oil pan, the Dana 20 transfer case, and the steering box all sit where a rock finds them. A skid plate spreads the impact across the frame instead of letting a point load punch a hole.\n\nThe honest priority order is transfer case first, then engine/steering box, then fuel tank. The Dana 20's case is cast iron and cracks on a sharp hit; once it's cracked you're walking. The oil pan dents and can split a seam. The steering box bracket bends, which changes your steering geometry mid-trail.\n\nSkid plates for the early Bronco are sold as a system or piece by piece. A transfer case / transmission skid bolts to the frame crossmember and the body mounts, cradling the bottom of the Dana 20. Engine skids protect the oil pan and often the steering box in one plate. Fuel tank skids matter once a lift exposes the tank below the frame rail.\n\nMaterial is the deciding factor. 3/16\" steel is the practical minimum for a skid that takes real rock hits; 1/4\" is better for dedicated crawling but adds weight low and forward. Aluminum skids exist and save weight, but they gouge and deform on hard hits \u2014 fine for forest-road protection, less so for rocks.\n\nSupport the truck on jack stands at the frame so the drivetrain sits at normal load. Test-fit the skid against the crossmember and body mounts, checking for driveshaft and exhaust clearance through the full suspension travel \u2014 a skid that contacts the driveshaft at flex will destroy both. Mark and drill mounting holes, deburr and prime every hole to stop rust at the bare metal, then bolt up with Grade 8 hardware torqued to the kit spec (typically 75\u201390 ft-lb for 1/2\" bolts).\n\nLeave drain access. A good transfer case skid has a hole or a removable section over the drain plug so you can change fluid without dropping the whole plate. If yours doesn't, you'll be pulling the skid every fluid change.\n\nDriveshaft clearance at full droop and full compression is the failure most people miss. Cycle the suspension before you call it done.\n\nGround clearance is a trade. A 1/4\" plate hanging flat is now your lowest point and your new high-centering surface. Tuck skids tight to the components and angle the leading edge so the truck slides over rather than catching.\n\nTrapped mud and water behind a skid rusts the frame and crossmember from the inside. Drill weep holes at the low points and clean behind the skids after wet trips.\n\nA transfer case skid runs $180\u2013280. An engine/steering-box skid is $130\u2013220. A full early Bronco skid system runs $500\u2013800. Budget universal weld-on plate is cheaper but rarely fits the EB drivetrain without fabrication. Add $35 for Grade 8 hardware.\n\nEarly Bronco-specific shops (Tom's Offroad, WFO Concepts, James Duff) cut skids to the EB frame and Dana 20. You probably don't need 1/4\" plate everywhere \u2014 3/16\" protects everything short of dedicated rock crawling, and the weight saved low and forward helps the truck's manners."},{"id":"bronco-axle-9in-rear-swap","title":"Ford 9-Inch Rear Axle \u2014 The Default Upgrade","vehicle":"bronco","category":"axles","subcategory":"axle swap","type":"upgrade","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":20},"cost_usd":{"low":900,"high":3500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","welder (for spring perches)","angle grinder","tape measure","driveline angle gauge"],"skills_required":["welding","leaf spring mounting","driveline measurement","brake line fabrication"],"build_tiers":["weekend_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["ford9","axle swap","rear axle","9-inch","axles","regear"],"parts":[{"name":"Ford 9-inch housing (Explorer or aftermarket)","vendor":"Salvage / Currie","price_estimate_usd":400},{"name":"New axle shafts (35-spline, alloy)","vendor":"Yukon / Moser","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Spring perches (weld-on)","vendor":"Ruffstuff","price_estimate_usd":40},{"name":"Disc brake conversion kit","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Detroit Locker or Truetrac carrier","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":600}],"citations":[{"title":"Ford 9-inch Swap Guides \u2014 fullsizebronco.com","url":"https://www.fullsizebronco.com/forum/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-axle-dana44-rear","bronco-axle-locker-options"],"summary":"The Ford 9-inch is the rear axle most serious early Bronco builds end up running. It is stronger than the factory rear, has a removable third member that makes gear and locker changes far less painful, and the aftermarket support is endless. If you are regearing and adding a locker anyway, swapping to a 9-inch first is often the better spend.","description":"The early Bronco shipped with a Ford 9-inch rear from the factory in many configurations, but axle width, gear ratio, and spline count vary, and decades of swaps mean you cannot assume what is under any given truck. A purpose-built 9-inch \u2014 whether a salvage Explorer housing narrowed and re-perched, or a new aftermarket housing from Currie or Dynatrac \u2014 gives you a known starting point.\n\nThe defining feature is the dropout third member. The entire ring-and-pinion, carrier, and locker live in a removable center section that bolts to the front of the housing. You can set up a spare third member on the bench, then swap gear ratios or add a locker in an afternoon without disturbing the axle shafts or brakes. No other common axle makes drivetrain changes this approachable.\n\nStrength scales with build. A 28-spline 9-inch is adequate for stock-ish tires; 31-spline handles 35s and moderate wheeling; 35-spline alloy shafts in a nodular case carry 37s and abuse. Match the spline count to your tire size and how hard you wheel.\n\nThis is a fabrication-adjacent job, not a bolt-in. The work breaks down as:\n\n1. **Width and perch location.** The new housing must match your wheel mounting width and your leaf spring spacing. A salvage housing usually needs to be narrowed and always needs new spring perches welded at the correct location and pinion angle.\n\n2. **Pinion angle.** With the perches tacked, measure the driveline angle before final welding. Get this wrong and you will fight vibration or a worn U-joint for the life of the build.\n\n3. **Brakes.** Plan a disc conversion at the same time. Doing brakes while the axle is out is far less work than revisiting it later, and a heavier, more capable rear deserves better stopping.\n\n4. **Gears and traction.** Set the third member up with your target ratio and a locker before it goes in. See the gear ratio and locker guides for ratio selection by tire size.\n\nSalvage yards sell both, and they get confused constantly. The quickest tell is the differential cover: the 9-inch uses a round, 10-bolt cover with a removable third member visible as a separate casting bolted to the front face of the housing. The 8.8 uses an oval, 10-bolt cover and has no separate third member -- the ring and pinion mount directly in the housing, so there's nothing to unbolt and drop out for a bench gear change. If you can't tell from the cover shape, check the nose of the housing for a third-member bolt pattern; the 9-inch has one, the 8.8 doesn't. An 8.8 out of a salvage Explorer or Ranger is a legitimate cheaper option for a milder build, but you're trading away the swap-in-an-afternoon serviceability that's the whole reason most builders reach for a 9-inch in the first place.\n\nOnce the third member is set up, torque it to the housing at 32-35 ft-lb in a star pattern -- running the bolts down in a circle puts an uneven clamp load on the gasket surface, distorts it, and starts a leak. Torque axle shaft retainers (C-clip style or bearing-retainer style, depending on what your shafts use) to factory spec before the housing goes back in the truck. Both steps are tempting to shortcut when you're eager to get the axle installed, and both are miserable to redo once the housing is under the truck and full of gear oil.\n\nPinion angle and perch placement are where these builds go wrong. Measure twice, tack, verify on the ground at ride height, then weld. A driveline angle gauge is worth the small cost.\n\nIf you are buying a salvage Explorer 8.8 instead of a true 9-inch, understand the tradeoff: the 8.8 is cheaper and adequate for many builds, but it lacks the dropout serviceability that makes the 9-inch worth the premium. For a build that will see multiple gear or locker changes over its life, the 9-inch pays you back.\n\n- Salvage 9-inch housing, re-perched: $400\u2013$700\n- New aftermarket housing (Currie/Dynatrac): $1,200\u2013$2,000\n- 35-spline alloy shafts: $450\n- Disc brake conversion: $350\n- Third member with locker and gears, set up: $1,000\u2013$1,600\n- Gear set-up labor if you're not doing it yourself: $300\u2013$500 per axle\n\nBudget $900 for a salvage-based build you assemble yourself, $3,500 for a new-housing build with premium internals and shop setup. This is a long-horizon upgrade \u2014 done once, it carries the truck through every future tire and gearing change."},{"id":"bronco-axle-dana44-front","title":"Dana 44 Front Axle \u2014 Stock Specs and Weak Points","vehicle":"bronco","category":"axles","subcategory":"front","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["grease gun","torque wrench","dial indicator","tape measure"],"skills_required":[],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1971-1977"],"tags":["axle","dana44","dana30","front","solid-axle","specs"],"parts":[{"name":"Spicer 5-760X U-joint (front axleshaft)","part_number":"5-760X","vendor":"Spicer","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Warn Premium manual locking hubs (pair, Dana 44)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Timken front wheel bearing and race set","part_number":"SET2/SET6","vendor":"Timken","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Closed-knuckle inner axle seal and kingpin rebuild kit","vendor":"Wild Horses 4x4","price_estimate_usd":140}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco front axle ID: Dana 30 vs Dana 44 \u2014 Wild Horses 4x4","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/early-bronco-front-axle-tech"},{"title":"The Early Bronco Dana 44 \u2014 The Ranger Station","url":"https://www.therangerstation.com/tech/the-early-bronco-dana-44/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-axle-gear-ratio","bronco-axle-locker-options","bronco-maint-closed-knuckle-service"],"summary":"The early Bronco's front axle is a closed-knuckle (kingpin) Dana 44 from mid-1971 through 1977 \u2014 strong enough for 35\" tires and moderate trail use. Trucks from 1966 to mid-1971 carry the smaller Dana 30 instead. The Dana 44's weak point is the inner axleshaft U-joint and the kingpin/knuckle seals, all of which live or die on regular service. Keep them greased and inspected and the axle outlasts almost everything bolted to it.","description":"Ford ran two front axles under the first-generation Bronco. From 1966 to mid-1971 the truck used the **Dana 30**; in mid-1971 Ford switched to the larger **Dana 44**, which carried through to the end of the run in 1977. Both are closed-knuckle, kingpin designs \u2014 the steering knuckle is a sealed ball housing, not the open ball-joint knuckle that appeared on later Ford trucks. The 1976\u20131977 Dana 44 also gained factory front disc brakes; everything earlier used drums.\n\nIdentifying which you have matters before you buy parts: the Dana 44 has a larger center casting, larger U-joints, and (76\u201377) disc brakes, while the Dana 30 is visibly smaller. If you are unsure, measure the ring gear or count the cover bolts against a known reference before ordering gears or shafts.\n\nDana 44 key specs:\n- Ring gear diameter: 8.5 in\n- Knuckle type: closed-knuckle, kingpin (sealed ball housing)\n- U-joint: Spicer 5-760X on the front axleshafts \u2014 run greaseable joints\n- Hubs: manual locking hubs (Warn or Spicer); worn factory hubs should be replaced\n- Brakes: drum through 1975, disc on 1976\u20131977\n- Differential: open from the factory; limited-slip and locking options available\n\n1. **Inner axleshaft U-joints.** The inner joint on each front shaft takes the worst of the abuse \u2014 steering load plus drive torque at an angle. A dry joint fails without warning and can take the shaft ears with it. Run greaseable Spicer 5-760X joints and service them every season; carry a spare on a long trip.\n2. **Kingpins and knuckle seals.** The closed-knuckle ball housing relies on its outer felt/rubber seal to keep grease in and water out. A torn seal lets differential oil and trail water mix into the knuckle, which then chews the kingpin bearings. This is its own job \u2014 the [closed-knuckle Dana 44 front service guide](/db/bronco/bronco-maint-closed-knuckle-service/) walks the kingpin and seal rebuild in detail.\n3. **Locking hubs.** Original mid-1970s hubs crack or seize. Warn Premium replacements are the standard upgrade and re-engage reliably in cold and mud.\n4. **Wheel bearings.** Tapered roller bearings, pre-1980 Ford pattern. Re-pack every 2 years or 20,000 miles with quality Timken bearings \u2014 cheap import sets run loose and wear fast.\n\nThe Dana 44 front is comfortable under 35\" tires and moderate rock and trail work. Past 35\", or with an aggressive locker and a heavy right foot, the limiting parts become the U-joints and the open differential rather than the housing itself. Stock gear ratios were 3.50, 3.73, or 4.09 depending on year and engine \u2014 and front and rear ratios must match exactly. Once you move past 33\" tires, plan to regear; the tire-size-to-ratio logic is laid out in the [gear ratio selection guide](/db/bronco/bronco-axle-gear-ratio/). For traction, the front axle is the higher-value end to lock on a closed-knuckle truck; the tradeoffs between selectable, automatic, and limited-slip units are covered in the [locker options guide](/db/bronco/bronco-axle-locker-options/).\n\nInspect the knuckle ball seals at every oil change \u2014 a weeping seal is cheap to fix and expensive to ignore. Pull and grease the U-joints at the start of each wheeling season. Re-pack wheel bearings on the 2-year/20k schedule. Pop the locking hubs apart annually to clean and re-grease the internals; grit and old grease are what seize them. None of this is expensive, and it is the difference between a Dana 44 that runs for decades and one that strands you on the trail.","pros":["closed-knuckle Dana 44 is strong enough for 35\" tires","forged housing with a large aftermarket, including lockers and gears","greaseable U-joints available and inexpensive","76\u201377 trucks came with factory front discs"],"cons":["open differential from the factory","inner U-joints and kingpin seals demand regular service or they fail","1966\u2013mid-1971 trucks have the smaller, weaker Dana 30 \u2014 confirm before buying parts","practical tire ceiling around 35\" without housing and shaft upgrades"]},{"id":"bronco-axle-dana44-rear","title":"Dana 44 Rear Axle \u2014 Stock Specs and Upgrades","vehicle":"bronco","category":"axles","subcategory":"rear","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":[],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["axle","dana44","rear","solid-axle","specs"],"parts":[{"name":"Eaton Detroit Truetrac for Dana 44 (27-spline)","part_number":"913A395","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":340},{"name":"ARB Air Locker for Dana 44 rear (30-spline)","part_number":"RD100","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":580},{"name":"Yukon chromoly rear axleshafts (30-spline)","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":320}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco Dana 44 rear axle identification","url":"https://www.broncodriver.com/axles"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Dana 44 rear is the right axle for a stock-to-mild build. Under 35\" and moderate trail pressure, the stock shafts hold up. Push harder and upgrade to Yukon chromoly shafts before you break one on the trail.","description":"All 1966\u20131977 Broncos use a full-floating Dana 44 rear axle. Full-floating means the axleshafts carry only rotational load \u2014 the housing bears the vehicle's weight \u2014 so shaft removal doesn't require jacking the vehicle.\n\nKey specs:\n- Ring gear diameter: 8.5 in\n- Axleshaft: 27-spline (1966\u20131972) or 30-spline (1973\u20131977)\n- Differential: open from factory; Ford Limited-Slip (Traction-Lok) available on some years\n- Housing: 3-bolt axleshaft flange\n\n**Spline count matters:** 27-spline shafts are weaker. If you're running more than 33\" tires or using a locker aggressively, upgrade to a 30-spline carrier and Yukon chromoly shafts ($320/pair).\n\n**Differential upgrades:**\n- Eaton Detroit Truetrac (helical gear LSD): $340. Transparent operation, no maintenance, good for daily driving and mild trail. Pairs well with a front locker.\n- ARB Air Locker: $580 + compressor. True positive lock front and rear; the trail standard for serious use. Requires air line installation and a compressor.\n- Aussie Locker (automatic locking): $200. Budget option. Clicks and clunks but locks reliably. Fine for dedicated trail trucks.","pros":["full-floating design (shafts pull without jacking)","30-spline shafts are strong","locker options widely available","good parts availability"],"cons":["27-spline early trucks are weak \u2014 identify before wheeling hard","open from factory","8.5\" ring gear limits maximum pinion torque vs. Dana 60"]},{"id":"bronco-axle-gear-ratio","title":"Gear Ratio Selection for Tire Size","vehicle":"bronco","category":"axles","subcategory":"gearing","type":"reference","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":650},"tools_required":["ring and pinion setup tools","inch-pound torque wrench","dial indicator","gear marking compound","bearing press"],"skills_required":["differential setup","backlash measurement"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["gear ratio","regear","tires","dana44","gearing"],"parts":[{"name":"Yukon 4.56 ring and pinion set (Dana 44)","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":290},{"name":"Yukon 4.88 ring and pinion set (Dana 44)","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":290},{"name":"Dana 44 master install kit","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":95}],"citations":[{"title":"Gear ratio calculator","url":"https://www.4lo.com/gear-ratio-calculator"},{"title":"Yukon Dana 44 gear sets","url":"https://www.yukongear.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-axle-locker-options","bronco-dt-dana20-tc","bronco-wt-tire-sizing"],"summary":"For every 1\" increase in tire diameter beyond stock, you lose roughly 3% of your effective gear ratio. Regearing is not optional once you go past 33\" tires \u2014 it's a question of when, not whether. Match the ratio to your tire size from the table below, run identical gears front and rear, and pay a driveline shop to set them up unless you own the tools and have done it before.","description":"The early Bronco left the factory with 3.50 or 4.11 gears depending on year and option. Those ratios were chosen for ~30\" bias-ply tires. Bolt on 33s or 35s and the engine has to work harder to turn the taller tire, low-range crawl suffers, and highway RPM drops into a lugging zone that hurts both throttle response and cooling.\n\nThis is the early Bronco starting point with the stock Dana 20 transfer case and its 2.03:1 low range:\n\n| Tire Size | Minimum Ratio | Recommended |\n|-----------|--------------|-------------|\n| 31\" | 3.73 | 3.73 or 4.09 |\n| 33\" | 4.09 | 4.09 or 4.56 |\n| 35\" | 4.56 | 4.56 or 4.88 |\n| 37\" | 4.88 | 4.88 or 5.13 |\n\nPick the recommended column if the truck spends real time on the trail or carries weight; the minimum column is for a mostly-street truck that occasionally wheels. Confirm your starting tire diameter against the [tire sizing guide](/db/bronco/bronco-wt-tire-sizing/) before you calculate \u2014 a \"35\" tire often measures 34.3\" loaded, which changes the math.\n\nThe Dana 20's 2.03:1 low range is modest by modern standards \u2014 many newer transfer cases run 2.7:1 to 4:1. Steeper axle gearing is how the early Bronco compensates: it does the work the transfer case can't. A truck on 35s with 4.88 axle gears and the stock Dana 20 has a usable crawl ratio in the low 30s, which is workable for moderate trails. The same truck left at 3.50 gears crawls in the high teens and will smoke the clutch or stall on a ledge. If you want a deeper crawl without regearing further, the alternative is a transfer case low-range upgrade \u2014 see the [Dana 20 transfer case guide](/db/bronco/bronco-dt-dana20-tc/).\n\nBoth axles must run identical ratios \u2014 a mismatch binds the driveline in 4WD and destroys gears or the transfer case. The Dana 44 front and rear on the early Bronco share the same 4.56, 4.88, and 5.13 options, so matching is a parts-ordering discipline, not a fitment problem. Yukon, G2, and Nitro all make complete Dana 44 sets; buy both axles' gears from the same manufacturer and the same batch where possible. Plan to install a [selectable or limited-slip locker](/db/bronco/bronco-axle-locker-options/) at the same time \u2014 pulling the third member apart twice doubles the labor.\n\nRing and pinion setup is the most precision-sensitive job on the truck. It requires a bearing press, a dial indicator, and gear marking compound, and backlash has to land within 0.006\"\u20130.010\" for a Dana 44 with the correct pinion-depth pattern. Get it wrong and the gears whine, overheat, and self-destruct inside 500 miles. Unless you own the tools and have set up a differential before, this is the job to hand to a driveline shop. Labor runs about $300\u2013450 per axle, which is cheaper than a second set of gears after a bad home setup.","pros":["restores throttle response and crawl ratio with larger tires","drastically improves low-range capability on the weak Dana 20","pulls highway RPM out of the lugging zone, helping cooling and economy"],"cons":["requires matched front and rear sets \u2014 two separate jobs","proper setup needs specialized tools and experience","incorrect backlash or pinion depth destroys gears within 500 miles"]},{"id":"bronco-axle-locker-options","title":"Locker Options \u2014 Selectable, Automatic, and Limited-Slip","vehicle":"bronco","category":"axles","subcategory":"traction","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":1400},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","dial indicator (for setup verification)","bearing puller","seal driver"],"skills_required":["axle service","carrier bearing preload","backlash measurement"],"build_tiers":["weekend_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["locker","traction","ARB","Detroit","dana44","ford9","axles"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Air Locker (Dana 44 front, 30-spline)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"Detroit Locker (Ford 9-inch rear, 31-spline)","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":650},{"name":"Lock-Right / Aussie Locker (drop-in)","vendor":"Powertrax","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"ARB compressor kit","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":320}],"citations":[{"title":"Traction Device Types \u2014 ARB Education","url":"https://www.arb.com.au/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-axle-gear-ratio"],"summary":"If you wheel an early Bronco on anything past a graded fire road, an open differential is the first thing holding you back. A selectable locker in the rear delivers the biggest single traction gain you can buy. Front locker comes next, and it should be selectable so the truck still steers predictably on the street.","description":"An open differential sends power to the wheel with the least grip. Lift one tire off a rock and that tire spins while the planted one does nothing. Lockers force both wheels on an axle to turn together, so the tire with grip gets power.\n\nThere are three honest categories, and the right pick depends on how the truck gets used.\n\nA selectable locker stays fully open until you flip a switch, then locks 100%. On the street it behaves like a stock open diff \u2014 no tire chirp in turns, no driveline bind. On the trail you lock it the instant you need it.\n\nThis is the pick for a truck that drives to the trail under its own power. The ARB Air Locker is the long-standing choice for early Broncos and needs an air source \u2014 either the ARB compressor or an existing onboard air system. Plan for the compressor cost in your budget, plus running an air line and a switch.\n\nTradeoff: more parts, more install time, and a small failure surface (air lines, seals). The payoff is street manners no other locker matches.\n\nAutomatic lockers are locked by default and \"unlock\" by ratcheting one wheel ahead of the other in turns. The full-case Detroit Locker is durable and a long-time favorite for the Ford 9-inch rear. Drop-in units like the Lock-Right and Aussie Locker reuse your existing carrier, which keeps cost and install time down.\n\nThe catch is street behavior. Automatic lockers bang, click, and can push the rear end wide in tight low-speed turns, especially in the wet. In a light early Bronco that behavior is noticeable. Many owners run an automatic in the rear and accept the manners; fewer want one in the front, because it affects steering.\n\nA limited-slip (clutch-type like a Trac-Lok, or gear-type like a Truetrac) biases torque toward the wheel with grip without fully locking. It is the mildest option \u2014 good for a mostly-street truck that sees occasional dirt, weak for hard articulation where a tire leaves the ground entirely. Truetrac gear units are maintenance-free; clutch units wear and eventually need a rebuild.\n\nMost early Bronco builds land here: automatic or selectable locker in the Ford 9-inch rear first, then a selectable ARB in the Dana 44 front when budget allows. Lock the rear and you solve the majority of traction problems. Add the front and the truck climbs almost anything its tires can reach.\n\nYou probably don't need a front locker if you mostly run graded trails and fire roads \u2014 a rear locker alone covers that. Save the front money for tires and recovery gear instead.\n\nInstalling a carrier \u2014 locker or otherwise \u2014 disturbs the ring-and-pinion setup. Drop-in lockers that reuse the factory carrier preserve your existing backlash and pinion depth, which is why they install faster. Full-case lockers (ARB, Detroit) replace the carrier entirely, so the gears must be re-shimmed and the backlash and pattern verified with a dial indicator.\n\nIf you have not set up a differential before, this is the step to hand to a shop. A locker installed with the wrong backlash will howl, run hot, and fail early. Budget $250\u2013$400 for shop setup labor if you supply the parts.\n\n- Drop-in automatic locker (Lock-Right / Aussie), per axle: $350\n- Detroit Locker, Ford 9-inch full case: $650\n- ARB Air Locker, Dana 44 front: $1,100 plus $320 compressor kit\n- Shop setup labor (full-case install): $250\u2013$400 per axle\n\n\nAlways confirm spline count and carrier break before ordering \u2014 Dana 44 fronts and Ford 9-inch rears come in more than one spline configuration depending on the prior build history of the truck."},{"id":"bronco-locker-install","title":"Installing a Locker \u2014 Ford 9-Inch Rear and Dana Front","vehicle":"bronco","category":"axles","subcategory":"lockers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":9},"cost_usd":{"low":300,"high":1300},"tools_required":["dial indicator with magnetic base","inch-pound torque wrench","foot-pound torque wrench","bearing puller/press","case spreader (front Dana only)","snap ring pliers","gear marking compound","brass drift"],"skills_required":["axle setup","advanced wrenching"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["locker","axle-upgrade","drivetrain"],"parts":[{"name":"Rear locker, Ford 9-inch (spline count must match your shafts)","vendor":"ARB Air Locker / Eaton Detroit Locker / Yukon Grizzly / Auburn","price_estimate_usd":700},{"name":"Front locker, Dana 30 or Dana 44 (selectable recommended)","vendor":"ARB Air Locker / Yukon Zip","price_estimate_usd":800},{"name":"Carrier bearing and master setup kit (per axle)","vendor":"Yukon / Wild Horses 4x4 / Timken","price_estimate_usd":130},{"name":"Air compressor and locker plumbing kit (selectable only)","vendor":"ARB CKSA12 / ARB CKMA12","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Spare Ford 9-inch third member, prebuilt with locker and gears","vendor":"Currie / Wild Horses 4x4 / local axle shop","price_estimate_usd":1900}],"citations":[{"title":"Wild Horses 4x4 \u2014 early Bronco differentials & lockers (ARB/Yukon/Eaton)","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/early-bronco-differentials-lockers"},{"title":"Kincer Chassis \u2014 understanding the axles on an early Ford Bronco","url":"https://kincerchassis.com/axles-on-an-early-ford-bronco/"},{"title":"Sexton Offroad \u2014 ultimate early Bronco parts guide","url":"https://www.sextonoffroad.com/blogs/4x4-parts-accessories/the-ultimate-early-ford-bronco-parts-guide-build-your-dream-1966-1977-rig"}],"alternatives":["bronco-axle-locker-options","bronco-regear","bronco-axle-9in-rear-swap","bronco-axle-dana44-front"],"summary":"Lock the rear first \u2014 it returns more climbing capability per dollar than anything else you can bolt to an early Bronco. Add a selectable front afterward for steep, off-camber ledges where an open front stalls the climb. Do the locker at the same time as a regear so you pay the precision setup labor once, and check your axle shaft spline count before you order anything, because a locker behind tired 28-spline shafts finds their limit fast. The companion [locker options guide](/db/bronco/bronco-axle-locker-options/) compares the types; this card is the build decision and the install.","description":"There is a persistent piece of misinformation in the early Bronco world worth clearing up before you spend money, because it changes what parts you order:\n\n**Every early Bronco, 1966 through 1977, came from the factory with a Ford 9-inch rear axle.** There is no factory Dana rear in these trucks. The Dana in an early Bronco is the *front* axle: a closed-knuckle **Dana 30 from 1966 to 1971**, and a closed-knuckle **Dana 44 from mid-1971 through 1977**. Both front axles use kingpins rather than ball joints, which is why front-end service on these trucks looks nothing like a modern solid-axle truck \u2014 see the [closed-knuckle service guide](/db/bronco/bronco-maint-closed-knuckle-service/).\n\nThree practical consequences follow from that:\n\n1. **You order a rear locker by 9-inch spline count, not by \"Dana 44.\"** Most early Broncos run 28-spline small-bearing shafts; upgraded trucks run 31 or 35-spline. ARB, Eaton, and Yukon all catalog the Ford 9-inch by spline count, and the wrong count is a returned part. Pull an axle shaft and count, or measure \u2014 do not assume.\n2. **The rear housing is not your limiting factor.** The 9-inch is strong enough that people race behind it. Your limit is shaft strength and, secondarily, the carrier.\n3. **The front axle is usually what breaks on 35s.** A Dana 30 with a locker under a 35-inch tire is the classic early Bronco failure. If that is your truck, the [Dana 44 front](/db/bronco/bronco-axle-dana44-front/) is the upgrade to plan around.\n\nThis is the single most useful thing to understand about locking an early Bronco, and it is specific to the Ford 9-inch.\n\nThe 9-inch uses a **removable carrier** \u2014 the ring gear, pinion, carrier bearings, and differential all live in a cast third member that unbolts from the front of the housing as a unit. The Dana front axle does not work this way; it is an integral-carrier design where all the setup happens inside the housing, on your back, under the truck.\n\nThat difference gives you an option nobody talks about often enough: **buy or build a second third member.** Instead of tearing down the axle in your truck, you acquire a spare 9-inch center section, have it set up on a bench with the locker and the gears you want, and then swap it in. The physical swap is a few hours \u2014 drain, unbolt the driveshaft, pull the axle shafts, unbolt ten nuts, slide the old one out, new gasket, slide the new one in.\n\nWhat that buys you:\n\n- **The truck is off the road for an afternoon, not two weekends.**\n- **The setup work happens on a bench**, where a shop can do it properly and where you can check backlash and pattern without lying in gravel.\n- **You keep the original third member intact.** If the build changes direction, or you sell the truck, or the new setup develops a whine, you have a known-good spare on the shelf. On a 50-year-old vehicle, that is real insurance.\n- A prebuilt 9-inch third member with a locker and gears runs roughly **$1,600 to $2,400** depending on the locker and gear brand. Compare that against $700 for the locker, $320 for gears, $130 for the master kit, and $500 to $800 in setup labor \u2014 the prebuilt unit is often within a couple hundred dollars of doing it the hard way, and it is dramatically less downtime.\n\nDo not extend that logic to the front. The Dana 30 or 44 front has to be set up in the housing, in the truck or on a stand.\n\n**Rear \u2014 automatic (Eaton Detroit Locker, Yukon Grizzly).** Mechanical, no air line, no switch, and it locks whenever it is loaded. On a light, short-wheelbase truck it is very effective and quite noticeable: it ratchets and clicks in tight pavement turns and can push the rear around in the wet. On a Bronco that mostly wheels, this is a defensible and cheaper choice at roughly **$450 to $700**.\n\n**Rear \u2014 selectable air locker (ARB).** Fully open until you flip the switch, so the truck drives and steers normally on the road and locks only when you want it. Costs more once you add the compressor: about **$1,000 to $1,400** all-in for the locker, compressor, and plumbing. The right answer if the Bronco sees meaningful street miles.\n\n**Rear \u2014 limited slip (Auburn, Truetrac).** The mild option for a street-biased truck. Adds traction without changing manners. Not enough for hard rock, but a real improvement over open at **$400 to $600**.\n\n**Front \u2014 selectable, and effectively only selectable.** An automatic locker in the front of an early Bronco fights the steering, and on the many trucks still running a manual box or a modestly assisted conversion it makes low-speed maneuvering genuinely difficult. Run an ARB up front, and leave it open until you need it.\n\nWild Horses and the other early Bronco specialists stock ARB, Yukon, Eaton, and Auburn units for both the 9-inch rear and the Dana fronts, so availability is not the constraint \u2014 the constraint is getting the spline count and the carrier series right.\n\nThis is precision work. The steps below are the shape of the job, not a substitute for the gear manufacturer's setup specifications.\n\n1. **Count splines and confirm the carrier series.** Pull one rear axle shaft and count the splines at the differential end. Order to that number.\n2. **Drain the axle** and catch the old gear oil. Look at what comes out \u2014 glitter means the ring and pinion is already shedding, and that decides whether you are also regearing.\n3. **Rear: pull the third member.** Remove the driveshaft, pull both axle shafts, unbolt the ten nuts on the housing face, and slide the carrier out. Have a helper; it is heavier than it looks.\n4. **Rear: set up on the bench.** Press the carrier bearings onto the new locker, install with the correct shim stack, and set **backlash to the gear manufacturer's spec \u2014 typically 0.008 to 0.012 inch** \u2014 measured with a dial indicator on the ring gear face. Set carrier bearing preload per spec.\n5. **Check the tooth contact pattern** with marking compound before anything is torqued for the last time. Pattern is the real verdict; backlash is only one input to it. Adjust pinion depth with shims until the pattern sits correctly on both drive and coast sides.\n6. **Reinstall with a new gasket**, torque the housing nuts evenly, and refill with the correct gear oil. A limited slip needs friction modifier; a locker generally does not \u2014 read the instructions rather than defaulting.\n7. **Front: work in the housing.** Remove the carrier with a case spreader, install the locker, and repeat the backlash, preload, and pattern process. The closed-knuckle front means the axle shafts and knuckle work come apart differently than a modern axle \u2014 plan on new knuckle seals and inspect the kingpin bearings while you are in there.\n8. **Air locker plumbing, if applicable.** The air line exits a bulkhead fitting at the differential and runs to the compressor. Route it away from the exhaust, away from the steering linkage, and away from anything that moves at articulation, and secure it every 12 inches. On a truck this old, mount the compressor somewhere dry and give it a properly fused feed with a good ground \u2014 see the [charging and wiring guides](/db/bronco/bronco-elec-wiring-harness/) if the harness is original.\n9. **Break it in.** Drive gently for the first 30 to 50 miles, let the axle cool, and avoid towing. Change the gear oil after the first 500 miles to clear the break-in metal.\n\n**A locker does not make weak shafts stronger \u2014 it makes them break.** An open differential lets a spinning tire relieve load. A locker refuses to, and sends that torque into whichever shaft has grip. If you are running 28-spline small-bearing shafts, a locker plus 35s plus a V8 is the combination that snaps them. Budget 31-spline chromoly shafts with a large-bearing conversion, roughly $550 to $800 a pair, at the same time. On a semi-float 9-inch, a shaft that lets go outboard of the bearing can allow the wheel to leave the truck, so treat this as a safety item rather than a durability nicety.\n\n**Regear at the same time or accept paying twice.** The carrier is out either way. Setup labor is the expensive part of both jobs, and combining them costs an extra $320 in gears and $130 in a master kit rather than another $500 to $800 in labor. See the [regear guide](/db/bronco/bronco-regear/).\n\n**Match ratios front to rear exactly.** The Dana 20 transfer case splits drive evenly. A ratio mismatch binds the driveline and destroys parts.\n\n**Know when to hand it off.** Ring and pinion setup is the most tolerance-critical work on the truck, and a pattern that looks close but is wrong produces a howl at 45 mph and a failed gear set at 8,000 miles. If you have not set up a third member before, the bench-swap route on the rear lets you buy the expertise without buying the tools.\n\n- Rear automatic locker (Detroit, Grizzly), part only: **$450\u2013700**\n- Rear ARB, plus compressor and plumbing: **$1,000\u20131,400**\n- Front ARB for Dana 30 or 44, part only: **$750\u2013900**\n- Master carrier setup kit, per axle: **$110\u2013160**\n- Professional setup labor, per axle: **$400\u2013800**\n- Prebuilt 9-inch third member with locker and gears: **$1,600\u20132,400**\n- 31-spline chromoly rear shafts with large-bearing conversion: **$550\u2013800**\n\nA realistic rear-only build \u2014 automatic locker, gears, master kit, and shop setup \u2014 lands around **$1,400 to $1,900**. Adding a selectable front later brings the pair to roughly **$2,800 to $3,600**. Sequence it as rear locker and gears first, shafts with or immediately after, selectable front last. That order puts the capability on the truck in the sequence you will actually notice it."},{"id":"bronco-regear","title":"Re-gear for Tire Size \u2014 4.56 and Beyond","vehicle":"bronco","category":"axles","subcategory":"gearing","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":450,"high":1500},"tools_required":["dial indicator with magnetic base","inch-pound torque wrench","foot-pound torque wrench","bearing press","case spreader (front Dana only)","gear marking compound","brass drift"],"skills_required":["axle setup","advanced wrenching"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["regear","gearing","drivetrain","axle-upgrade"],"parts":[{"name":"Ring and pinion, Ford 9-inch rear (4.11 / 4.56 / 4.88)","vendor":"Yukon Gear / Motive Gear / Richmond","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"Ring and pinion, Dana 30 or Dana 44 front (4.10 / 4.56 / 4.88)","vendor":"Yukon Gear / Motive Gear","price_estimate_usd":190},{"name":"Master install kit, per axle (bearings, races, shims, seals, crush sleeve)","vendor":"Yukon / Timken / Wild Horses 4x4","price_estimate_usd":130},{"name":"Dana 20 speedometer driven gear (recalibration)","vendor":"Wild Horses 4x4 / Bronco Graveyard","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"Gear oil, 80W-90 or 75W-140 GL-5 (both axles)","vendor":"Valvoline / Lucas / Red Line","price_estimate_usd":55}],"citations":[{"title":"Wild Horses 4x4 \u2014 early Bronco differentials & gears","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/early-bronco-differentials-lockers"},{"title":"Sexton Offroad \u2014 early Bronco build guide (3.5in lift, 35s, 4.56 gears)","url":"https://www.sextonoffroad.com/blogs/4x4-parts-accessories/the-ultimate-early-ford-bronco-parts-guide-build-your-dream-1966-1977-rig"},{"title":"Kincer Chassis \u2014 understanding the axles on an early Ford Bronco","url":"https://kincerchassis.com/axles-on-an-early-ford-bronco/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-axle-gear-ratio","bronco-tires-mt","bronco-locker-install","bronco-dt-dana20-tc"],"summary":"Bolt 33s or 35s onto an early Bronco still wearing factory gears and the truck goes soft \u2014 it labors off the line, hunts on grades, and crawls poorly because the engine never reaches the part of its curve where the torque lives. **Run 4.11 rear and 4.10 front with 33s, and 4.56 with 35s.** Gear both axles in the same visit so you pay the precision setup labor once, and budget a $30 speedometer driven gear so the odometer stays honest. The [gear ratio reference](/db/bronco/bronco-axle-gear-ratio/) covers the underlying math; this card is the build.","description":"Order the wrong parts and this job stops before it starts, so start here. **Every early Bronco, 1966 through 1977, has a Ford 9-inch rear axle.** The Dana in these trucks is the front: a closed-knuckle **Dana 30 from 1966 to 1971**, then a closed-knuckle **Dana 44 from mid-1971 through 1977**. Factory ratios were 3.50, 4.11, and 4.57, with the lower two typically paired to the 3-speed and the smaller engines.\n\nThat split matters more than it sounds, because **the two axles are not always cataloged at identical nominal ratios**:\n\n- 4.56, 4.88, and 5.13 exist for both the Ford 9-inch and the Dana front. Order those and the numbers match exactly.\n- At the 4.1 step they do not. The 9-inch is cataloged as **4.11** and the Dana front as **4.10** \u2014 a 0.24 percent difference. That is well inside normal tire diameter variation between a front and a rear tire, and pairing them is longstanding early Bronco practice.\n- Anything past roughly 1 percent apart is a different story and will load the driveline in 4WD.\n\nConfirm what is in the truck before you order rather than trusting the year. Fifty years is plenty of time for someone to have changed both ends.\n\nThe arithmetic for how a tire change affects your gearing is one line:\n\n**effective ratio = current ratio \u00d7 (old tire diameter \u00f7 new tire diameter)**\n\nThe factory tire on these trucks was roughly 30 inches, so:\n\n- A **4.11 truck moving to 35s** works out to 4.11 \u00d7 (30 \u00f7 35) = **3.52 effective**. It now drives like a 3.52-geared truck on stock tires. That is the entire explanation for why a freshly lifted Bronco feels gutless.\n- A **3.50 truck moving to 33s** becomes 3.50 \u00d7 (30 \u00f7 33) = **3.18 effective**, which is unpleasant behind a carbureted 302 and abusive to a C4's torque converter.\n- To restore the *factory* feel on 35s from a 3.50 baseline you would need about 4.08. Nobody stops there, because 3.50 was a highway ratio for a 3-speed truck and was never good off-road.\n\nThat gap is why the documented early Bronco baseline, echoed in the Sexton build guide, is **4.56 with 35s** and **4.11/4.10 with 33s** \u2014 restoring the stock ratio plus one meaningful step of extra crawl. A heavy 35 behind a tired small-block, or a truck that spends its life in low range, sometimes wants **4.88**. A 31-inch tire on a mostly-street truck is livable on 4.11 and does not require the job at all.\n\nAxle ratio is one of three multipliers, and on an early Bronco it is often the one that matters least:\n\n**crawl ratio = transmission first gear \u00d7 transfer case low range \u00d7 axle ratio**\n\nThe Dana 20 transfer case low range is **2.46:1**. Then:\n\n- **Factory 3-speed (about 3.0:1 first) with 4.11 gears:** 3.0 \u00d7 2.46 \u00d7 4.11 \u2248 **30:1**\n- **Same 3-speed with 4.56:** \u2248 **33:1**\n- **C4 automatic (2.46:1 first) with 4.56:** \u2248 **28:1**\n- **NP435 4-speed (6.69:1 first) with 4.11:** \u2248 **68:1**\n\nRoughly 40:1 is where a truck starts crawling comfortably, and 50:1 or better is where you stop riding the clutch on ledges. The uncomfortable conclusion is that a factory-transmission early Bronco is **gear-limited at the transmission, not the axle**, and no realistic axle ratio fixes that alone. If your goal is genuine rock crawling rather than restoring drivability, price a granny-low transmission or a low-range kit for the [Dana 20](/db/bronco/bronco-dt-dana20-tc/) alongside the gears. If your goal is a Bronco that drives properly on 33s or 35s, the axle ratio by itself does that job well.\n\nThe Dana 20 is a part-time case, so a front-to-rear ratio mismatch does not hurt anything in 2WD. It destroys the driveline the moment you engage 4WD \u2014 which is precisely when you cannot afford it. Gear both ends to the same number.\n\nThere is also a straightforward economic argument. The expensive part of this job is not the gears, it is the setup: pinion depth, backlash, and bearing preload, on two axles. Doing both at once means one round of that labor and one period with the truck off the road. And if a [locker](/db/bronco/bronco-locker-install/) is anywhere in your plan, install it in the same teardown \u2014 the carrier is already out.\n\n**The rear has an advantage the front does not.** The Ford 9-inch is a drop-out design: the ring, pinion, and carrier all live in a third member that unbolts from the housing. You can pull it, have it set up on a bench, and reinstall \u2014 or buy a second third member already built with your gears and locker and swap it in an afternoon, keeping the original intact as a known-good spare. The Dana front is an integral-carrier axle and has to be set up in the housing.\n\nFollow the gear manufacturer's specifications over any general guidance, including this page.\n\n1. **Confirm existing ratios.** Count driveshaft turns per wheel revolution, or read the tag, or count ring and pinion teeth once the cover or third member is off.\n2. **Drain both axles** and inspect what comes out. Metallic glitter, a milky emulsion, or chunks change the scope of the job.\n3. **Rear: pull the third member.** Driveshaft off, axle shafts out, ten housing nuts, slide it out. It is heavier than it looks.\n4. **Set pinion depth with shims**, install the pinion with a new crush sleeve, and set **pinion bearing preload** to the manufacturer's inch-pound spec \u2014 this is the step people rush and the one that kills gear sets.\n5. **Set backlash**, typically **0.008 to 0.012 inch** measured on the ring gear face with a dial indicator, using the carrier shims or adjusters.\n6. **Paint the pattern.** Marking compound on several teeth, rotate under load in both directions, and read the drive and coast contact. The pattern is the verdict; backlash alone is not. Adjust pinion depth and re-check until it is right.\n7. **Front: repeat inside the housing.** The closed-knuckle Dana means new knuckle seals and an inspection of the kingpin bearings while it is apart.\n8. **Reassemble** with new gaskets and seals, torque to spec, and fill with **80W-90 or 75W-140 GL-5**. Add friction modifier only if a limited slip is present.\n9. **Swap the Dana 20 speedometer driven gear** to match the new ratio and tire diameter. The early Bronco speedometer is cable-driven off the transfer case, so this is a physical gear, not an electronic recalibration.\n10. **Break the gears in.** Gentle driving for 500 miles, no towing, and let the axles cool between highway stints. Change the gear oil at 500 miles to clear break-in metal, and treat that first oil change as part of the job rather than optional.\n\n**Setup is where gear jobs fail, not parts quality.** A pattern that looks close but sits wrong produces a howl at 45 mph and a destroyed gear set somewhere around 8,000 miles. If you have not set up a third member before, the drop-out rear gives you a clean way to buy expertise without buying a bearing press and a case spreader.\n\n**Buy a master kit per axle, not loose bearings.** New carrier and pinion bearings, races, crush sleeve, shims, and seals. Reusing a bearing to save $60 on a job with $600 of labor in it is a poor trade.\n\n**Do not stack this on weak shafts.** Lower gears multiply torque into the axle shafts. If the truck still runs 28-spline small-bearing rear shafts and you are adding a locker and 35s, price 31-spline chromoly shafts at the same time.\n\n**Recheck the driveshaft angles** if the truck has been lifted. Gears do not cause vibration, but a gear job is when people first drive the truck hard enough to notice one that was already there \u2014 see the [driveshaft and SYE guide](/db/bronco/bronco-dt-sye-cv-driveshaft/).\n\n- Ring and pinion set, per axle: **$180\u2013260**\n- Master install kit, per axle: **$110\u2013160**\n- Gear oil, both axles: **$45\u201370**\n- Dana 20 speedometer driven gear: **$25\u201340**\n- Professional setup labor, per axle: **$400\u2013800**\n- Prebuilt Ford 9-inch third member with gears and locker: **$1,600\u20132,400**\n\n**DIY, both axles, parts only: $560\u2013800.** **Shop-installed, both axles: $1,400\u20132,100.** Combining a locker install into the same teardown adds the locker's price plus little or no extra labor, which makes it the cheapest time you will ever buy that upgrade. Re-gear whenever you move up in tire size, match the ratios front to rear, and be honest about whether your real goal is drivability \u2014 which gears deliver \u2014 or crawl ratio, which needs the transmission addressed too."},{"id":"bronco-body-fender-trim","title":"Fender Trimming and Flares for Tire Clearance","vehicle":"bronco","category":"body","subcategory":"sheet metal","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":400},"tools_required":["angle grinder with cutoff wheel","marker and masking tape","metal file","drill","rivet gun or hardware","primer and paint","measuring tape"],"skills_required":["sheet metal cutting","edge finishing","trim sealing"],"build_tiers":["weekend_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["fender trim","body","tire clearance","flares","sheet metal"],"parts":[{"name":"Aftermarket fender flares (early Bronco set)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Edge trim / pinch weld molding","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"Self-etching primer and paint","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":35}],"citations":[{"title":"Fender Trimming for Tire Fitment \u2014 early Bronco builds","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-wt-tire-sizing","bronco-susp-soa"],"summary":"Lift gets the tire room to droop; fender trimming gets it room to stuff. If your early Bronco rubs at full compression or on tight turns even after a lift, the fender opening is the limit, not the suspension. Trimming sheet metal and adding flares is how you clear 35s and up without slamming the tire into the body on every dip.","description":"A lift creates static clearance, but a tire moves through its full travel under load. When the suspension compresses over a bump or in a turn, the tire moves up and back into the fender. That is where rubbing happens \u2014 not while parked. Trimming removes the metal the tire contacts at full stuff, and flares cover the wider stance and protect the body from trail debris.\n\nCutting sheet metal is permanent, so map the contact zone first. With the truck on the ground, turn the wheels lock to lock and watch where the tire approaches the fender. Then cycle the suspension \u2014 drive a wheel up onto a ramp or curb, or disconnect a shock and stuff the tire by hand \u2014 and mark every point of contact with tape.\n\nThe marks show the cut line. Add a margin so the tire clears under full articulation with a flexing sidewall, not the bare minimum at static height. A tire that barely clears on the bench will rub on the trail.\n\nWork in stages. Mark conservatively, cut, re-check clearance, and trim more only if needed. You cannot put metal back.\n\nUse a cutoff wheel for the main cut and a flap disc or file to dress the edge smooth. A raw cut edge is sharp and will rust, so finish it: file it clean, hit it with self-etching primer, and seal the edge with paint or pinch-weld trim molding. On an Arizona truck rust is slower, but bare steel still corrodes at a cut edge.\n\nA cutoff wheel is fastest for a straight-ish cut but throws sparks and leaves a rougher edge than a jigsaw with a metal-cutting blade, which follows a curved line more cleanly but needs a pilot hole to start and takes longer. When a flare is going to cover the edge, speed wins -- take the grinder. When the cut stays exposed, as with a mild lip trim and no flare, take the slower jigsaw route and put the time you saved into filing, since that edge is what people actually see.\n\nAftermarket flares serve two purposes: they cover the wider track of a lifted truck with offset wheels (often required to keep tires legally covered), and they protect the body from rocks and mud the wider tire throws. Early Bronco flare kits bolt or rivet to the trimmed opening. Match the flare width to how far your tires stick out past the body \u2014 too narrow and the tire is still exposed, too wide and it looks unfinished.\n\nMost trimming discussion, and most flare kits, center on the front fenders, since turning the wheels adds lateral tire movement on top of suspension travel. The rear wheel wells only see vertical travel, so they typically need less trimming for the same tire size -- but check them anyway. A wheel pushed outward by [wheel spacers](/db/bronco/bronco-wt-wheel-spacers-adapters/) or a wider aftermarket wheel with less backspacing can rub the rear opening even with modest suspension travel. Measure both ends before you buy a kit; some flare kits are front-only and pair a narrower rear flare or none at all, which won't help if the rear turns out to be the tighter fit.\n\nMany states set a minimum fender coverage requirement -- commonly that tire tread can't extend past the body or flare by more than a set distance, though the exact figure and how it's measured varies by state. A wide tire on a trimmed, unflared fender can put you outside that limit and draw an equipment citation independent of anything else on the truck. If you're trimming to clear a genuinely wide tire, budget for flares wide enough to actually cover it, not merely wide enough to stop the rubbing you have today.\n\nThe most common mistake is cutting to static clearance and discovering rub on the first trail run. Always check at full compression and full lock, not only sitting level.\n\nThe second is leaving raw edges unsealed. A cut fender edge is the fastest place for rust to start on an otherwise solid truck. Prime and seal every cut.\n\nIf you run 33s on a moderate lift, you may not need to cut at all \u2014 a mild trim of the inner lip or a pinch-weld roll can be enough. Save the angle grinder for when the tire actually contacts paint.\n\n- Trimming only (consumables): $40\n- Fender flare set with hardware: $280\n- Edge trim and finishing supplies: $30\n\nBudget $40 if you are trimming and sealing bare metal, $400 for a full flare kit painted and installed. The work itself costs little \u2014 the value is in measuring carefully so you cut once."},{"id":"bronco-body-floor-pan","title":"Floor Pan Rust Repair","vehicle":"bronco","category":"body","subcategory":"rust","type":"task","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":20,"high":60},"cost_usd":{"low":600,"high":3500},"tools_required":["MIG welder (0.023-0.030 wire)","angle grinder with cutoff and flap discs","body saw or plasma cutter","spot weld cutter","body hammer and dolly set","wire brush / wire wheel","rust converter","weld-through primer","seam sealer gun","welding helmet and fire extinguisher"],"skills_required":["panel welding","rust identification","body metalwork"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["rust","floor pan","body","restoration","welding","structural"],"parts":[{"name":"AMD complete floor pan set (driver + passenger)","vendor":"AMD (Auto Metal Direct)","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"Toe board / kick panel patch section (each side)","vendor":"Wild Horses 4x4 / Tom's Bronco Parts","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Seat riser / seat mount reinforcement pair","vendor":"Bronco Graveyard","price_estimate_usd":130},{"name":"Eastwood weld-through primer (aerosol)","vendor":"Eastwood","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"3M or SEM urethane seam sealer (tube)","vendor":"3M / SEM","price_estimate_usd":20},{"name":"SEM Rust Shield cavity wax (aerosol)","vendor":"SEM","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"POR-15 rust preventive coating (quart)","vendor":"POR-15","price_estimate_usd":35}],"citations":[{"title":"AMD early Bronco floor pans","url":"https://www.amdrestorationparts.com/"},{"title":"Wild Horses 4x4 \u2014 early Bronco sheet metal","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/"},{"title":"Floor pan replacement discussion \u2014 ClassicBroncos forums","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-body-rocker-panel","bronco-int-seats-harness","bronco-body-fender-trim"],"summary":"Floor pan rust is the number one early Bronco structural issue. If you can see daylight through the floor, the repair is mandatory \u2014 the pan is the foundation for the seat mounts, the seatbelt anchors, and body rigidity, not a cosmetic flaw you can carpet over. Budget a contingency of 30-50% on both time and money, because the rot always reaches further than the hole you can see. A full two-side floor replacement runs $600 in parts if you weld it yourself and $2,500-3,500 if a body shop does the metalwork.","description":"The early Bronco's body-on-frame construction means the floor pans are structural but replaceable, which is the good news \u2014 AMD, Wild Horses, Tom's Bronco Parts, and Bronco Graveyard all sell reproduction pan sections that put the truck back on solid steel. A complete floor replacement on a heavily rusted truck is a two-weekend job for an experienced welder, and considerably longer once the seat-mount and seatbelt-anchor metal is also gone.\n\nThese trucks trap water in four predictable ways, and knowing the mechanism tells you where to look before you start cutting.\n\n- **The cowl vent.** The 1966-1977 cowl vent drains through a pair of tubes that clog with pine needles and debris. Once blocked, cowl water dumps straight onto the toe board and forward floor from the inside. This is why the front floor often rots before the rear on a truck that lived under cover.\n- **Windshield frame and top seals.** The removable top and the windshield frame gasket both leak with age. Water runs down the kick panels into the floor-to-rocker seam.\n- **The floor-to-rocker seam.** This is a lap joint with a closed section behind it. It rots from both sides at once and is the seam most often patched badly.\n- **Carpet and jute padding.** Original jute holds moisture against bare steel indefinitely. A truck that got a carpet kit over a damp floor in 1994 has been quietly composting ever since.\n\nFixing the drainage before you fix the metal is the difference between a repair that lasts 30 years and one that rots again in six. Clear the cowl drain tubes, replace the windshield gasket, and reseal the top before final paint.\n\nPull the seats, the carpet, and the sound deadener completely. Rust hides under all three, and a visual check with the carpet in place tells you nothing useful.\n\nPoke every seam and low spot with an awl or a small screwdriver. Soft, flaky, or punch-through metal is rust-through. Work through these zones in order:\n\n1. **Toe board and forward floor**, directly under the cowl vent drains.\n2. **Seat bolt areas** \u2014 the front seat mounts sit in a low spot where water pools under the carpet. Rust here is a safety issue, not a cosmetic one.\n3. **The floor-to-rocker seam**, full length on both sides.\n4. **Rear floor toward the tailgate** and the wheel-well transitions.\n5. **Body mount points** \u2014 the pads where the tub bolts to the frame. Rot here means the body is no longer properly located on the chassis.\n\nThen grade what you found, because the grade sets the budget:\n\n- **Surface scale, no perforation:** wire wheel, rust converter, epoxy primer. No welding, one weekend.\n- **Localized perforation, solid surrounding metal:** patch panels cut to fit. Half a weekend per patch.\n- **Large-area perforation or soft seat mounts:** full pan section replacement, one side at a time.\n- **Both pans plus body mount or rocker involvement:** full floor kit and structural fabrication. This is the two-weekends-minimum tier, and the one where a $600 parts order turns into a $3,000 project.\n\n1. **Brace the body before cutting.** On a full-floor job, run a length of square tube across the door openings or bolt the doors shut with the hinges torqued. Cutting both pans out of an unbraced tub lets the body relax and the door gaps change.\n2. **Cut back to solid metal.** Use a body saw or a cutoff wheel and go 1-2\" into clean steel past the rot. Welding new pan to compromised metal produces a joint that fails at the weld line within a few years.\n3. **Drill or cut the factory spot welds** at the pan-to-rocker and pan-to-crossmember seams with a spot weld cutter rather than grinding through the flange. Preserving the flange gives the new panel something square to land on.\n4. **Treat everything exposed.** Wire wheel the mating flanges to bright steel, then rust-convert or encapsulate any surface rust in the surrounding area. Let it cure fully \u2014 POR-15 needs a full overnight, longer in humidity.\n5. **Test fit before any welding.** Clamp the new pan in, check that the seat mount holes land where the seat tracks want them, and verify the pan sits flat on the crossmembers. Reproduction panels usually need trimming at one edge.\n6. **Weld-through primer on every mating surface**, then stitch-weld the pan in \u2014 roughly 1\" of weld every 3\", moving around the panel to spread the heat. Continuous seam welding will warp an 18-gauge pan into a shape no amount of hammer-and-dolly work will fix.\n7. **Dress the welds**, then seam-seal every interior seam with urethane body sealer.\n8. **Coat the underside** with POR-15 or a chassis coating, and inject cavity wax into the closed rocker section adjacent to the repair.\n\nFloor pan metal anchors the seat tracks and the seatbelt mounts, which makes this a safety repair rather than bodywork. A seat track bolted through rusted metal is not a repair; it is a failure waiting for a rollover. If the seat-mount or belt-anchor area is soft, replace that metal and add a reinforcement plate \u2014 a 1/8\" backing plate welded or through-bolted behind the anchor spreads the load into sound steel.\n\nRebuilding this area is the right moment to sort [seat and harness mounting](/db/bronco/bronco-int-seats-harness/) properly, since the interior is already out and you have clear access from both sides. If a cage is anywhere in the truck's future, plan the [roll cage](/db/bronco/bronco-interior-roll-cage/) footplate locations now and weld the plates in while the floor is open \u2014 retrofitting cage feet through a finished floor means cutting it again.\n\n- **Fire.** Sound deadener and undercoating burn, and on most early Broncos the main fuel tank sits directly under the driver's seat (the last-year trucks moved it rearward). Drain and remove the tank before welding anywhere near the floor, keep an extinguisher within arm's reach, and have a second person watching the underside while you weld.\n- **Rot creep.** The same trapped-moisture pattern that killed the floor is working on the [rocker panels](/db/bronco/bronco-body-rocker-panel/) and the body mounts. Inspect and treat all of it in one session \u2014 coming back for the rockers after paint costs twice.\n- **Warping from heat.** Stitch, skip around, and let the panel cool. Impatience here is the most common self-inflicted wound on a first floor job.\n- **Ignoring the cause.** If the cowl drains are still blocked when the carpet goes back in, the new pan starts rusting the first time it rains.\n\n| Item | Typical cost |\n|---|---|\n| AMD complete floor pan set, both sides | $480 |\n| Toe board / kick panel patch sections | $95 each |\n| Seat riser reinforcement pair | $130 |\n| Weld-through primer, seam sealer, cavity wax | $70 |\n| POR-15 quart | $35 |\n| **DIY total, full floor** | **$600-850** |\n| Body shop labor, full floor, 20-40 hrs @ $75-110/hr | $1,800-3,000 |\n\nDIY with a borrowed MIG lands around $600 in materials. Farming out the welding puts a full two-side floor between $2,500 and $3,500 depending on how much of the rocker and body-mount metal goes with it. The gap between those two numbers is why floor rust is the single biggest factor in what an early Bronco is worth.","pros":["reproduction pans are widely available for both sides at reasonable cost","a structural fix that adds decades of longevity to the tub","far cheaper than farming the whole job out to a body shop","opens access for cage footplates, harness mounts, and wiring while the floor is out"],"cons":["requires welding \u2014 not a bolt-on repair","rust always extends further than expected, so budget contingency","full interior removal is required to do it right","fire risk near the fuel tank demands real preparation"]},{"id":"bronco-body-rocker-panel","title":"Rocker Panel Rust Repair and Replacement","vehicle":"bronco","category":"body","subcategory":"rust","type":"task","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":12,"high":40},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":2500},"tools_required":["MIG welder (0.023-0.030 wire)","angle grinder with cutoff and flap discs","body saw","spot weld cutter","hammer and dolly set","wire wheel","weld-through primer","cavity wax applicator wand","clamps and Cleco fasteners","awl or pick"],"skills_required":["panel welding","rust identification","door gap alignment"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["rust","rocker panel","body","restoration","welding"],"parts":[{"name":"Early Bronco outer rocker panel sections (pair)","vendor":"AMD / Bronco Graveyard","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Inner rocker / sill reinforcement section (each)","vendor":"Wild Horses 4x4 / Tom's Bronco Parts","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Kick panel and hinge pillar patch (each)","vendor":"AMD","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"3M cavity wax (aerosol, for closed sections)","vendor":"3M","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"Eastwood Rust Encapsulator (quart)","vendor":"Eastwood","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"Weld-through primer (aerosol)","vendor":"Eastwood / SEM","price_estimate_usd":28}],"citations":[{"title":"Bronco Graveyard \u2014 early Bronco rocker panels","url":"https://www.broncograveyard.com/"},{"title":"AMD Restoration Parts \u2014 early Bronco sheet metal","url":"https://www.amdrestorationparts.com/"},{"title":"Rocker rust repair discussion \u2014 ClassicBroncos forums","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-body-floor-pan","bronco-body-fender-trim","bronco-armor-rock-rails"],"summary":"Rocker rust is structural on the early Bronco, not cosmetic. The rocker sill ties the body together between the door hinge pillar and the kick panel \u2014 when it rots, the door sags and the gap goes uneven. Fix the rockers before you rehang doors or chase a body gap, because the rocker sets the geometry everything else references. A pair of outer skins runs $280; a full inner-and-outer section replacement with door realignment lands between $400 DIY and $2,500 at a shop.","description":"Rockers on an early Bronco rust from the inside out. Trapped moisture in the closed section corrodes the inner sill while the outer skin still looks presentable from three feet away. Before you buy panels, probe the rocker with an awl along its entire length: soft, flaky, or hollow means the inner structure is gone and you are into a section replacement rather than a skim patch.\n\nThe early Bronco rocker is not a single stamping. It is an outer skin, an inner sill, and a closed cavity between them, with the assembly spot-welded to the floor pan flange at the bottom and running into the hinge pillar at the front and the rear quarter at the back. Three consequences follow:\n\n1. **It carries load.** The rocker resists the body's tendency to twist, and the front door's lower hinge geometry references it. A rotted rocker is why an otherwise straight truck has a door that drops when you open it.\n2. **You cannot see the part that fails.** The inner sill is the structural member and it rusts first, behind an outer skin that looks fine.\n3. **Water gets in and stays.** Drain holes at the bottom of the rocker plug with mud and undercoating. Once the cavity holds water, corrosion runs year-round.\n\nHow far the rust has gone decides whether this is an afternoon or two weekends:\n\n| Finding | Repair | Time |\n|---|---|---|\n| Surface rust, outer skin only | Sand, treat, prime, paint \u2014 no welding | 3-5 hrs |\n| Perforation in outer skin, solid inner | Outer patch or full skin section | 6-10 hrs |\n| Inner sill compromised | Full section replacement, structural | 12-20 hrs |\n| Inner and outer destroyed, floor flange involved | Section replacement plus inner fabrication, floor tie-in | 25-40 hrs |\n\nMisjudging this is the classic early-Bronco trap \u2014 the outer skin photographs fine while the inner sill behind it is lace. Cut a 2\" inspection window with a hole saw if you are unsure. It costs a patch and 20 minutes and saves you from ordering the wrong panels.\n\n1. **Remove the door on that side.** You need access to the rocker end at the hinge pillar, and you will want the door available to check gap during fitment. Mark the hinge positions before unbolting so you have a baseline to return to.\n2. **Brace the opening.** On a full inner-and-outer section replacement, tack a length of square tube diagonally across the door opening before cutting. Removing the rocker without bracing lets the opening relax and the door will never fit the same way again.\n3. **Cut out the damaged section**, going 1-2\" into clean steel past the rot at each end. Drill the factory spot welds along the floor flange with a spot weld cutter rather than grinding through it \u2014 the flange is your fitment reference.\n4. **Treat all exposed metal**, especially inside the closed section. Wire wheel to bright steel where the new panel will land, encapsulate the surrounding surface rust, and let it cure fully.\n5. **Fit and clamp the new section, then check door gap before any welding.** Hang the door temporarily and verify the gap top to bottom. A panel tacked in 3mm high shows up as a sagging door and an uneven gap that no amount of hinge shimming will hide.\n6. **Weld-through primer on every lap joint**, then stitch-weld \u2014 short welds spaced out, skipping around the panel to control heat. Continuous seam welding warps rocker skins badly.\n7. **Dress the welds** with a flap disc, working light so you do not thin the panel, then seam-seal the joints.\n8. **Drill a wax injection hole**, spray cavity wax through the entire closed section with a wand, and plug the hole with a rubber grommet. Verify the factory drain holes at the bottom of the rocker are open before you call it done.\n\nThe repair only lasts if you stop the next round of inside-out rust. Inject 3M cavity wax (or the SEM or Eastwood equivalent) into every closed body section once the metalwork is finished \u2014 rockers, door bottoms, hinge pillars, and the rear quarter cavities. This is the single highest-value rust-prevention step on the truck, and it is the reason a properly repaired rocker outlives the factory one by decades.\n\nEqually important: leave the drain holes open. Undercoating sprayed over the bottom of a rocker seals water in rather than out, which is how a well-intentioned 1980s rustproofing job accelerated the rot on half the surviving early Broncos.\n\nThe same trapped-moisture pattern drives [floor pan rot](/db/bronco/bronco-body-floor-pan/) and [fender and body-mount corrosion](/db/bronco/bronco-body-fender-trim/), so treat all three areas in the same session while the interior is apart. If you are already in there for the floor, doing rockers at the same time saves a second teardown and a second round of paint blending.\n\nIf [rock rails or sliders](/db/bronco/bronco-armor-rock-rails/) are in the truck's future, sort the rocker metal first and then mount the armor to the frame rather than to the body. A slider bolted or welded to a body rocker transfers rock impacts directly into sheet metal that was never designed to take them, and it traps mud against the freshly repaired panel. Frame-mounted sliders protect the rocker; body-mounted sliders eventually destroy it.\n\n- **Fire.** On most early Broncos the main fuel tank sits under the driver's seat, close enough to the rocker work that a drained-and-removed tank is the right call before striking an arc. Keep an extinguisher within reach.\n- **Heat warp.** Stitch, skip, and let the panel cool between passes. Patience is worth more than technique here.\n- **Door realignment.** Plan on re-shimming hinges and adjusting the striker after the rocker is in. Check the gap with the truck on level ground and the body mounts torqued, not on jack stands.\n- **Hidden hinge pillar rot.** The rocker and the hinge pillar rot together more often than not. Probe the pillar before you order, or you will be ordering twice.\n\n| Item | Typical cost |\n|---|---|\n| Outer rocker sections, pair | $280 |\n| Inner sill sections, pair | $240 |\n| Kick panel / hinge pillar patches | $90 each |\n| Cavity wax, encapsulator, weld-through primer | $90 |\n| **DIY parts total** | **$400-700** |\n| Body shop labor, both sides, 12-25 hrs @ $75-110/hr | $1,200-2,000 |\n\nDIY with a MIG welder puts both rockers in for $400-700 in parts. Shop work on both sides, including door realignment and blend paint, runs $1,800-2,500. As with floor pans, the spread between those numbers is most of what separates a $12,000 early Bronco from a $30,000 one.","pros":["reproduction sections are widely available for both sides, inner and outer","fixes door sag and uneven gaps when done with the door fitted for reference","cavity wax treatment prevents recurrence for decades","open access while the interior is out makes it the cheapest time to do it"],"cons":["inner rust is almost always worse than the outer skin suggests","door removal, bracing, and realignment are required to do it correctly","welding near the under-seat fuel tank demands real preparation","budget 30-50% more time than your first estimate"]},{"id":"bronco-brakes-dual-master-cylinder","title":"Dual-Circuit Master Cylinder and Booster Upgrade","vehicle":"bronco","category":"brakes","subcategory":"hydraulics","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":500},"tools_required":["line wrench set","floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","brake bleeder","bench vise"],"skills_required":["brake hydraulics","bleeding brakes","brake line fabrication"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","weekend_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["master cylinder","brakes","dual circuit","booster","safety","hydraulics"],"parts":[{"name":"Dual reservoir master cylinder (disc/drum or disc/disc)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Brake booster (7-inch dual diaphragm)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Proportioning valve / combination valve","vendor":"Wilwood / Various","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Brake line and fittings","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":50}],"safety_critical":true,"citations":[{"title":"Single to Dual Master Cylinder Conversion \u2014 fullsizebronco.com","url":"https://www.fullsizebronco.com/forum/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-brakes-front-disc-swap"],"summary":"**Safety critical.** If your early Bronco still has a single-reservoir master cylinder, replace it. A single-circuit system means one leak anywhere \u2014 a blown line, a failed wheel cylinder, a bad seal \u2014 leaves you with no brakes at all. A dual-circuit master splits the system so a failure in one half still leaves you stopping power in the other. This is the single most important brake upgrade you can make.","description":"Early Broncos from 1966 to mid-1967 left the factory with a single-reservoir master cylinder. Federal regulation required dual-circuit systems starting in 1967, so later trucks got them, but many early trucks have been through enough hands that you cannot assume what is installed. Look at the master cylinder: one reservoir chamber means single-circuit, two chambers means dual.\n\nA single-circuit system has one hydraulic loop feeding all four corners. Lose pressure anywhere \u2014 a rock-torn line on the trail, a corroded fitting, a worn seal \u2014 and the pedal goes to the floor with nothing behind it. There is no backup. On a 50-year-old truck with original lines, that is a genuine risk, not a hypothetical.\n\nA dual-circuit master cylinder has two separate hydraulic systems, typically split front and rear. A failure in one circuit still leaves the other working. You lose braking on two wheels, not four. The truck stops longer and pulls, but it stops. That difference is the line between an inconvenience and a crash.\n\nThe clean way to do this is a combined conversion:\n\n1. **Dual master cylinder** sized for your brakes. If you run front discs, use a disc/drum or disc/disc master with the correct bore. Bore size affects pedal feel and pressure \u2014 match it to whether you have a power booster.\n\n2. **Brake booster**, if you are converting to discs or want lighter pedal effort. A 7-inch dual-diaphragm booster fits the early Bronco engine bay with common brackets. Manual (unboosted) setups work but require firmer pedal pressure.\n\n3. **Proportioning or combination valve.** A disc/drum system needs proportioning to keep the rear drums from locking before the front discs bite. An adjustable proportioning valve lets you tune the front-to-rear balance.\n\n4. **New lines from the master.** Reusing crusty 50-year-old hard lines on a fresh master is a missed opportunity. Replace at least the lines coming off the master cylinder.\n\nBench-bleed the master cylinder before installing it. A master with air trapped inside will never give a firm pedal no matter how long you bleed at the wheels. Mount it in a vise, attach short bleed tubes back into the reservoirs, and stroke the piston until no bubbles appear.\n\nAfter installation, bleed the full system in the correct sequence \u2014 farthest wheel from the master first \u2014 and confirm a firm, high pedal before the truck moves under its own power. A spongy pedal means air remains; do not drive until it is solid.\n\nGet the bore size and proportioning right. Too large a bore gives a hard, grabby pedal; too small gives a long, mushy one. If you are unsure, match the master cylinder bore the kit vendor specifies for your exact brake configuration.\n\n- Dual master cylinder: $90\n- 7-inch dual-diaphragm booster: $180\n- Adjustable proportioning valve: $60\n- Lines and fittings: $50\n\nBudget $150 for a manual dual-master conversion reusing the booster, up to $500 for a full booster-and-master setup with new lines. There is no cheaper insurance on this truck. If money is tight, do the master cylinder alone first \u2014 the dual circuit is the part that saves you."},{"id":"bronco-brakes-front-disc-swap","title":"Front Disc Brake Conversion","vehicle":"bronco","category":"brakes","subcategory":"disc conversion","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":700},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","brake line wrench (flare nut)","socket set","torque wrench","brake bleeder","wire brush"],"skills_required":["brake bleeding","hub and bearing service","line fitting"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1975"],"tags":["brakes","disc conversion","front brakes","safety","drum to disc"],"parts":[{"name":"Front disc conversion kit (Dana 44, EB-specific)","vendor":"Wild Horses / Toms Bronco","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Proportioning/combination valve","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"DOT 3/4 brake fluid","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Braided stainless flex lines (pair)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":60}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco disc brake conversion","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/"},{"title":"Drum-to-disc proportioning","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-brakes-master-upgrade"],"summary":"SAFETY CRITICAL \u2014 brake work is one of the few jobs where a mistake can kill you or someone else. Bleed thoroughly, double-check every fitting, and road-test at low speed before trusting the system.","description":"> If your early Bronco still has front drums, converting to discs is the highest-value safety upgrade you can make \u2014 better stopping, far better fade resistance, and no more chasing a grabbing drum after every water crossing. Pre-1976 trucks need a kit; 1976\u201377 came with discs from the factory.\n\nFront drum brakes on an early Bronco fade hard on a long descent, pull when one shoe grabs, and turn useless after a water crossing fills the drum. A front disc conversion fixes all three. Factory front discs arrived for 1976, so 1966\u20131975 trucks are the conversion candidates; 1976\u201377 owners should focus on pad and rotor refresh instead.\n\nThe honest tradeoff: a disc conversion is a real afternoon of work involving hubs, bearings, and a full bleed, plus you usually need to address the master cylinder and proportioning to match. Done right it transforms the truck. Done half-way \u2014 old master cylinder, no proportioning valve \u2014 it can feel worse than the drums it replaced.\n\nA Bronco-specific Dana 44 front disc kit includes calipers, rotors, caliper brackets, new spindle hardware, and usually inner/outer bearings and seals. You also need a master cylinder rated for discs (a drum master pushes too little volume) and a combination or proportioning valve to balance front disc / rear drum pressure. Braided stainless flex lines are a worthwhile add \u2014 the original rubber lines are likely 50 years old and swelling internally.\n\nSupport the front on jack stands, remove the wheels, and pull the drums, hubs, and old backing plates. Clean and inspect the spindle; a worn spindle bearing surface needs addressing before the new hub goes on. Install the caliper bracket, pack and set the wheel bearings, mount the rotor and hub, then bolt on the caliper with new pads. Plumb the new flex line and combination valve, then bleed the entire system \u2014 start at the corner farthest from the master cylinder and work in. If you changed the master cylinder, bench-bleed it first.\n\nSet wheel bearing preload to spec: snug while spinning the rotor to seat the bearings, back off, then finger-tight plus the cotter-pin slot \u2014 not cranked down. Over-tight bearings cook and fail; loose bearings let the rotor wobble into the pads.\n\nSkipping the proportioning valve is the classic mistake. Front discs need more pressure than drums; without a valve to bias the system, the rears can lock early or the fronts feel dead. Adjustable proportioning valves let you dial it in.\n\nA drum-era master cylinder won't move enough fluid for calipers \u2014 pedal goes to the floor. Match the master to the conversion kit's spec.\n\nAir in the lines is the number-one cause of a soft pedal after the job. Bleed until the fluid runs clear with zero bubbles, then bleed again. A pressure or vacuum bleeder makes this far more reliable than the two-person pump method.\n\nAfter assembly, pump the pedal until firm before you move the truck \u2014 the first pumps take up caliper clearance. Road-test at parking-lot speed first.\n\nA Bronco-specific front disc kit runs $350\u2013450. Add a disc-rated master cylinder ($60\u2013110), combination/proportioning valve ($55), braided lines ($60), and fluid ($12). Total a realistic $450\u2013700 depending on how much of the hydraulic system you refresh. Wild Horses 4x4, Toms Bronco Parts, and James Duff sell complete EB kits with matched components \u2014 buying a kit rather than piecing it together avoids proportioning mismatches."},{"id":"bronco-brakes-rear-disc-swap","title":"Rear Disc Brake Conversion for the Early Bronco","vehicle":"bronco","category":"brakes","subcategory":"disc conversion","type":"upgrade","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":800},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","brake line flare tool","tube bender","brake bleeder","snap-ring pliers"],"skills_required":["brake plumbing and flaring","bleeding brakes","setting up an emergency brake","torquing to spec"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"safety_critical":true,"tags":["brakes","disc brakes","rear disc","safety","brake upgrade","brakes"],"parts":[{"name":"Rear disc conversion kit (Explorer 8.8 or Dana 44 specific)","vendor":"Wild Horses / TSM / Right Hand","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Adjustable proportioning valve","vendor":"Wilwood","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Brake line and fittings","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":40},{"name":"DOT 3/4 brake fluid","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco rear disc conversion","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/"},{"title":"Proportioning and emergency brake setup","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-brakes-front-disc-swap"],"summary":"**Safety-critical.** Brakes are not the place to improvise, take shortcuts, or leave a job half-finished. A rear disc conversion on an early Bronco improves stopping in mud and water and ends the chore of adjusting rear drums, but only if it's plumbed, proportioned, and bled correctly. If you are not confident flaring brake lines and bleeding a system to a firm pedal, have a shop do this part. A soft pedal or a rear bias that locks the back tires can put you off the trail or into traffic.","description":"The honest verdict: a rear disc swap is a real upgrade but not a mandatory one. The early Bronco's rear drums work when adjusted and dry. Where they fall down is after a water crossing or a muddy day \u2014 drums pack with grit and fade, and they self-adjust poorly on a lifted truck. Discs shed water and mud and need no adjustment. If you wheel wet or muddy terrain, the swap is worth it. If you mostly run dry desert trails around Phoenix, well-maintained drums may serve you fine and save the money.\n\nA conversion kit matched to your rear axle. Many early Broncos run the original Dana 44 rear, but a common upgrade swaps in a Ford Explorer 8.8 axle that already has factory disc provisions or bolt-on kits. Buy the kit for the axle you actually have. You also need an adjustable proportioning valve, brake line and fittings, a flaring tool and bender, fresh DOT 3 or DOT 4 fluid, and a plan for the emergency brake \u2014 disc conversions change how the parking brake works, and many kits include a cable-actuated caliper or a separate drum-in-hat park brake.\n\nSupport the truck on jack stands rated for the load, chock the front wheels, and never work under a vehicle held only by a jack. Remove the rear wheels, drums, and the old drum hardware. Install the caliper brackets and rotors per the kit instructions, torque the caliper bracket bolts to spec, and fit the calipers and pads. Run new brake line to each caliper with proper double-flares \u2014 a single flare or a compression fitting on a brake line is a failure waiting to happen.\n\nPlumb the adjustable proportioning valve into the rear circuit. Discs need different pressure balance than drums, and without proportioning the rear can lock before the front, which is dangerous \u2014 the back end steps out under braking. Start the proportioning valve closed-ish and dial it in on a safe, empty road.\n\nSet up the emergency brake per the kit. Bleed the entire system, starting at the wheel farthest from the master cylinder, until the pedal is firm and high with no sponginess. Then bleed again. Test at very low speed in a safe area before any real driving, checking that the truck pulls up straight and the pedal holds.\n\nThe rear-bias danger is the one to respect. Too much rear braking locks the back tires and can swap ends, especially on a light, short-wheelbase truck on loose surface. Dial the proportioning valve so the front brakes always lock first. Test it deliberately.\n\nA spongy pedal after bleeding means air is still in the system or a flare is leaking. Do not drive on it. Re-bleed and inspect every fitting. Brake fluid weeps show up as a wet, shiny film at a joint.\n\nDon't neglect the emergency brake. A disc swap that leaves you with no working parking brake fails inspection in many states and leaves you nothing to hold the truck on a grade. Confirm the park brake holds on a hill before you call the job done.\n\nA complete rear disc conversion kit runs $350\u2013550 depending on axle; an Explorer 8.8 swap-plus-disc path can run higher once you account for the axle. Add $60 for an adjustable proportioning valve, $40 for line and fittings, and fluid. Reputable early Bronco vendors (Wild Horses, TSM, James Duff) sell kits cut for these axles. You probably don't need this upgrade if your drums are healthy and you wheel dry \u2014 spend honestly here, because the front disc swap (if you still have front drums) buys far more stopping power per dollar than the rear conversion does."},{"id":"bronco-build-stock-resto","title":"Stock Restoration Build Recipe","vehicle":"bronco","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"restoration","type":"build_recipe","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":200,"high":600},"cost_usd":{"low":25000,"high":80000},"tools_required":["full shop setup","MIG welder","air compressor","media blaster","body tools","engine stand"],"skills_required":["engine rebuild","body metalwork","painting","full mechanical"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["restoration","stock","build recipe","full restore","numbers matching"],"parts":[{"name":"Reproduction floor pan set (full, left + right)","vendor":"Bronco Graveyard","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Full reproduction wiring harness","vendor":"Wild Horses 4x4","price_estimate_usd":650},{"name":"Complete interior soft-trim kit (seats, carpet, panels)","vendor":"Bronco Graveyard","price_estimate_usd":2200},{"name":"Epoxy primer and single-stage color (factory-correct, gallon kit)","vendor":"Summit Racing","price_estimate_usd":700},{"name":"Dana 44 / 9-inch master rebuild kits (front + rear)","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":230}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco restoration cost discussion \u2014 Full Size Bronco forum","url":"https://www.fullsizebronco.com/forum/"},{"title":"Early Bronco reproduction parts catalog \u2014 Bronco Graveyard","url":"https://www.broncograveyard.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-build-trail","bronco-body-floor-pan","bronco-maint-closed-knuckle-service"],"summary":"A stock restoration is a 12\u201336 month project that almost always costs more than the number you first wrote down. Budget $35K\u2013$50K for a solid driver-quality result, and another $20K\u2013$40K on top of that for show quality. You do a stock restore to preserve the truck's value and originality \u2014 the math rarely breaks even, and that is not the reason to do it.","description":"The goal of this recipe is a period-correct early Bronco: factory appearance, factory-correct mechanicals, and the originality that the collector market rewards. It is the opposite of the [trail build recipe](/db/bronco/bronco-build-trail/), which spends the same money on capability instead of correctness. Decide which truck you are building before you cut anything apart, because the two paths diverge immediately and reversing course is expensive.\n\nTear down to a bare tub and bare frame. Catalog and bag every fastener and bracket, and photograph each assembly before it comes apart \u2014 you will not remember how the dash harness routed eight months from now. Note what is original versus previously replaced; numbers-matching components are worth preserving even when they need rebuilding. This phase is mostly labor and consumables, but it sets up everything after it.\n\nThis is the single most variable line in the budget, and it is driven entirely by where the truck lived. A dry Southwest truck might need floor pans and a rocker section, $3,000\u2013$6,000 in metalwork. A salt-belt truck with structural rot \u2014 full floors, rockers, toe board, and inner structure \u2014 can run $15,000\u2013$25,000 in professional bodywork. The floor pans are the usual starting point; the [floor pan rust repair guide](/db/bronco/bronco-body-floor-pan/) shows what that job actually involves before you decide whether to do it yourself or farm it out.\n\nMedia-blast the frame, inspect carefully for cracks at the body-mount holes and crossmembers, weld any you find, then encapsulate and paint. A clean frame is the foundation the rest of the restoration sits on, and finding a crack after the body is back on is the kind of mistake that undoes months of work.\n\nRebuild or replace the engine and transmission, service the transfer case, and rebuild both axles \u2014 bearings, seals, and correct preload front and rear. Replace every U-joint. The closed-knuckle Dana 44 front in particular wants a full kingpin-and-seal service while everything is apart; the [closed-knuckle service guide](/db/bronco/bronco-maint-closed-knuckle-service/) covers it. If you are keeping the original ratios, verify them against tire size using the [gear ratio guide](/db/bronco/bronco-axle-gear-ratio/) so a \"restored\" truck does not drive poorly on modern rubber.\n\nEpoxy primer, block sanding to flat, color, and clear. Factory colors include Poppy Red, Lime Gold, Candyapple Red, Midnight Blue, and Wimbledon White; pull the correct code from the door-jamb data tag for an accurate match. Paint quality is where a driver-grade restoration and a show truck visibly part ways, and where labor cost climbs fastest.\n\nSeat upholstery, carpet, headliner, dash pad, and door panels. Early Bronco soft trim is well reproduced \u2014 Bronco Graveyard, Wild Horses, and Toms Bronco Parts stock most of it \u2014 so this phase is more about patience and fit than sourcing.\n\nA high-quality stock restoration on a desirable 1969\u20131972 truck can bring $65,000\u2013$120,000-plus at auction; a driver-quality restored example lands around $35,000\u2013$60,000; a correct-appearing, unrestored truck with good bones sits at $25,000\u2013$40,000. Restoration cost rarely equals market value. Go in understanding that, and the budget overruns sting less.","pros":["preserves maximum market value and originality","factory-correct early Broncos are appreciating","the most satisfying long-term ownership for a purist"],"cons":["almost always costs more than the original budget","a 2\u20133 year project minimum for quality results","requires professional-grade bodywork and paint to come out right","spends the same money on correctness that a trail build spends on capability"]},{"id":"bronco-build-trail","title":"Trail Build Recipe \u2014 35s, Dana 44 Lockers, 4\" Lift","vehicle":"bronco","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"trail","type":"build_recipe","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":80,"high":180},"cost_usd":{"low":15000,"high":35000},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","welder (for skids/sliders)"],"skills_required":["suspension work","driveline basics","wiring basics"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["build recipe","trail","35 inch","lockers","4 inch lift","dana 44"],"parts":[{"name":"Skyjacker 4\" suspension kit","vendor":"Skyjacker","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"ARB Air Locker front (Dana 44)","part_number":"RD103","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":580},{"name":"ARB Air Locker rear (Dana 44)","part_number":"RD100","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":580},{"name":"ARB twin air compressor","part_number":"CKMTA12","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":340},{"name":"35x12.50R15 BFG KO2 (set of 5)","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":1200},{"name":"15x8\" steel wheels, 4.5\" backspacing (set of 5)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":500},{"name":"5.0 HO long block + Edelbrock intake + carb","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":2200},{"name":"Hooker shorty headers + 2.5\" dual exhaust","vendor":"Hooker / Various","price_estimate_usd":650},{"name":"Yukon 4.56 gear sets front and rear","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":680},{"name":"Skid plates (transfer case + engine)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":500}],"citations":[{"title":"Trail Bronco build thread","url":"https://www.fullsizebronco.com/forum/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-build-stock-resto","bronco-susp-4in-lift","bronco-axle-locker-options"],"summary":"This is the standard capable trail build for an early Bronco \u2014 not a show truck, not a dedicated rock buggy, but a rig that handles moderate-to-difficult trails reliably and still drives home on the street. Plan on $15K\u2013$35K depending on starting condition and how much you do yourself. The single biggest variable is rust: a solid truck builds at the low end, a rusty one adds $5K\u2013$15K before the fun parts start.","description":"The recipe below assumes a 1969\u20131977 Bronco with solid floors and a solid frame as the starting point. If the body or frame is rust-compromised, fix that first \u2014 building a trail truck on rotten [floor pans](/db/bronco/bronco-body-floor-pan/) or [rockers](/db/bronco/bronco-body-rocker-panel/) is wasted money.\n\nA clean 5.0/302 truck with good sheet metal is the ideal core. Verify the floors, rockers, and frame rails with an awl before you commit a dollar to mods. Rust-compromised structure adds $5K\u2013$15K to this budget before the build even begins, and it's the number one reason these builds blow past their estimate.\n\n- 5.0L HO engine swap if you're starting from an I6 or a tired 302: ~$2,200 with an Edelbrock intake and carb, covered in the [5.0 HO / EFI swap guide](/db/bronco/bronco-eng-302-ho-swap/)\n- Shorty headers and 2.5\" dual exhaust: ~$650\n- Pertronix ignition conversion: ~$90 and the best small dollar on the engine\n\n- Skyjacker 4\" complete kit: ~$1,100 with springs, extended shocks, and hardware \u2014 see the [4\" lift baseline guide](/db/bronco/bronco-susp-4in-lift/) for fitment and geometry notes\n- Extended stainless brake lines: ~$80, safety-critical, never skip them at 4\"\n\n- ARB Air Lockers front and rear: ~$1,160 for both, plus ~$340 for the twin compressor and ~$200 for air line and mounting\n- Yukon 4.56 gear sets: ~$680 for both axles, plus $700\u2013$900 for professional setup; match the ratio to your tire per the [gear ratio guide](/db/bronco/bronco-axle-gear-ratio/)\n\n- 35x12.50R15 BFG KO2: ~$1,200 mounted and balanced for five\n- 15x8 steel wheels, 4.5\" backspacing: ~$500 for five\n\n- Transfer-case and engine skids: ~$500 purchased or fabricated\n- Tube rock sliders: ~$400\u2013600 fabricated\n\nThe parts list above targets the middle of the market. A budget version swaps the ARB Air Lockers for a Detroit Locker or Truetrac (saves roughly $700-900 combined but loses selectable open-diff mode), uses a mid-tier lift like a Rough Country in place of the Skyjacker (saves $200-300), and holds off on the 5.0 HO swap in favor of tuning the existing 302 (saves $1,800-2,000 but keeps the carbureted engine's cold-start and altitude quirks). That combination lands closer to $10,000-12,000 on a solid core truck. A premium version adds a coilover conversion, full tube doors, and a fully built (not merely regeared) axle on both ends -- $45,000-plus without much effort and closer to a dedicated rock buggy than a trail-and-street truck.\n\nEngine first, so you're not building around a tired powerplant. Then suspension, brake lines, and an alignment. Then the axle regear, and lockers in the same teardown to avoid pulling the diffs twice. Tires last \u2014 confirm clearance at full stuff before you buy five of them.\n\nThe budget above gets the truck built \u2014 it doesn't cover what it costs to keep it running. 35s wear faster than stock-size tires under trail use; budget a replacement set every 25,000-35,000 miles at roughly the $1,200 figure above. The ARB lockers and twin compressor are low-maintenance but not zero-maintenance \u2014 inspect the air line fittings and the compressor's intake filter yearly. Fuel economy on a 5.0 HO with 35s and 4.56s typically lands in the 10-13 mpg range, worth weighing before you decide this is a daily driver rather than a trail-and-back rig. Insurance on a heavily modified classic usually runs through an agreed-value classic policy rather than standard coverage \u2014 get a quote before you spend the build budget so the number doesn't surprise you afterward.","pros":["genuinely capable on moderate-to-difficult trails","streetable enough for the drive to and from the trailhead","ARB lockers stay transparent until you switch them on","builds resale as a clean, sorted driver \u2014 not a science project"],"cons":["starting condition swings the budget by tens of thousands","ARB locker installation requires professional axle setup","35s on 4.56 with the stock Dana 20 is marginal \u2014 4.88 is preferred for 35s"]},{"id":"bronco-cooling-electric-fan","title":"Electric Fan Conversion \u2014 Worth It or Not","vehicle":"bronco","category":"cooling","subcategory":"fan","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":400},"tools_required":["socket set","wire crimpers","multimeter","drill","zip ties","thread sealant"],"skills_required":["12V wiring","relay and thermostat-switch install","reading a temp gauge"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","daily_driver"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["cooling","electric fan","radiator","overheating","charging load"],"parts":[{"name":"16\" puller fan with thermostat kit (2,000+ CFM)","vendor":"SPAL / Derale","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Adjustable thermostatic fan switch + relay harness","vendor":"Derale / Hayden","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Fan shroud (matched to radiator)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":70}],"citations":[{"title":"Electric fan sizing and CFM","url":"https://www.spalusa.com/"},{"title":"Early Bronco cooling threads","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-cooling-radiator-upgrade","bronco-electrical-alternator-upgrade"],"summary":"An electric fan is worth it on an early Bronco mainly for low-speed crawling and engine-bay clearance, not because it cures highway overheating \u2014 that's almost always a radiator or timing problem. If your truck runs hot on the trail at idle, an electric puller fan with a good shroud helps. If it runs hot at 65 mph, fix the radiator first.","description":"Swapping the engine-driven fan for an electric fan does a few real things: it pulls air at idle and crawl speed when the mechanical fan is barely spinning, it frees up a little power and a little engine-bay room (handy after a V8 swap), and it lets you control cooling with a thermostat instead of engine RPM. What it does not reliably do is fix a truck that overheats on the highway \u2014 at speed the airflow through the radiator is doing the work, and an undersized or clogged radiator will cook regardless of the fan.\n\nBe honest about your problem before spending the money. Trail overheating at low speed is a fan-and-shroud problem. Highway overheating is a radiator, timing, or coolant-flow problem. The electric fan only addresses the first.\n\nSize the fan to the engine and radiator. A 170 I6 lives fine with a single 14\u201316\" puller fan around 1,500\u20132,000 CFM. A 302 or 351W wants 2,000\u20132,500 CFM, usually a 16\" fan or dual fans. A puller fan mounted behind the radiator with a proper shroud always beats a pusher fan in front \u2014 the shroud is what makes the fan pull through the whole core instead of a circle in the middle.\n\nYou also need a control: an adjustable thermostatic switch that closes a relay to power the fan above a set temperature (typically on at ~195\u00b0F, off at ~180\u00b0F), wired through a relay because the fan draws 15\u201330 amps and you do not want that through a dash switch.\n\nMount the fan and shroud to the radiator, keeping the blades clear of the core and tank. Run the fan's power through a relay fed directly from the battery with an inline fuse \u2014 the thermostatic switch triggers the relay's coil, not the fan current itself. Mount the temp sensor in the radiator (in-tank probe or threaded port), set the on-temp, and ground everything to clean bare metal.\n\nWire a manual override switch in parallel so you can force the fan on during a hard crawl or recovery. Test by letting the engine warm up and confirming the fan kicks on at your set temperature and off when it cools.\n\nThe biggest mistake is treating an electric fan as a cure for a tired radiator. If the core is clogged or undersized for a V8 swap, the fan won't save it.\n\nCharging-system load matters. A 16\" fan pulling 20\u201330 amps on a Bronco still running a 40\u201360 amp alternator can drag the system down at idle, especially with lights and a winch. If you're going electric fan plus accessories, plan the alternator upgrade alongside it.\n\nA fan with no shroud, or mounted with the blades too close to the core, moves far less air than its rating and can chew the radiator. Use the matched shroud.\n\nA quality 16\" puller fan with thermostat kit runs $150\u2013250. A matched shroud adds $50\u201390. A relay-and-switch harness is $40\u201370. Budget no-name fans under $80 move less air than their box claims and burn out \u2014 skip them. Total for a done-right single-fan setup is $200\u2013350.\n\nYou probably don't need a dual-fan setup unless you've swapped a big V8 and crawl in the heat. A single well-shrouded 16\" fan handles most early Bronco builds, and pairing it with a healthy radiator solves more than the fan alone ever will."},{"id":"bronco-cooling-radiator-upgrade","title":"Cooling System Upgrade \u2014 Radiator, Fan, and Flow","vehicle":"bronco","category":"cooling","subcategory":"radiator","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":650},"tools_required":["socket set","screwdrivers","drain pan","hose clamp pliers","coolant"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","cooling system fill and bleed"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["cooling","radiator","overheating","fan","aluminum radiator"],"parts":[{"name":"3-row aluminum radiator (EB V8 fit)","vendor":"Champion / Griffin","price_estimate_usd":260},{"name":"Flex-a-lite electric fan kit with thermostat","vendor":"Flex-a-lite","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"180\u00b0F thermostat","vendor":"Stant","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"Silicone radiator hose set","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":55}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco cooling upgrades","url":"https://www.tomsbroncoparts.com/"},{"title":"Electric fan conversion guidance","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-maint-tune-up"],"summary":"If your early Bronco runs hot crawling in low range or sitting in traffic, the fix is almost always airflow at low speed, not radiator size \u2014 a multi-row aluminum radiator plus a thermostatic electric fan solves the overwhelming majority of EB overheating complaints. Throwing a colder thermostat at it without fixing airflow only masks the problem.","description":"Early Broncos overheat in two situations: crawling slow in low range with the V8 working hard, and idling in summer traffic. Both are low-airflow conditions where the stock mechanical fan and a tired single-row radiator can't shed enough heat. A 3- or 4-row aluminum radiator paired with a thermostatically controlled electric fan moves enough air at zero road speed to hold temperature.\n\nThe honest verdict: most EB cooling problems are airflow, not coolant capacity. Before spending on a radiator, confirm the basics \u2014 correct thermostat, no air pocket, clean fins, a working fan clutch (if mechanical), and proper timing. A radiator upgrade on top of a fixed foundation is the lasting answer.\n\nFor a V8 early Bronco, a 3-row aluminum crossflow or downflow radiator sized for the EB core support is the baseline; 4-row for big V8s or desert heat. An electric fan with a thermostatic controller (turns on around 195\u00b0F, off around 175\u00b0F) replaces or supplements the mechanical fan and is the part that actually saves you at idle and crawl speed. A correct 180\u00b0F thermostat and fresh silicone hoses round it out.\n\nDrain the coolant into a pan, pull the old radiator and shroud, and test-fit the new radiator to the core support \u2014 EB-specific radiators bolt in, universal ones may need bracket work. Mount the electric fan to the radiator (pusher in front or puller behind; puller is more efficient) and wire the controller to a switched 12V source through a relay, with the temperature sensor in the radiator or intake. Install the 180\u00b0 thermostat, fit new hoses, fill slowly with the heater on and the front of the truck raised to push air out, and run it up to temp to burp the system.\n\nWire the fan through a relay, never straight off a switch \u2014 an electric fan pulls 15\u201330 amps and will cook a thin wire or switch. Add an inline fuse.\n\nAir pockets are the most common post-job overheat. Fill with the nose up and the heater valve open, run to temp with the cap off until the thermostat opens and coolant circulates, then top off. A no-spill funnel makes this far cleaner.\n\nDon't delete the thermostat to \"run cooler.\" Without it the coolant moves through the radiator too fast to shed heat and the engine never reaches efficient operating temp. Run the correct 180\u00b0.\n\nIf you keep the mechanical fan, a proper shroud matters more than the fan itself \u2014 without a shroud the fan pulls air through only the center of the core.\n\nCheck timing and mixture too. A V8 running retarded timing or lean dumps heat into the cooling system no radiator can keep up with.\n\nA quality EB aluminum radiator runs $200\u2013320 (Champion, Griffin, Be Cool). An electric fan kit with thermostatic controller is $120\u2013250. Add a thermostat ($14) and silicone hose set ($55). A full upgrade lands at $300\u2013650. Toms Bronco Parts and Wild Horses sell radiators cut for the EB core support \u2014 a direct-fit unit saves a fabrication afternoon. You probably don't need a 4-row unless you run a stroker or live somewhere genuinely hot; a 3-row handles a stock-ish 302 fine."},{"id":"bronco-dt-dana20-tc","title":"Dana 20 Transfer Case \u2014 Specs, Service, and Limitations","vehicle":"bronco","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"transfer_case","type":"reference","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":350},"tools_required":["drain pan","socket set","torque wrench","gasket scraper"],"skills_required":["basic drivetrain service"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["dana 20","transfer case","drivetrain","t-case","service"],"parts":[{"name":"Dana 20 rebuild kit (bearings, seals, gaskets)","vendor":"Spicer / Various","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Dana 20 output shaft seal (rear)","vendor":"National Seals","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Gear oil 80W-90 (1 quart)","vendor":"Pennzoil / Mobil 1","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"Dana 20 service information","url":"https://www.jeep4x4center.com/dana20.htm"},{"title":"Transfer case identification guide","url":"https://www.broncodriver.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-dt-sye-cv-driveshaft","bronco-dt-driveshaft-upgrade","bronco-axle-gear-ratio"],"summary":"The Dana 20 is a robust, gear-driven transfer case with a 2.03:1 low range \u2014 adequate for mild trail use, marginal for serious rock crawling. Service it every 30,000 miles and watch the output shaft seals.","description":"All 1966\u20131977 Broncos used the Dana 20 part-time transfer case. It's a chain-free, gear-driven unit \u2014 extremely reliable and direct to service. This page is the reference for what it is, how it's geared, how to keep it alive, and where its limits are.\n\nKey numbers for the early Bronco Dana 20:\n\n- Low range ratio: 2.03:1\n- High range: 1:1 (direct)\n- Shift: floor-mounted twin levers (2H / 4H / 4L)\n- Input: from the transmission output\n- Front output: to the front driveshaft\n- Rear output: long shaft with a sliding slip yoke (relevant once you lift \u2014 see below)\n\nCrawl ratio is the number that matters off-road. With a 3-speed manual (3.07:1 first gear), the 2.03:1 low range, and a 4.09:1 axle ratio: 3.07 \u00d7 2.03 \u00d7 4.09 = **25.5:1**. That's acceptable for mild trails and forest roads, but inadequate for technical rock crawling without steeper axle gearing. If you're chasing a deeper crawl ratio, the lever to pull is axle ratio \u2014 see [Gear Ratio Selection](/db/bronco/bronco-axle-gear-ratio/), because the Dana 20's 2.03 low range is fixed and the transmission first gear is what it is.\n\nThe Dana 20 asks for very little, but ignore it and the cheap failures turn expensive:\n\n- Drain and refill gear oil every 30,000 miles. Use 80W-90 GL-5 gear oil. Capacity is roughly 1.5 pints \u2014 fill to the bottom of the fill plug hole.\n- Inspect the output shaft seals annually. A leaking rear seal soaks the driveshaft yoke and eventually destroys the driveshaft U-joint. An $18 seal prevents a $250 U-joint and a roadside failure.\n- Inspect the front output yoke for elongation \u2014 the retaining nut backs off over time on high-mileage units, and a loose yoke chews the splines.\n- After any water crossing deep enough to submerge the case, check the oil for milky contamination and change it if water got past the seals.\n\nIf the Dana 20 won't shift into 4L or pops out of low range under load, the most common cause is a worn detent ball and spring in the shift rail. A rebuild kit ($180) includes these along with all bearings, seals, and gaskets, and the unit comes apart on a bench with hand tools \u2014 there's no chain or pump to fight. While it's open, replace the rear output seal while you are in there rather than chasing a leak later.\n\nTwo real-world notes for a built Bronco:\n\nThe factory rear output uses a long shaft and slip yoke. Once you lift the truck about 4 inches or more, the rear driveshaft angle gets steep and the slip yoke is exposed to trail damage. The fix is a slip yoke eliminator with a CV-style driveshaft \u2014 covered in [Slip Yoke Eliminator and CV Driveshaft](/db/bronco/bronco-dt-sye-cv-driveshaft/). For milder lifts, a [revised stock-style driveshaft](/db/bronco/bronco-dt-driveshaft-upgrade/) with fresh U-joints is often enough.\n\nSome builders swap to a Dana 300 (1980\u20131986 CJ) for a deeper 2.62:1 low range. This is a custom fabrication project \u2014 the adapter, bolt pattern, and dimensions differ from the Dana 20. It's not a common early Bronco modification, and most owners get where they need to go by regearing the axles instead.","pros":["gear-driven (no chain to stretch or replace)","direct, proven design that rebuilds on a bench with hand tools","cheap parts and excellent reliability"],"cons":["2.03:1 low range is modest versus modern transfer cases","limited crawl ratio without steep axle gearing","twin-lever shift can be notchy when cold","exposed long rear output and slip yoke once the truck is lifted"]},{"id":"bronco-dt-driveshaft-upgrade","title":"Driveshaft and U-Joint Upgrades for a Lifted Early Bronco","vehicle":"bronco","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"driveshaft","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":700},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","u-joint press or shop press","paint pen (for phasing marks)","angle finder"],"skills_required":["measuring driveshaft length and angle","pressing u-joints","phasing a driveshaft","checking pinion angle"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["drivetrain","driveshaft","u-joint","vibration","lift","pinion angle","drivetrain"],"parts":[{"name":"Rear CV / double-cardan driveshaft (built to length)","vendor":"Tom Wood's / High Angle","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Front driveshaft (lengthened or CV)","vendor":"Tom Wood's","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Heavy-duty u-joints (1310 series)","vendor":"Spicer","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"Degree shims or adjustable arm hardware","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":40}],"citations":[{"title":"Driveshaft angle and lift vibration","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"},{"title":"CV driveshaft and pinion angle setup","url":"https://www.tomwoods.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-susp-4in-lift","bronco-susp-soa","bronco-dt-dana20-tc"],"summary":"Lift an early Bronco a few inches and the stock driveshafts start working at angles they were never built for \u2014 the result is a vibration at speed and u-joints that wear out fast. The fix depends on how much lift: 2.5\u20133\" of lift can often be corrected with pinion-angle shims and fresh u-joints, but past about 3.5\", and especially with a spring-over conversion, you want a CV (double-cardan) driveshaft built to length. Diagnose the angle before you buy a shaft \u2014 a vibration is a geometry problem, not a balance problem, most of the time.","description":"When you lift the truck, the axles drop away from the transfer case and transmission, steepening the angle the driveshaft has to turn through at each u-joint. A single u-joint running at a steep angle speeds up and slows down twice per revolution; that pulsing is the vibration you feel, and it's also what eats the joint. The taller the lift, the worse it gets.\n\nThe honest answer: don't reflexively buy driveshafts when you lift. Measure first. A mild lift with correct pinion angle and good u-joints often runs smooth. Tall lifts and spring-overs genuinely need the CV shaft.\n\nAn angle finder to measure driveshaft and pinion angles, a u-joint press (a shop press or a screw-type tool), heavy-duty 1310-series Spicer u-joints, and \u2014 if the geometry calls for it \u2014 a CV/double-cardan driveshaft built to your truck's measured length and angle by a driveshaft shop. For pinion-angle correction you'll want degree shims (leaf-spring trucks) or adjustable suspension links if you've converted to a link setup.\n\nDiagnose first. With the truck at ride height on the ground, measure the angle of the driveshaft and the angle of the pinion (the yoke on the axle) with an angle finder. For a standard two-u-joint shaft, the working angle at each end should be small and roughly equal, and the pinion should point at the transfer case output within a couple of degrees. A spring-over or a tall lift typically leaves the pinion pointing well below the shaft \u2014 that's your vibration.\n\nFor mild lifts, correct the rear pinion angle with degree shims between the spring and axle (or by adjusting links), aiming the pinion up toward the transfer case output. Replace tired u-joints with fresh 1310s and grease them. This alone cures many 2.5\u20133\" lift vibrations.\n\nFor tall lifts and spring-overs, fit a CV (double-cardan) driveshaft. A CV shaft puts a pair of joints at the transfer case end so it tolerates a steep operating angle, and the pinion is then set to point directly at the driveshaft rather than splitting the angle. Have the shaft built to your measured collapsed and extended lengths so it can't bottom out at full droop or pull apart at full stuff.\n\nWhen you reinstall any shaft, phase it correctly \u2014 the slip-yoke splines have a relationship that must line up, marked by an arrow or a missing spline. Reassembling a shaft out of phase introduces its own vibration. Torque the U-bolts or strap bolts to spec.\n\nThe most common mistake is treating a lift-angle vibration as a balance problem and sending the shaft out for balancing repeatedly. Balancing won't fix an angle problem; measure the angles first.\n\nWatch driveshaft length at the extremes. On a flexy trail truck, a shaft that's correct at ride height can run out of slip-yoke travel at full droop and pull apart, or bottom the slip and bind at full compression \u2014 both are destructive. Measure collapsed and extended length through the full range and have the shaft built for it.\n\nDon't reuse old u-joints to save $50 when you're already under there. A failed u-joint at speed can whip the driveshaft into the floor or the brake lines. Fresh, greaseable joints are cheap insurance.\n\nPinion shims and a pair of u-joints to cure a mild-lift vibration run $60\u2013120 in parts. A built CV rear driveshaft runs $300\u2013400; a matched front shaft is similar, so a both-ends job lands around $600\u2013700. Tom Wood's and other off-road driveshaft specialists build them to your measurements and know the early Bronco geometry. You probably don't need new driveshafts at all on a 2.5\" lift \u2014 shims and u-joints fix most of those \u2014 so measure before you spend, and put the savings toward gearing if you also went up in tire size."},{"id":"bronco-dt-sye-cv-driveshaft","title":"Slip Yoke Eliminator and CV Driveshaft \u2014 Lifted-Rig Vibration Fix","vehicle":"bronco","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"driveshaft","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":600,"high":1100},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands (4)","torque wrench","snap ring pliers","digital angle gauge or driveline angle finder","pry bar","seal driver","drain pan","paint marker"],"skills_required":["transfer case service","driveline angle measurement","pinion angle adjustment"],"build_tiers":["weekend_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["SYE","slip yoke eliminator","CV driveshaft","dana20","drivetrain","vibration"],"parts":[{"name":"Slip yoke eliminator / fixed yoke kit for Dana 20","vendor":"Advance Adapters","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"CV-style (double-cardan) rear driveshaft, built to measurement","vendor":"Tom Woods Custom Drive Shafts","price_estimate_usd":500},{"name":"Transfer case rear output seal","vendor":"National / Timken","price_estimate_usd":15},{"name":"Tapered pinion angle shims, pair","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"Transfer case fluid (GL-4 80W-90, 4 qt)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":40}],"citations":[{"title":"SYE and CV driveshaft theory \u2014 Tom Woods Custom Drive Shafts","url":"https://www.4xshaft.com/"},{"title":"Advance Adapters \u2014 Dana 20 fixed yoke conversion","url":"https://www.advanceadapters.com/"},{"title":"Driveline angle discussion \u2014 ClassicBroncos forums","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-dt-driveshaft-upgrade","bronco-dt-dana20-tc","bronco-susp-soa"],"summary":"If you lifted your early Bronco 4 inches or more and feel a vibration that worsens with speed, the rear driveline angle is almost certainly the cause. A slip yoke eliminator paired with a CV-style rear driveshaft is the correct fix, and it costs roughly $600-850 for the pair with a custom-built shaft. This is not optional equipment on a tall build: running steep angles on a stock single-joint shaft chews U-joints, and a slip yoke torn off on the trail dumps your transfer case fluid on the ground. Below about 2.5\" of lift you likely do not need it \u2014 a quality stock-style shaft with fresh joints runs smooth at mild height.","description":"Lift raises the body and the transfer case relative to the axles. The rear driveshaft then operates at a steeper angle, and a conventional U-joint at each end transmits that angle as a twice-per-revolution speed fluctuation you feel through the floor. The taller the lift, the worse it gets.\n\nA single Cardan U-joint running at an angle does not transmit rotation at a constant speed \u2014 the output speeds up and slows down twice per revolution. In a stock driveline, the joint at each end runs at the same angle in opposite directions, so the two fluctuations cancel and you feel nothing.\n\nLift breaks that cancellation in two ways. The operating angles at the two ends are no longer equal, and both are steeper than the joint was designed to run. Practical limits worth remembering:\n\n- **Under 3 degrees** operating angle at each joint: smooth at any speed.\n- **3 to 7 degrees**, equal and opposite at both ends: acceptable, with U-joint life falling as angle rises.\n- **Over 7-8 degrees** on a single-joint shaft: vibration and rapid joint wear. This is the range a 4\" early Bronco lands in.\n- **Over 12 degrees**: joint failure becomes a question of when, not whether.\n\nThe other thing to know: a short shaft at a given lift height runs a steeper angle than a long one, because the vertical offset is spread over less length. That is the mechanism the SYE exploits.\n\nThe factory Dana 20 uses a long rear output shaft with a sliding slip yoke that the driveshaft rides on. A slip yoke eliminator replaces the long output and slip section with a short, fixed yoke. Two things improve at once:\n\n1. **The driveshaft gets longer** \u2014 typically 4-6 inches \u2014 which reduces the operating angle for the same lift height.\n2. **The slip function moves into the driveshaft itself**, where a splined slip section sits behind a CV joint that can handle a large angle.\n\nThere is a trail benefit as well. The factory long output shaft is exposed and vulnerable. If a rear driveshaft gets torn off on an obstacle, the long slip yoke lets all the transfer case fluid drain out and strands you. The SYE's short fixed output keeps the case sealed. For a truck that wheels seriously, that alone justifies the cost.\n\nFor background on what you are working inside, read the [Dana 20 transfer case guide](/db/bronco/bronco-dt-dana20-tc/) before you start \u2014 the case is gear-driven cast iron and forgiving, but the output shaft and bearing stack want to come apart in the right order.\n\nA constant-velocity (double-cardan) joint is a pair of U-joints with a centering ball between them. It splits one large operating angle into two smaller ones and cancels the speed fluctuation internally. With the SYE aiming the transfer case output at the pinion and the CV joint absorbing the remaining angle, the driveline runs smooth even at significant lift.\n\nThe pinion angle has to change to match. With a CV shaft, the pinion is rotated to point directly at the CV joint center \u2014 not parallel to the transfer case output, which is the setup a conventional two-joint shaft wants. Aim for the pinion within 0-3 degrees of pointing at the CV, with the single U-joint at the pinion end running under 3 degrees. On a leaf-sprung Bronco you achieve this with tapered shims between the spring and the perch, or by cutting and rotating the perches on a rebuilt axle.\n\nTom Woods, Adams, and JE Reel all build to your numbers, so the measurement is the job.\n\n1. Put the truck on the ground at final ride height with the lift fully installed and the suspension settled. Measurements taken on jack stands are meaningless.\n2. With a digital angle gauge, record the transfer case output flange angle, the driveshaft angle, and the pinion flange angle. Note whether each is nose-up or nose-down.\n3. Measure the shaft length from the SYE yoke's U-joint center to the pinion yoke's U-joint center, with the suspension at ride height.\n4. Report all of it to the driveshaft builder along with your lift height and tire size. A good builder will tell you if your pinion angle needs correcting before they cut the shaft.\n\nOrder the shaft only after the lift is installed and the pinion angle is set. Ordering off a projected ride height is the most common reason a new shaft still vibrates.\n\n- **Replace the rear output seal.** The SYE kit disturbs it, and reusing an old seal produces a leak that means pulling everything apart again. Fifteen dollars against three hours.\n- **Mark the driveshaft orientation** before removing anything so you can index the new parts consistently.\n- **Torque the yoke nut to the kit spec** and use the supplied retention method. A yoke nut that backs off destroys the output shaft splines.\n- **Refill the case** with the correct fluid \u2014 the Dana 20 takes roughly 3.5-4 quarts of GL-4 80W-90. Check the drain and fill plugs for the flat-washer seals.\n- **Road test in stages**: 30 mph, 45, then highway. A vibration that appears only above 55 mph usually means the pinion angle needs a shim change, not a shaft problem.\n\nA transfer case drop kit (spacers that lower the case 1\") is the $60 alternative and it does reduce driveline angle. The tradeoff is real: you give up an inch of the ground clearance under the transfer case you paid for with the lift, and that is the lowest point on the truck's belly on most builds. It is a reasonable stopgap at 2.5-3\" of lift and a poor answer at 4\" and up.\n\nIf your vibration only shows up under hard acceleration rather than scaling with road speed, the problem is likely axle wrap or worn U-joints instead of geometry \u2014 check those against the [driveshaft and U-joint upgrade guide](/db/bronco/bronco-dt-driveshaft-upgrade/) before spending on an SYE.\n\n| Item | Typical cost |\n|---|---|\n| SYE / fixed yoke kit for Dana 20 | $280-400 |\n| CV rear driveshaft, custom built | $450-600 |\n| Rear output seal and transfer case fluid | $55 |\n| Pinion shims | $30 |\n| **Rear driveline total** | **$800-1,050** |\n| Front CV shaft as well (SOA / tall builds) | +$450-600 |\n\nBudget $600 if you source a used SYE and a shorter shaft, $850 for new parts done properly, and past $1,300 if a [spring-over-axle build](/db/bronco/bronco-susp-soa/) means the front shaft needs the same treatment.","pros":["eliminates lift-induced driveline vibration at its cause rather than masking it","keeps transfer case fluid in the case if the rear shaft is lost on the trail","longer shaft reduces operating angle and extends U-joint life","a capable home-garage job at three to eight hours"],"cons":["costs $800-1,050 done properly with a custom shaft","requires accurate angle measurement at ride height, on the ground","pinion angle usually needs shims or perch rotation to suit the CV setup","unnecessary below roughly 2.5\" of lift, where a quality stock-style shaft runs smooth"]},{"id":"bronco-electrical-alternator-upgrade","title":"Charging System Upgrade \u2014 Higher-Output Alternator","vehicle":"bronco","category":"electrical","subcategory":"charging","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":450},"tools_required":["socket set","wire crimpers","multimeter","wire stripper","heat gun (heat shrink)"],"skills_required":["12V wiring","crimping high-current lugs","reading a voltmeter"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","daily_driver"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["electrical","alternator","charging","winch","EFI","wiring"],"parts":[{"name":"1-wire or 3-wire alternator (100-140 amp, EB bracket fit)","vendor":"Powermaster / Tuff Stuff","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"4-gauge charge wire + lugs","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Upgraded ground straps (engine-to-frame, frame-to-body)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":25}],"citations":[{"title":"High-output alternator selection","url":"https://www.powermastermotorsports.com/"},{"title":"Early Bronco charging upgrades","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-electrical-wiring-harness","bronco-recovery-winch-install"],"summary":"Upgrade the alternator when you add load the factory 40\u201360 amp unit can't carry \u2014 EFI, an electric fan, a winch, or a stack of lights. For a stock-ish trail truck the original charging system is adequate; add it up before you spend. The number that matters is total amp draw with everything on, and the alternator should beat it with margin at idle.","description":"The early Bronco left the factory with a 40\u201355 amp alternator, sized for points ignition, a heater, and headlights. Modern additions change that math fast: an EFI conversion needs steady voltage and pulls a fuel pump, an electric fan is 15\u201330 amps, a winch under load can pull 200+ amps momentarily, and LED or halogen light bars add up. When the system can't keep up, you get dim lights at idle, a fuel pump that drops out, and a battery that slowly dies.\n\nThe honest version: if your Bronco is mostly stock with points or a basic HEI and no winch, the factory alternator is fine and this is money better spent elsewhere. The upgrade earns its keep the moment you add EFI, an electric fan, or a winch.\n\nPick an alternator that beats your total load at idle, not only at cruise \u2014 trail electrical demand happens at low RPM. Add up the continuous draws (fan, fuel pump, lights, ignition, accessories); a winch is intermittent but still wants a healthy system behind it. A 100\u2013140 amp unit covers most built early Broncos. One-wire alternators are tidy to install but only start charging above a certain RPM; three-wire units sense voltage and charge at idle better, which is what you want for a trail rig.\n\nEqually important is the wiring. A bigger alternator pushing current through the original undersized charge wire and tired grounds gains you little. Upgrade the charge wire to 4-gauge and add fresh engine-to-frame and frame-to-body ground straps.\n\nMatch the alternator to the early Bronco bracket or use an adapter \u2014 V8-swap trucks may already have a different bracket. Mount it, set belt tension so the belt deflects about 1/2\" under thumb pressure. Run a new 4-gauge charge wire from the alternator output post to the battery positive (or starter solenoid stud), fused or with a fusible link near the battery. Upgrade the grounds. For a three-wire unit, connect the sense and exciter wires per the alternator's diagram.\n\nStart the truck and check voltage at the battery with a multimeter: 13.8\u201314.4V at idle with accessories off is healthy. Turn on the fan and lights and confirm it holds above 13V at idle.\n\nThe most common mistake is a big alternator on small wire. Current the charge wire can't carry shows up as voltage drop and heat, and you lose the benefit you paid for.\n\nGrounds are the silent failure on a 50-year-old truck. Corroded engine and body grounds cause the same dim-lights symptom as a weak alternator. Replace the straps when you upgrade \u2014 it's the cheapest part of the job and often the real fix.\n\nA fusible link or fuse at the battery end of the charge wire is not optional. An unfused high-current wire that chafes through to the frame is a fire risk.\n\nA quality 100\u2013140 amp alternator runs $130\u2013280. Charge wire and lugs add $30\u201340, ground straps $20\u201330. Premium 200+ amp units for serious winch and audio builds run $350\u2013450 and are overkill for a trail truck. Total for a sensible upgrade with wiring is $200\u2013350.\n\nYou probably don't need a 200-amp alternator. Size it to your real load with margin \u2014 most built early Broncos are well served by a 120-amp three-wire unit and fresh wiring, and the new grounds alone fix more problems than people expect."},{"id":"bronco-elec-headlight-relay-upgrade","title":"Headlight Relay Harness and LED Upgrade","vehicle":"bronco","category":"electrical","subcategory":"lighting","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":250},"tools_required":["wire crimpers","wire strippers","test light or multimeter","drill (for relay mount)","heat shrink and lighter"],"skills_required":["12V wiring","crimping connectors","circuit testing"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","weekend_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["headlights","relay harness","LED","electrical","lighting","wiring"],"parts":[{"name":"Headlight relay harness (H4 ceramic, dual relay)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"LED or halogen H4 conversion bulbs (pair)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"Inline fuses and ring terminals","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":15}],"citations":[{"title":"Headlight Relay Harness \u2014 why and how","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-elec-wiring-harness","bronco-elec-alternator-upgrade"],"summary":"If your early Bronco's headlights are dim and yellow, the bulbs are probably not the problem \u2014 the wiring is. Fifty-year-old harnesses, a long path through the headlight switch, and aged connectors drop voltage before it ever reaches the bulb. A relay harness feeds the headlights straight from the battery and lets the factory switch trigger relays instead of carrying the full current. Pair it with modern bulbs and the difference at night is dramatic.","description":"The factory headlight circuit routes full lamp current through the headlight switch, the dimmer switch, and a long run of original wiring. Every connection and every foot of aged wire adds resistance, and resistance drops voltage. A bulb rated for 12.8 volts might see 11 or less at the socket, and brightness falls off fast with voltage. The bulbs are doing their best with what they get.\n\nA relay harness adds two relays near the battery. The factory headlight switch no longer carries lamp current \u2014 it only energizes the relay coils, a tiny load. The relays then switch heavy-gauge wire running directly from a fused battery feed to the headlights. The bulbs get nearly full battery voltage, so they run brighter and the factory switch runs cooler and lasts longer.\n\nThis is one of the highest-value electrical upgrades on the truck for the money. It improves the lights you have before you spend anything on bulbs.\n\nWith proper voltage at the socket, you can choose:\n\n**Upgraded halogen H4** bulbs are inexpensive, give a clean beam pattern in the factory reflector, and are a reliable improvement. They are the safe default.\n\n**LED H4 conversions** draw less current and run cooler, and the good ones are noticeably brighter. The catch is beam pattern \u2014 a poorly designed LED in a reflector housing built for a filament can scatter light and blind oncoming drivers without lighting the road well. Buy LEDs designed to place the emitters where the halogen filament sat, and aim them after install. Cheap LED bulbs are the common disappointment here.\n\n1. Mount the relays near the battery, away from exhaust heat.\n2. Run a fused feed from the battery positive to the relay common terminals.\n3. Tap the factory headlight wiring at the bulb connectors to trigger the relay coils (low and high beam).\n4. Run new heavy-gauge wire from the relays to the headlight connectors.\n5. Ground the relays and the new circuit to clean bare metal.\n6. Test low and high beam, then aim the lights against a wall at the correct distance.\n\nA pre-made harness comes with the relays, fuses, and connectors sized correctly, which makes this a clean afternoon job rather than a wiring project.\n\nGrounds matter as much as the feed. A relay harness that improves the positive side but grounds to a corroded factory point still loses voltage. Run the grounds to clean, bare metal and protect the connection.\n\nAim the headlights after any bulb change. Brighter lights aimed wrong are worse than dim lights aimed right \u2014 for you and for oncoming traffic.\n\nYou probably don't need an LED light bar to see at night on an early Bronco. Fix the headlight circuit and run decent bulbs first; that solves the actual visibility problem for a fraction of the cost.\n\n- Relay harness (H4 dual relay): $45\n- H4 bulbs (halogen or quality LED): $80\n- Fuses and terminals: $15\n\nBudget $60 for harness plus good halogens, up to $250 for a harness with premium LED conversions. Start with the harness \u2014 it improves whatever bulbs you run."},{"id":"bronco-elec-wiring-harness","title":"Wiring Harness Replacement and Charging Upgrade","vehicle":"bronco","category":"electrical","subcategory":"wiring","type":"upgrade","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":25},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":900},"tools_required":["wire crimpers","wire strippers","multimeter","soldering iron","heat gun","test light","socket set"],"skills_required":["reading wiring diagrams","crimping and soldering","circuit testing"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["electrical","wiring harness","charging","alternator","fuse box"],"parts":[{"name":"Complete reproduction wiring harness (year-specific)","vendor":"Painless / Centech","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"1-wire 100A alternator conversion","vendor":"Powermaster / Tuff Stuff","price_estimate_usd":130},{"name":"Modern blade-fuse panel","vendor":"Centech","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Heat-shrink connectors and loom","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":40}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco wiring harness options","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"},{"title":"Alternator conversion guidance","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-maint-tune-up"],"summary":"Fifty-year-old wiring is the root cause of more early Bronco gremlins than any other system \u2014 brittle insulation, corroded grounds, and an undersized fuse box that was marginal when new. If you're chasing intermittent electrical faults, a full harness replacement plus a modern alternator and fuse panel ends the whack-a-mole for good.","description":"The original early Bronco harness uses cloth-and-vinyl insulation that's now brittle, grounds that have spent decades corroding, and a fuse box with too few circuits. The classic symptoms are dim or flickering lights, gauges that read wrong, an ammeter pegging, and faults that come and go with bumps. You can chase these one at a time for years, or replace the harness and be done.\n\nThe honest assessment: a full harness swap is a real project \u2014 plan a weekend or two, not an afternoon \u2014 and it demands patience and a wiring diagram. But it's within reach for a methodical DIYer, and a labeled reproduction harness makes it far more approachable than splicing into the original. If your truck's electrical issues are isolated to one circuit, fix that circuit; if they're everywhere, replace the harness.\n\nA reproduction harness comes two ways: a faithful year-correct OEM-style harness (Bronco Graveyard, Wild Horses) that plugs into original-style components, or a modern labeled chassis harness (Painless, Centech) with a blade-fuse panel and relay provisions. The modern route adds circuits for electric fans, lights, and accessories and uses standard ATO fuses. Pair either with a 1-wire alternator conversion \u2014 the original generator or low-output alternator can't keep up with electric fans, modern lighting, and a winch.\n\nPhotograph and label everything before you touch it. Disconnect the battery. Work one circuit at a time, removing the old wire as you run the new one so you never lose track of where it goes. Mount the new fuse panel somewhere dry and accessible (under the dash on the driver's side is typical). Run grounds to clean, bare metal and add a dedicated engine-to-frame and frame-to-body ground strap \u2014 bad grounds cause more \"electrical\" faults than bad hot wires. For the alternator conversion, run the proper-gauge charge wire to the battery or starter relay and delete or bypass the external voltage regulator per the alternator's instructions.\n\nCrimp with a ratcheting crimper and use heat-shrink connectors, then loom and secure the harness away from heat and sharp edges. Solder-and-shrink any high-vibration or critical joints.\n\nThe original ammeter routes full system current through the dash \u2014 a known fire risk on old Fords. When you rewire, convert to a voltmeter (which taps a low-current signal) and route charging current directly. This is the most important safety change in the whole job.\n\nGrounds are half the battle. Add engine-to-frame and frame-to-body straps and clean every ground point to bare metal. A \"dead\" component is often a bad ground, not a bad wire.\n\nDon't run a high-output alternator's charge wire on the original undersized gauge \u2014 it'll overheat. Match wire gauge to alternator output (typically 6 AWG for 100A over a short run).\n\nDisconnect the battery for the entire job, and fuse every new circuit. An unfused hot wire that chafes through to the frame on a 50-year-old truck is how Broncos burn.\n\nA reproduction OEM-style harness runs $250\u2013450; a modern Painless/Centech chassis harness with fuse panel is $400\u2013700. Add a 1-wire 100A alternator ($110\u2013160), a voltmeter to replace the ammeter ($30), and loom/connectors ($40). A full electrical refresh lands at $400\u2013900. Centech and Painless harnesses are the common upgrade path for EBs because they're labeled and add modern circuits; a faithful OEM reproduction is the choice for a numbers-matching restoration where originality matters more than extra circuits."},{"id":"bronco-eng-170-i6","title":"Factory 170 I6 \u2014 Specs and Limitations","vehicle":"bronco","category":"engine","subcategory":"factory","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["engine hoist","basic hand tools","compression tester"],"skills_required":[],"build_tiers":["daily_driver"],"year_compatibility":["1966"],"tags":["engine","i6","factory","170"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco Engine Options \u2014 Bronco Driver","url":"https://www.broncodriver.com/engine-options"},{"title":"Ford 170/200 small-six history \u2014 Falcon/Bronco","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_straight-six_engine"}],"alternatives":["bronco-eng-302-v8","bronco-eng-302-ho-swap"],"summary":"The 170 I6 is the rarest factory Bronco engine \u2014 1966 only, and almost every survivor has had it pulled. Treat a numbers-matching 170 truck as a collector piece to preserve, and treat a 170 in a driver as the starting point for a 302 swap. It is not a platform you build power on.","description":"The 1966 Bronco shipped standard with the 170 cubic inch (2.8L) inline-six, a derivative of the Falcon economy engine. Output was roughly 105 hp at 4,400 rpm and 158 lb-ft at 2,400 rpm, fed by a single-barrel Holley/Autolite carburetor and backed by a 3-speed manual. Ford replaced it with the larger 200 I6 for 1967, then offered the 289/302 V8 as the engine most surviving Broncos now wear.\n\nKey specs:\n- Displacement: 170 ci (2.8L)\n- Horsepower: ~105 hp @ 4,400 rpm\n- Torque: ~158 lb-ft @ 2,400 rpm\n- Bore \u00d7 stroke: 3.50 \u00d7 2.94 in\n- Compression: 9.1:1\n- Block: integral-log intake (intake runners cast into the head), 7 main bearings\n\nDecades of swaps mean a 1966 title doesn't guarantee a 170 is still under the hood -- plenty of these trucks picked up a 200 I6 or a 302 V8 along the way. The 170 and the later 200 share the same basic block architecture and look nearly identical at a glance, so don't assume from displacement badges or shop lore. The reliable check is a direct bore measurement: 3.50\" bores to a 170, 3.68\" to a 200. A machine shop or engine builder can confirm this in minutes with a bore gauge during a compression test or teardown. If you can't verify and the numbers matter to you -- for a numbers-matching restoration, they should -- pull the casting number off the block below the cylinder head and cross-reference it against a Ford small-six casting number list before you order parts or commit to \"170-specific\" pricing.\n\nThe 170 was engineered for light Falcon road duty, not for moving a 3,000-plus pound 4x4 on tall gearing. The integral-log cylinder head is the core limitation: the intake ports are cast into the head and cannot be replaced with a better manifold, so the engine cannot breathe past its factory ceiling no matter what carburetor you bolt on. Paired with the Dana 20's modest 2.03:1 low range (covered in the [Dana 20 transfer case guide](/db/bronco/bronco-dt-dana20-tc/)), the 170 leaves you lugging on climbs and downshifting constantly. Add 31\" or larger tires without [regearing the axles](/db/bronco/bronco-axle-gear-ratio/) and the truck becomes genuinely slow.\n\nA 170 rebuild is viable but not well supported. Standard wear parts \u2014 rings, bearings, a timing set, gaskets, a water pump \u2014 are shared with the broader Ford small-six family and remain available through suppliers like Summit and Rock Auto. The hard parts are what bite: a cracked integral-log head, a worn cam, or a spun crank can leave you hunting salvage yards, because nobody reproduces 170-specific castings. Budget a machine-shop rebuild at $2,000\u2013$3,500 depending on what the block needs, and accept that you are spending it for originality, not capability.\n\nIf you do keep it running, the maintenance basics still apply: a proper ignition and carburetor tune is the highest-value work you can do, and the same approach in the [tune-up guide](/db/bronco/bronco-maint-tune-up/) \u2014 fresh points or a breakerless conversion, correct timing, a clean carburetor \u2014 wakes up a tired six as much as a tired V8.\n\nBefore committing to a rebuild, run a compression test cold and find out what's actually wrong. Pull all six plugs, ground the coil wire so it can't fire, and crank each cylinder through 4-5 compression strokes with the throttle held wide open. A healthy 170 reads 130-150 psi across all six cylinders with less than 10% spread between the highest and lowest reading. Anything under 100 psi, or a spread past 15%, points to worn rings or valve trouble worth a real teardown rather than a gasket-and-tune refresh.\n\nIf one or two cylinders read low, add a small squirt of oil into the low cylinder through the plug hole and retest. A reading that climbs noticeably with the oil added points to worn rings, since the oil briefly reseals the ring gap; a reading that barely moves points to a valve or head-gasket problem instead. Given how scarce good 170 heads are, a compression test that turns up valve damage is often what actually decides whether you rebuild the original engine or move straight to the 302 swap below.\n\nFor a driver-grade 170 truck, the 302 Windsor is the established answer. The early Bronco was factory-engineered around the small-block Ford V8, so motor mounts, the Dana 20 adapter, and exhaust routing are all known quantities. Two routes:\n\n- **Carbureted 302:** the period-correct swap, detailed in the [factory 302 V8 guide](/db/bronco/bronco-eng-302-v8/). Strong parts availability, torquey, and it drops in with off-the-shelf mounts.\n- **Fuel-injected 5.0L HO:** the modern-reliability route, covered in the [302 HO / EFI swap guide](/db/bronco/bronco-eng-302-ho-swap/). Cold starts, altitude tolerance, and fuel economy all improve at the cost of wiring complexity.\n\nEither swap also means upgrading the cooling system and the charging system to V8 spec \u2014 plan those alongside the engine, not after.","pros":["numbers-matching originality on a 1966-only engine carries collector value","light front-end weight versus a V8","straightforward single-barrel carburetor with few moving parts"],"cons":["severely underpowered for any trail use, especially on larger tires","integral-log head caps breathing \u2014 no bolt-on intake fix exists","hard parts are scarce; a cracked head can strand a rebuild","no meaningful aftermarket performance support"]},{"id":"bronco-eng-302-ho-swap","title":"5.0L HO / EFI 302 Swap","vehicle":"bronco","category":"engine","subcategory":"swap","type":"task","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":20,"high":50},"cost_usd":{"low":1800,"high":4500},"tools_required":["engine hoist","load leveler","floor jack","complete socket set","torque wrench","multimeter","fuel pressure gauge","harmonic balancer puller","engine stand","compression tester"],"skills_required":["engine removal","wiring basics","fuel system knowledge","exhaust fabrication or sourcing"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["swap","302","5.0","ho","efi","engine"],"parts":[{"name":"Ford 5.0L HO long block (Fox-body donor)","vendor":"Salvage/Core","price_estimate_usd":900},{"name":"Edelbrock Performer 289/302 dual-plane intake","part_number":"2091","vendor":"Edelbrock","price_estimate_usd":260},{"name":"Edelbrock Performer 600 cfm carburetor, manual choke","part_number":"1405","vendor":"Edelbrock","price_estimate_usd":400},{"name":"Hooker shorty headers for 302 in early Bronco","vendor":"Hooker","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"MSD 6AL ignition box","part_number":"6420","vendor":"MSD","price_estimate_usd":210},{"name":"Front-sump truck oil pan, pickup, and dipstick","vendor":"Ford / Milodon","price_estimate_usd":150},{"name":"50 oz-in balanced flexplate or flywheel","vendor":"Ford Racing / Various","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Bronco V8 motor mounts (if swapping from I6)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":120}],"citations":[{"title":"5.0 HO swap discussion \u2014 ClassicBroncos forums","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"},{"title":"Fox-body 5.0L HO specifications","url":"https://www.ford-trucks.com/"},{"title":"Edelbrock Performer intake and carb application data","url":"https://www.edelbrock.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-eng-302-v8","bronco-eng-351w-v8","bronco-fuel-efi-conversion"],"summary":"The 5.0 HO (Fox-body 302) is the most cost-effective real power upgrade for a carbureted early Bronco. Bolt a dual-plane intake, shorty headers, and a 600 cfm carb onto an HO long block and you are making 240-260 hp for well under $3,000 \u2014 and because it is still a 302 Windsor, it drops onto existing Bronco V8 mounts. Build it carbureted for a clean install, or retain the factory EFI if you want modern cold-start and altitude behavior and can accept 20-30 hours of harness work. Three details decide whether this swap goes smoothly: the oil pan, the balance weight, and the firing order.","description":"The Fox-body 5.0L HO (High Output) ran from 1982-1995 in the Mustang and is the donor of choice because cores are cheap and everywhere. The block is a 302 Windsor, so it bolts where any Bronco 302 already lives and mates to the same small-block Ford bellhousing pattern your existing transmission uses.\n\nThe HO is a hot-cammed, better-breathing version of the same 302 covered in the [factory V8 guide](/db/bronco/bronco-eng-302-v8/). What to look for when shopping a donor:\n\n- **Compression:** 8.3-8.4:1 on the 1982-1986 HO, 9.0:1 on 1987 and later. The later engines make more power and tolerate pump gas fine.\n- **Camshaft:** 1985 and later are hydraulic roller blocks. A roller block is worth seeking out \u2014 it takes modern roller cams without a $400 conversion and does not have the flat-tappet break-in risk.\n- **Heads:** E7TE castings on 1987-1993 are adequate. GT40 heads (1993 Cobra, 1996-1997 Explorer) and GT40P heads (1997-2001 Explorer and Mountaineer) flow considerably better. GT40P heads have a relocated spark plug angle and need GT40P-specific headers \u2014 fitting standard headers to P heads results in plug wires against the tubes.\n- **Rating:** 225 hp / 300 lb-ft as delivered in a 1987-1993 Mustang. In a Bronco with a mild carb setup, expect 240-260 hp and 310+ lb-ft.\n\n**Balance weight.** Every 302 from 1982 forward is 50 oz-in externally balanced; 1981 and earlier 302s are 28 oz-in. The flexplate or flywheel and the harmonic balancer both carry the counterweight, so an HO long block bolted to your old Bronco's 28 oz-in flexplate will vibrate badly and eventually crack something. Buy the matching 50 oz-in flexplate or flywheel and use the donor's balancer.\n\n**Firing order.** The HO camshaft uses firing order 1-3-7-2-6-5-4-8, not the 1-5-4-2-6-3-7-8 of a standard 302. Wire the distributor cap to the HO order if you keep the HO cam. Installing a non-HO replacement cam flips you back to the standard order. Getting this wrong produces an engine that fires, runs terribly, and sends people chasing carburetor problems for a weekend.\n\n**Oil pan.** The Fox-body Mustang pan is a rear-sump design; the early Bronco's front crossmember demands a front-sump truck pan with the matching pickup tube and dipstick. Swap the pan on the stand before the engine goes in the truck \u2014 discovering the clearance problem with the engine hanging on the hoist costs an afternoon.\n\nFor most early Broncos, carbureted is the path of least resistance and the one most owners should choose. Drop the HO long block onto the existing 302 mounts and add:\n\n- Edelbrock Performer 289/302 dual-plane intake (~$260)\n- Edelbrock Performer 600 cfm 4-bbl, #1405 manual choke or #1406 electric (~$400), or a 650 cfm #1477 for larger builds\n- Hooker shorty headers into 2.5\" dual exhaust (~$280 plus fabrication)\n- Duraspark distributor, or a PerTronix Ignitor with an MSD 6AL box (~$210)\n- Mechanical fuel pump feeding 5-7 psi \u2014 no return line or high-pressure plumbing required\n\nThat combination gives 240-260 hp with a street tune and good idle quality on the dual-plane manifold. Total parts, with a $900 core, land near $2,200-2,600. Set it up per the [tune-up guide](/db/bronco/bronco-maint-tune-up/) once it runs, and run a [compression and leak-down test](/db/bronco/bronco-diag-compression-test/) on the core before you spend a dollar on top-end parts.\n\nIf the donor is a 1988-1993 Mustang, you can keep the mass-air EFI rather than carbureting it. That means retaining the donor ECU, engine harness, MAF, injectors, and TFI distributor, then stripping the harness of A/C, emissions, and body branches until only the engine circuits remain. Plan 20-30 hours for a first-time harness cleanup, and budget for the supporting systems the carb build does not need:\n\n- **High-pressure fuel supply.** EFI wants roughly 39 psi at the rail with a return line. That means an in-tank or frame-mounted high-pressure pump, a return line run, and usually a new sender \u2014 see the [fuel tank and sending unit guide](/db/bronco/bronco-fuel-tank-sending-unit/).\n- **Clean power and ground.** The ECU needs a dedicated switched feed and solid grounds. A 50-year-old Bronco harness is not a good foundation; pair this with the [wiring harness replacement](/db/bronco/bronco-elec-wiring-harness/).\n- **Accessory drive.** Fox-body 5.0s are serpentine; the early Bronco is V-belt. Pick one system and source a complete matching bracket set rather than mixing.\n\nThe payoff is genuinely better cold starts, better fuel economy, and automatic altitude compensation \u2014 meaningful if you wheel above 6,000 feet. If a turn-key injected setup is the actual goal, weigh this against a purpose-built [aftermarket EFI conversion](/db/bronco/bronco-fuel-efi-conversion/), which is designed for retrofit rather than adapted from a car harness and often costs less in hours than it does in dollars.\n\n- **From an existing 302:** motor mounts, bellhousing, and transmission carry over unchanged. This is the low-drama version of the swap.\n- **From the [170/200 I6](/db/bronco/bronco-eng-170-i6/):** you need V8 motor mounts and frame stands, a V8 bellhousing for the T-18 or three-speed, a V8 radiator and shroud, V8 exhaust, and often a different driveshaft length. Add $600-1,000 and a weekend.\n- **Cooling.** The early Bronco's cooling is marginal at stock power and more power makes more heat. Plan the [radiator and fan upgrade](/db/bronco/bronco-cooling-radiator-upgrade/) into the same project rather than chasing overheating in July.\n- **Exhaust.** Headers demand full exhaust fabrication behind them. A muffler shop will bend 2.5\" duals for $350-500.\n\n- **Buy the core running if possible.** A $900 engine you heard idle is worth more than a $500 unknown. Compression-test before purchase or immediately after.\n- **Check the roller lifter retainers** on 1985+ blocks. Cores that sat outdoors rust the spider and dog bones.\n- **Do not reuse the old fuel line for EFI.** Low-pressure rubber hose will fail under 39 psi, and the failure mode is fuel spraying onto a hot exhaust. Use proper submersible or PTFE-lined EFI hose with fuel injection clamps throughout.\n- **Verify hood and firewall clearance** with the intake and air cleaner mocked up before final wiring. Tall aftermarket intakes fit a Mustang, not always a Bronco.\n\n| Item | Carbureted | EFI retained |\n|---|---|---|\n| HO long block core | $600-1,200 | $800-1,500 (complete takeout) |\n| Intake + carb | $660 | \u2014 |\n| Ignition (MSD/Duraspark) | $210-320 | included with donor |\n| Front-sump pan, pickup, dipstick | $150 | $150 |\n| 50 oz-in flexplate/flywheel | $120 | $120 |\n| Headers + exhaust | $600-800 | $600-800 |\n| Fuel system | $80 (mech pump) | $450-700 (HP pump, return line, sender) |\n| Wiring | $50 | $200-400 |\n| Cooling upgrade | $400-700 | $400-700 |\n| **Total** | **$1,800-2,900** | **$2,900-4,500** |","pros":["bolts onto existing 302 mounts with the stock bellhousing pattern","cheap HO cores are widely available, and 1985+ blocks are roller-cam","240+ hp achievable with basic bolt-ons","enormous aftermarket and forum support behind every step"],"cons":["EFI retention adds 20-30 hours of harness and fuel system work","a carbureted build gives up cold-start and altitude advantages","requires full exhaust fabrication once headers are fitted","balance weight, firing order, and oil pan mistakes each cost a weekend to unwind"]},{"id":"bronco-eng-302-v8","title":"Factory 302 V8 \u2014 Specs, Carb, and Tune","vehicle":"bronco","category":"engine","subcategory":"factory","type":"reference","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["timing light","vacuum gauge","carburetor tuning tools","feeler gauge"],"skills_required":["basic carb tuning"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1969-1977"],"tags":["engine","v8","302","factory","windsor"],"parts":[{"name":"Autolite 2100 2-bbl carburetor (factory-style)","vendor":"Autolite","price_estimate_usd":190},{"name":"Edelbrock Performer 600 cfm 4-bbl (mild upgrade)","part_number":"1405","vendor":"Edelbrock","price_estimate_usd":360},{"name":"Pertronix Ignitor I breakerless conversion","part_number":"1281","vendor":"Pertronix","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Motorcraft SP432 spark plugs (set of 8)","vendor":"Motorcraft","price_estimate_usd":24},{"name":"Fel-Pro rear main seal (rubber conversion)","part_number":"BS40596","vendor":"Fel-Pro","price_estimate_usd":22}],"citations":[{"title":"302 carburetor options for the Early Bronco \u2014 Full Size Bronco forum","url":"https://www.fullsizebronco.com/forum/"},{"title":"Pertronix Ignitor breakerless conversion \u2014 tech","url":"https://www.pertronix.com/tech"}],"alternatives":["bronco-eng-351w-v8","bronco-eng-302-ho-swap"],"summary":"The 302 is the sweet spot for a stock-appearing, reliable early Bronco. It is torquey enough for trail driving on 31\u201333\" tires, parts are everywhere, and the single best dollar-for-dollar improvement is dropping a Pertronix module in place of the points. Leave the displacement alone and spend your money on ignition, cooling, and a clean carburetor.","description":"Ford's 302 Windsor (5.0L) arrived in the Bronco in 1969 and was the dominant engine through 1977. Early trucks used the 2-barrel Autolite 2100; later ones got the 2150. Gross output was rated around 170 hp under the pre-1972 SAE gross standard, which works out to roughly 130\u2013140 hp by modern net measurement. It is well matched to stock gearing with 31\u201333\" tires and starts to feel strained above 35\", especially with a tall rear gear.\n\nThe factory points-and-condenser ignition is the 302's biggest maintenance liability and its easiest win. A Pertronix Ignitor I (P/N 1281, about $90) bolts in under the distributor cap, eliminates the points entirely, and holds timing steady as the engine heats and cools. It is the highest-value $90 on the whole engine. While you are in there, set initial timing to 10\u201312\u00b0 BTDC with the vacuum advance disconnected, then reconnect it; worn advance springs and a leaky vacuum can both soften throttle response. The full procedure \u2014 timing, dwell, vacuum advance, carburetor adjustment \u2014 lives in the [ignition and carburetor tune-up guide](/db/bronco/bronco-maint-tune-up/).\n\nThe Autolite 2100 flows enough for a stock 302 and is worth keeping if it tunes cleanly; a $30 rebuild kit fixes most leaks and hesitation. If you want more, an Edelbrock Performer intake and a 600 cfm 4-barrel adds top-end and crisper response, though a stock-displacement trail 302 rarely needs it. Before you blame the carburetor for a stumble, confirm it is not actually an ignition or vacuum problem \u2014 a vacuum gauge tells you which, and the [vacuum gauge diagnosis guide](/db/bronco/bronco-diag-vacuum-gauge/) shows how to read it.\n\nThe 302 in an early Bronco runs hot in slow going \u2014 the engine bay is tight and the factory cooling system is marginal. Keep the 7-blade steel clutch fan and shroud intact; lightweight aluminum fans do not move enough air at crawl speed. If the truck creeps up the gauge on hot-day trails, treat it as a system problem and address the radiator and fan together per the [cooling system upgrade guide](/db/bronco/bronco-cooling-radiator-upgrade/) rather than chasing it with a thermostat alone.\n\nThe 302 uses a rope rear main seal that weeps with age. When you have a reason to be in there \u2014 clutch, flexplate, oil pan \u2014 replace it with a Fel-Pro rubber conversion seal (BS40596). Front oil pan gasket leaks at the timing-cover corners are also common; a bead of RTV at the four corner junctions during a pan gasket job stops most of them. None of this is failure; it is the character of a 50-year-old small block.\n\nIf you need more low-end grunt for a heavy big-tire build, the factory [351 Windsor](/db/bronco/bronco-eng-351w-v8/) shares the 302's mounts and accessories and brings noticeably more torque. If your priority is modern cold-start, altitude tolerance, and fuel economy rather than more cubes, a fuel-injected 5.0L HO is the route \u2014 wiring complexity in exchange for turn-key reliability, covered in the [302 HO / EFI swap guide](/db/bronco/bronco-eng-302-ho-swap/).","pros":["excellent parts availability and a deep aftermarket","torquey and trail-capable on 31\u201333\" tires","drops into any early Bronco with off-the-shelf mounts","responds well to a basic ignition and tune"],"cons":["factory points ignition is maintenance-intensive until converted","gross-rated horsepower flatters the real output","rope rear main seal weeps with age","marginal cooling in the early Bronco bay needs attention for hot-weather wheeling"]},{"id":"bronco-eng-351w-v8","title":"Factory 351 Windsor \u2014 Specs and Swap Value","vehicle":"bronco","category":"engine","subcategory":"factory","type":"reference","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["timing light","vacuum gauge","carburetor tuning tools"],"skills_required":["basic carb tuning"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1977"],"tags":["engine","v8","351w","factory","windsor"],"parts":[{"name":"Edelbrock Performer 600 cfm 4-bbl carburetor","part_number":"1405","vendor":"Edelbrock","price_estimate_usd":360},{"name":"Edelbrock Performer 351W intake manifold","part_number":"2181","vendor":"Edelbrock","price_estimate_usd":230},{"name":"Pertronix Ignitor II for 351W","part_number":"91281","vendor":"Pertronix","price_estimate_usd":125},{"name":"Shorty headers, 351W (ceramic-coated pair)","vendor":"Hedman / Patriot","price_estimate_usd":320}],"citations":[{"title":"351 Windsor in the Early Bronco \u2014 Full Size Bronco forum","url":"https://www.fullsizebronco.com/forum/"},{"title":"Ford 335/Windsor engine family overview","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Windsor_engine"}],"alternatives":["bronco-eng-302-v8","bronco-eng-302-ho-swap"],"summary":"The 351 Windsor is the factory big-cube option \u2014 only installed in 1976 and 1977 Broncos. These trucks carry a premium and the engine is worth keeping. A 4-barrel carburetor, a real ignition, and a free-flowing exhaust wake it up substantially without touching the bottom end. As a swap into a non-351 truck it is only worth the trouble if you are building a heavy trail rig or already own the engine.","description":"Ford offered the 351 Windsor (5.8L) in the Bronco for the final two model years. Net output was rated around 154 hp in the smog era \u2014 modest on paper, but the point of the 351W has never been peak horsepower. It makes meaningfully more torque than the 302, roughly 280 lb-ft versus about 220, and torque is what moves a heavy 4x4 on tall gears and big tires.\n\nThe 351W shares the 302's small-block architecture but uses a taller deck and a longer 3.50\" stroke (versus 2.87\"). It accepts the same accessories, the same motor mounts, and most of the same bolt-on aftermarket, which is why it drops into the Bronco's engine bay the same way a 302 does.\n\nA loaded early Bronco on 33\u201335\" tires asks for low-rpm grunt, not top-end power. The 351W's longer stroke delivers that \u2014 it pulls a hill at lower rpm and lugs less than a 302 in the same truck. Paired with the Dana 20's modest 2.03:1 low range (see the [Dana 20 transfer case guide](/db/bronco/bronco-dt-dana20-tc/)) and appropriate axle gearing from the [gear ratio guide](/db/bronco/bronco-axle-gear-ratio/), the extra torque is what keeps you out of constant downshifting on the trail.\n\nThe factory smog-era 2-barrel and weak ignition leave power on the table:\n\n- **Carburetor and intake.** The factory 2-bbl is the biggest restriction. An Edelbrock Performer 351W intake (P/N 2181) topped with a 600 cfm Performer carburetor (P/N 1405) is the classic mild combination \u2014 expect 25\u201335 hp and a noticeable improvement in throttle response. Budget about $550\u2013$600 for the pair.\n- **Ignition.** Convert the points to a Pertronix Ignitor II (the 351W unit is P/N 91281) for stable timing and no maintenance. The broader tune-up procedure \u2014 timing, dwell, vacuum advance, carburetor adjustment \u2014 is in the [ignition and carburetor tune-up guide](/db/bronco/bronco-maint-tune-up/), and it applies the same way to the 351W as to the 302.\n- **Exhaust.** A set of shorty headers and 2.5\" dual exhaust frees up more usable power than any single carburetor change, and it lets the bigger engine breathe out as well as in.\n\nThe 351W carries more mass and makes more heat than the 302, and the early Bronco's small engine bay and marginal factory cooling were never generous to begin with. Low-speed crawling in summer is where these trucks overheat. Keep the factory 7-blade steel clutch fan and shroud intact, and plan a cooling upgrade alongside any 351 \u2014 a higher-capacity radiator and correct fan setup, covered in the [cooling system upgrade guide](/db/bronco/bronco-cooling-radiator-upgrade/), is the difference between a truck you can wheel on a hot day and one that boils over in the rocks.\n\nIf you are putting a Ford V8 into a non-351 Bronco from scratch, the carbureted 302 is cheaper, lighter on the front axle, and plenty for most builds \u2014 its details are in the [factory 302 V8 guide](/db/bronco/bronco-eng-302-v8/). The 351W swap only earns its keep when you need the torque for a heavy, big-tire rig or you already have a good 351 core. If your priority is modern cold-start and altitude reliability rather than displacement, a fuel-injected 5.0L is a different path entirely \u2014 see the [302 HO / EFI swap guide](/db/bronco/bronco-eng-302-ho-swap/).","pros":["factory fitment in 76\u201377 (no swap required)","significantly more torque than the 302","shares the 302's mounts, accessories, and much of its aftermarket","responds strongly to carburetor, ignition, and exhaust work"],"cons":["factory-installed in only two model years; good cores are getting scarce","heavier than the 302, adding front-end weight","runs hotter \u2014 a cooling upgrade is effectively mandatory","smog-era factory tune leaves it dull until the carburetor and ignition are addressed"]},{"id":"bronco-fuel-efi-conversion","title":"Carburetor to EFI Conversion","vehicle":"bronco","category":"fuel","subcategory":"efi","type":"upgrade","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":10,"high":20},"cost_usd":{"low":1200,"high":2600},"tools_required":["socket set","wire crimpers","multimeter","fuel line tools","drill","fuel pressure gauge","laptop or handheld tuner"],"skills_required":["fuel system plumbing","basic wiring","EFI base-map setup"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["fuel","efi","fuel injection","throttle body","carburetor","self-tuning"],"parts":[{"name":"Self-tuning throttle-body EFI kit (Holley Sniper / FiTech)","vendor":"Holley / FiTech","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"EFI return-style fuel system / in-tank pump module","vendor":"Holley / Aeromotive","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"EFI-rated fuel line and fittings (-6 AN)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Dedicated grounding and relay kit","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"Throttle-body EFI on early Ford V8","url":"https://www.holley.com/products/fuel_systems/"},{"title":"EB EFI conversion threads","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-maint-tune-up"],"summary":"A self-tuning throttle-body EFI kit cures the two things people hate about a carbureted early Bronco off-road: stalling on steep climbs and off-camber, and cold-start fussiness. It's a real conversion costing $1,200\u20132,600 and a couple of solid days, and a well-tuned carb is genuinely fine for mild use \u2014 so be honest about whether you need it before you spend.","description":"A carburetor relies on gravity and a level float bowl. Point an early Bronco up a 30\u00b0 climb or hang it off-camber on a ledge and the fuel sloshes away from the jets \u2014 the engine stumbles or stalls at the worst possible moment. EFI meters fuel by pressure and injector pulse, so it doesn't care about attitude. That, plus instant cold starts and self-adjusting altitude compensation, is why EFI is the popular EB drivetrain upgrade.\n\nThe honest counterpoint: a correctly tuned Holley or Edelbrock carb with the right jets and a good accelerator pump handles mild trails fine, and it's vastly cheaper to keep running. EFI earns its money on steep, technical terrain, at high altitude where carbs go rich, and for anyone tired of seasonal carb tuning. If you mostly do dirt roads and fire trails, you probably don't need it.\n\nA self-tuning throttle-body system (Holley Sniper, FiTech) bolts onto the existing intake manifold's 4150 flange and learns the engine through a wideband O2 sensor \u2014 no laptop tuning required for a baseline. The critical companion is the fuel system: EFI needs 45\u201360 psi return-style fuel delivery, so you need a high-pressure pump (in-tank module is the reliable choice), EFI-rated line, a regulator, and usually a return line back to the tank. Skimping on the fuel system is the number-one cause of EFI conversions that run poorly.\n\nStart with the fuel system, not the throttle body. Install the high-pressure pump and EFI-rated supply and return lines, set the regulator to the kit's spec (typically 58 psi), and verify pressure with a gauge before going further. Mount the throttle body on the intake, install the wideband O2 bung in the exhaust ahead of any collector, and wire the harness: switched 12V, a clean dedicated ground to the block, and a tach signal. Bolt the handheld controller, enter the engine parameters (displacement, cam, target idle), and let the self-learn run through warm-up and a series of drives.\n\nRun a dedicated, heavy ground from the EFI to the engine block, not to a painted bracket. EFI is ground-sensitive and a marginal ground causes maddening intermittent faults.\n\nUnder-built fuel systems sink more EFI conversions than anything else. A low-pressure carb pump won't feed EFI, and a too-small line starves it under load. Build the fuel side to the kit's spec first.\n\nO2 sensor placement matters \u2014 too far downstream and it reads slow and cold, throwing off the self-tune. Put the bung 6\u201318\" after the head, before the catalytic converter (if any).\n\nGrounding, again: a poor ground produces random stalls and check-engine behavior that mimic a hundred other faults. Do the grounds right.\n\nDon't expect EFI to fix a tired engine. Worn rings, a vacuum leak, or bad timing will fight the self-tune. Sort the mechanical engine first.\n\nA self-tuning throttle-body kit is $900\u20131,300 (Holley Sniper 2, FiTech). A proper return-style fuel system with in-tank pump adds $400\u2013600, line and fittings $150\u2013250, grounding/relay kit $45. A complete conversion lands at $1,200\u20132,600 depending on how much fuel plumbing you fabricate versus buy as a kit. Holley and FiTech dominate the EB market; buy the matched fuel-system bundle rather than piecing it together, since fuel-supply mismatches cause most of the running problems people blame on the EFI."},{"id":"bronco-fuel-tank-sending-unit","title":"Fuel Tank and Sending Unit \u2014 Replacement and EFI-Ready Options","vehicle":"bronco","category":"fuel","subcategory":"tank","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":700},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","fuel line wrenches","drain pan","fire extinguisher","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["draining and handling fuel safely","fuel line fittings","sending unit calibration"],"build_tiers":["stock_resto","mild_trail","daily_driver"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["fuel","fuel tank","sending unit","EFI","fuel pump","safety"],"parts":[{"name":"Replacement fuel tank (17.5 gal, early Bronco)","vendor":"Tom's Offroad / LMC","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"EFI-ready in-tank pump + sending unit module","vendor":"Tanks Inc. / Aeromotive","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Fuel tank skid plate","vendor":"Various EB fab","price_estimate_usd":120}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco fuel tank options","url":"https://www.tomsoffroad.com/"},{"title":"EFI in-tank pump conversion","url":"https://www.tanksinc.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-fuel-efi-conversion","bronco-armor-skid-plates"],"summary":"Replace the tank when it's rusty, leaks, or you're converting to EFI \u2014 and if you're going EFI, buy the in-tank pump tank now rather than hanging an external pump that whines and cavitates. A bad sending unit usually means a stuck or fouled float; you can sometimes fix the gauge without dropping the tank, but a rusty tank is a safety issue, not a maintenance item.","description":"The early Bronco's fuel tank sits under the cargo floor (later models) or in the original under-seat location on early trucks, and after 50 years many are rusty inside. Rust flakes clog the pickup and EFI injectors, and a pinholed tank is a fire hazard you don't drive on. The sending unit \u2014 the float-and-resistor assembly that drives your fuel gauge \u2014 fails separately and is the usual reason the gauge reads wrong.\n\nIf you're staying carbureted and the tank is sound, a new sending unit fixes a dead gauge. If the tank is rusty, or you're converting to EFI, replace the tank \u2014 and for EFI, get a tank built for an in-tank pump so the pump runs cool, quiet, and primed.\n\nDecide your fuel system first. A carbureted truck uses a mechanical or low-pressure electric pump and a basic sending unit. An EFI conversion needs 40\u201360 psi from a high-pressure pump, and the right way to feed it is an in-tank pump module that sits submerged in fuel \u2014 it stays cool, doesn't cavitate on hills or off-camber, and is quieter than an external pump. Tanks built for the early Bronco with an in-tank pump and matched sending unit make this a bolt-in.\n\nFor the sending unit, match the ohm range to your gauge. The factory early Bronco gauge expects a specific resistance sweep; an EFI module's sending unit must match it or the gauge reads wrong even with a full tank.\n\nWork outside or in a well-ventilated space with a fire extinguisher in reach, and disconnect the battery. Drain the tank as low as possible \u2014 run it down before the job. Support the tank, disconnect the filler, vent, fuel lines, and sending-unit wiring, then lower it. Transfer or install the sending unit and pump module, using a new lock ring gasket. Reinstall, reconnect lines and wiring, and pressurize the system to check for leaks before the first start.\n\nCalibrate or verify the gauge: with a known fuel level, confirm the gauge tracks. Mismatched ohm ranges are the usual reason a fresh sending unit still reads wrong.\n\nFuel handling is a safety issue. Vapor is the hazard, not the liquid \u2014 no sparks, no smoking, ventilate, and keep an extinguisher within reach. Don't drain a tank near a water heater or any pilot light.\n\nAn external high-pressure pump feeding EFI is the common shortcut and the common headache: it whines, runs hot, and can lose prime on a steep climb or off-camber, stalling the engine at the worst time. An in-tank pump avoids all of it.\n\nOhm-range mismatch between sending unit and gauge is the quiet failure \u2014 the tank's fine, the gauge lies. Confirm the range before you button it up.\n\nA replacement carbureted tank with sending unit runs $200\u2013320. An EFI-ready tank with in-tank pump module runs $350\u2013550. A fuel tank skid is $100\u2013150 and matters once a lift drops the tank below the frame. Total for an EFI-ready setup with skid is $450\u2013700.\n\nYou probably don't need to drop the tank for a gauge problem if the tank is sound \u2014 test the sending unit and wiring first. But if there's rust inside, replace it; a clean fuel supply protects everything downstream, and on EFI a dirty tank kills injectors."},{"id":"bronco-int-gauge-monitoring","title":"Aftermarket Gauges \u2014 Monitoring an Engine the Factory Cluster Ignores","vehicle":"bronco","category":"interior","subcategory":"instrumentation","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":350},"tools_required":["wire crimpers","wire strippers","drill and hole saw","test light or multimeter","wrench set (for sender fittings)","thread sealant"],"skills_required":["12V wiring","sender installation","panel mounting"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","weekend_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["gauges","interior","monitoring","temperature","oil pressure","voltmeter"],"parts":[{"name":"Mechanical oil pressure gauge with sender","vendor":"Autometer / VDO","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Coolant temperature gauge with sender","vendor":"Autometer / VDO","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"Voltmeter gauge","vendor":"Autometer / VDO","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Triple gauge pod or A-pillar mount","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":40}],"citations":[{"title":"Aftermarket Gauge Selection \u2014 engine monitoring basics","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-elec-wiring-harness","bronco-cooling-electric-fan"],"summary":"The factory early Bronco cluster tells you almost nothing useful about engine health \u2014 a temperature gauge that reads vague and an idiot light for oil. If you wheel this truck or have swapped in a V8, add real gauges: oil pressure, coolant temperature, and voltage. They turn a guessing game into early warning, and on the trail that warning is the difference between pulling over and grenading an engine.","description":"The factory instrumentation was built to a 1960s economy-truck standard. The temperature gauge often reads in vague zones rather than real numbers, oil pressure is frequently a warning light that only triggers after damage is underway, and there is no clear picture of charging system health. For a stock-engine truck driven gently, that was acceptable. For a built or swapped Bronco worked hard off-road, it is not enough.\n\n**Oil pressure (mechanical preferred).** Oil pressure is the earliest warning of serious engine trouble \u2014 a failing pump, a bearing problem, oil starvation on a steep climb. A real gauge shows the number; a warning light shows nothing until pressure has already collapsed. Mechanical gauges run a small line of actual oil to the gauge and are accurate and reliable. Electric gauges are easier to route but depend on a sender and wiring.\n\n**Coolant temperature.** An early Bronco, especially with a V8 swap, can build heat on slow technical trails where airflow is low. A precise temperature gauge lets you see heat climbing and back off or stop before it boils over. The factory gauge often does not show the problem until it is already serious.\n\n**Voltmeter.** A voltmeter is the clearest window into charging system health. It shows whether the alternator is keeping up under load \u2014 lights, fan, winch, accessories. A dropping voltage reading warns of a failing alternator or a bad connection before the battery dies on the trail.\n\nMost builds use a triple gauge pod under the dash, an A-pillar mount, or a panel in the factory dash. Choose a location you can read at a glance without taking your eyes far off the trail.\n\nWiring is straightforward 12V work. Gauges need switched power (on with the key), a clean ground, and gauge lighting tied into the dash lights so they dim at night. Senders thread into the engine: the oil pressure sender at an oil galley port, the temperature sender into a coolant passage. Use thread sealant rated for the application and confirm no leaks after the first heat cycle.\n\nA mechanical oil pressure gauge runs a small line of live oil into the cabin. Route it carefully, use the supplied fittings, and protect the line from heat and chafing \u2014 a failed line means oil where you do not want it. Many builders prefer electric oil pressure gauges in the cabin for exactly this reason, accepting the small accuracy and reliability tradeoff.\n\nMatch the sender to the gauge brand and resistance range. A VDO sender and an Autometer gauge may not read correctly together. Buy the gauge and sender as a matched set.\n\nIf your truck is bone-stock and street-only, the factory gauges plus a careful eye may be enough. The case for aftermarket gauges gets stronger the moment you swap engines, add load, or take the truck somewhere a breakdown is a long walk home.\n\n- Oil pressure gauge with sender: $60\n- Coolant temperature gauge with sender: $55\n- Voltmeter: $45\n- Gauge pod or mount: $40\n\nBudget $80 for a single critical gauge to start (oil pressure), up to $350 for a matched triple set with a pod. If you add one gauge, make it oil pressure \u2014 it is the one that warns you in time to save the engine."},{"id":"bronco-interior-roll-cage","title":"Roll Cage and Roll Bar \u2014 What Actually Protects You","vehicle":"bronco","category":"interior","subcategory":"roll_cage","type":"kit","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":40},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":3000},"tools_required":["tube bender","welder","angle grinder","drill","tape measure","level","floor jack"],"skills_required":["tube fabrication","MIG or TIG welding","measuring and fitment","frame tie-in"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["interior","roll cage","roll bar","safety","rollover protection","safety_critical"],"safety_critical":true,"parts":[{"name":"Bolt-in family-style roll bar (early Bronco)","vendor":"Tom's Offroad / Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"DOM tube (1.75\" x .120\") for full cage","vendor":"Various steel supply","price_estimate_usd":300},{"name":"Weld-in frame tie-in plates","vendor":"Various EB fab","price_estimate_usd":80}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco roll cage design","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"},{"title":"Tube and material standards for cages","url":"https://www.tomsoffroad.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-interior-seats-harness"],"summary":"The factory \"roll bar\" in an early Bronco is barely better than nothing \u2014 a real rollover happens, and a single hoop bolted to the floor pan folds. If you wheel hard or run off-camber rock, a frame-tied roll cage is the difference between rolling and walking away versus rolling and not. This is a safety system, not a styling accessory, and it has to be built to a standard.","description":"Rollovers on the trail are not rare, and the early Bronco's short wheelbase and high center of gravity make it more prone than most. The factory roll bar and the common bolt-in \"family\" bars are designed for a tip-over at low speed, not a real roll on rock. They bolt to the sheet-metal floor and tear out under the loads a rollover actually generates.\n\nThe honest answer: a roll cage is worth building correctly or not bothering with the pretense. A cage that's tied to the frame, triangulated, and built from the right tube protects you. A cosmetic hoop gives false confidence. If your wheeling is mild fire-road stuff, the factory bar plus belts is your reality; if you're climbing ledges and running off-camber, build the cage.\n\nMaterial and design are non-negotiable for a cage that works. DOM (drawn-over-mandrel) tube, typically 1.75\" x .120\" wall for an early Bronco's weight, is the standard \u2014 never use ERW pipe, EMT conduit, or exhaust tubing, which crush instead of bending and absorbing energy. The cage must tie into the frame, not only the floor: cap the floor penetrations with plates that spread load into the frame rail.\n\nA real cage has a main hoop behind the seats, an A-pillar or windshield bar, connecting bars along the roofline, rear down-bars to the frame, and triangulation so the structure doesn't parallelogram in a roll. Harness bars for proper belts come with it. This is fabrication work \u2014 bending, fitting, and welding tube accurately is a skill, and a poorly welded cage can fail at the weld.\n\nThis is a fabrication job, not a bolt-on. Bolt-in family bars install in a few hours with hand tools and frame-tie-in plates. A full welded cage is days of work: bend and fit each tube, tack everything, verify fitment and clearances (head clearance to the tube is critical \u2014 you do not want your head against bare tube in a roll), then weld out. Frame tie-ins get welded or through-bolted with backing plates into the frame rail.\n\nIf you don't have tube-bending and welding skills, this is a job for a fab shop. A cage is the one piece of the truck where \"close enough\" can cost a life.\n\nWrong tube is the deadly mistake. ERW, conduit, and exhaust tube look the same to the eye and crush in a roll. Buy DOM or proper cage tube and keep the certs.\n\nBolting to the floor pan alone is the second deadly mistake. The floor is thin sheet metal; the cage has to reach the frame. Plate every penetration.\n\nPadding matters. Any tube within head-strike range needs SFI roll-bar padding \u2014 bare tube against a helmetless head in a roll is its own injury. And a cage is only half the system: it does nothing without a proper harness holding you inside it.\n\nA bolt-in family roll bar runs $350\u2013600 and is an improvement over stock but is not rollover protection for hard wheeling. A full welded, frame-tied cage runs $400\u2013800 in materials if you build it yourself, or $1,500\u20133,000 built and installed by a fab shop. The tube alone for a full EB cage is around $300.\n\nYou probably don't need a chromoly competition cage for a trail truck \u2014 DOM tube, built to a real design and tied to the frame, protects you. But don't talk yourself into a cosmetic hoop as \"safety.\" If the goal is to protect the people in the truck, build it right or have it built right."},{"id":"bronco-int-seats-harness","title":"Seat Upgrade and Harness Mounting","vehicle":"bronco","category":"interior","subcategory":"seats","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":1800},"tools_required":["socket set","drill","welder (for harness bar/cage tie-in)","tape measure","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["measuring and fitment","drilling","welding (harness bar only)"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["interior","seats","harness","safety","seat brackets"],"parts":[{"name":"Suspension or fixed-back seats (pair)","vendor":"PRP / Corbeau","price_estimate_usd":700},{"name":"EB-specific seat brackets/sliders (pair)","vendor":"Wild Horses / Toms Bronco","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"4-point harnesses (pair)","vendor":"Crow / PRP","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"Harness bar or cage tie-in tab kit","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":90}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco seat fitment","url":"https://www.tomsbroncoparts.com/"},{"title":"Harness mounting geometry","url":"https://www.prpseats.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-interior-roll-cage","bronco-int-gauge-monitoring"],"summary":"Replacing the original early Bronco bench or low-back buckets with proper seats is a genuine comfort and control upgrade \u2014 you stop sliding around on the trail and your back stops hating you on the highway. But if you add a multi-point harness, you must mount it correctly: a harness bolted at the wrong angle is more dangerous than the factory lap-and-shoulder belt it replaces.","description":"The original early Bronco seats are flat, unsupportive, and let you slide off-camber so you're fighting the wheel to stay planted. Modern suspension seats or fixed-back buckets hold you in place, add lumbar support for the highway, and make technical trail driving far less tiring. This is one of the higher-impact comfort upgrades for an EB that gets used.\n\nThe honest part: seats themselves are a straightforward bolt-in with the right brackets. Harnesses are where people get hurt by good intentions. A 4- or 5-point harness needs correct geometry \u2014 shoulder belts pulling at the right downward angle to an anchor at shoulder height, mounted to structure that can take crash loads. Done wrong, a harness can cause spinal compression in a rollover. A harness only belongs in a truck with a proper [roll cage](/db/bronco/bronco-interior-roll-cage/) or a structural harness bar to anchor it \u2014 if you're not adding one, keep the 3-point belts and upgrade the seat alone.\n\nEB-specific seat brackets (Wild Horses, Toms Bronco) bolt the new seats to the original floor mounting points without fabrication. Choose seats sized to the EB's narrow tub \u2014 full-size truck seats often won't fit between the wheel wells. For harnesses, you need a harness bar or cage with anchor points at the right height; a 4-point harness anchored to the floor at the wrong angle is the configuration to avoid.\n\nBolt the seat brackets to the original floor mounts, verifying clearance to the shifter, door, and wheel well at full travel of the slider. Mount the seats and torque the bracket bolts. For harnesses, the shoulder belts must run from your shoulders rearward and slightly down (no more than ~20\u00b0 below horizontal) to an anchor at or slightly below shoulder height \u2014 that's why you need a harness bar, not a floor anchor behind the seat. The lap belt anchors low at the hips, pulling at roughly 45\u00b0 back. Tie harness-bar tabs into the [roll cage](/db/bronco/bronco-interior-roll-cage/) or weld a proper bar to structure, not to sheet metal. While the interior is apart for seat work is also the natural time to address [gauges and monitoring](/db/bronco/bronco-int-gauge-monitoring/), since the dash and console are already accessible.\n\nVerify the slider's full range doesn't let the seat foul the shifter or trap a foot, and that you can still reach the pedals and exit the door.\n\nThe dangerous mistake is mounting shoulder-harness anchors below the shoulders \u2014 pulling up and back from a low floor anchor compresses the spine in a frontal impact. The anchor must be at shoulder height. If you can't mount it there, keep the factory 3-point belt.\n\nDon't anchor harnesses to sheet metal or thin brackets. Crash loads will rip them out. Anchor to the cage, a welded harness bar, or reinforced structure.\n\nCheck seat width before buying \u2014 the EB tub is narrow and many popular seats won't clear the wheel wells or will block the door.\n\nKeep the factory 3-point belts if the truck is a daily driver without a cage. A 3-point belt is engineered to work without a harness bar; a 4-point isn't a casual upgrade.\n\nEB seat brackets run $180\u2013260 a pair. Seats span a wide range: budget fixed-back buckets $300\u2013500/pair, mid-tier suspension seats (PRP, Corbeau) $600\u20131,000, premium $1,200+. Harnesses are $80\u2013130 each plus a harness bar or tabs ($90\u2013250). A practical seat-and-bracket upgrade lands around $400\u2013700; add harnesses and a bar and it's $900\u20131,800. Toms Bronco and Wild Horses sell EB-fit brackets \u2014 buy brackets cut for the Bronco floor rather than universal rails to avoid fitment fights."},{"id":"bronco-maint-closed-knuckle-service","title":"Closed-Knuckle Dana 44 Front Service \u2014 Kingpins and Knuckle Seals","vehicle":"bronco","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"axle service","type":"procedure","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":400},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","large socket set","spring scale (for kingpin preload)","seal driver","wheel bearing grease","pry bar","brass drift"],"skills_required":["axle disassembly","kingpin preload","wheel bearing service"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","weekend_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["dana44","closed knuckle","kingpin","front axle","maintenance","service"],"parts":[{"name":"Kingpin rebuild kit (bearings, springs, caps)","vendor":"Spicer / Various","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Knuckle seal kit (felt and metal wipers)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":40},{"name":"Inner axle seals and gaskets","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"Wheel bearing and seal set","vendor":"Timken","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Knuckle grease (00 or 0 semi-fluid)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":20}],"citations":[{"title":"Closed Knuckle Dana 44 Rebuild \u2014 classicbroncos.com","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-axle-dana44-front","bronco-maint-fluids-schedule"],"summary":"The early Bronco's closed-knuckle Dana 44 front is durable but needs periodic service most owners skip. Worn kingpin bearings cause wandering steering and uneven tire wear; failed knuckle seals let grease leak out and water in. If your front end wanders, clunks over bumps, or weeps grease at the knuckles, this is the job. Done at home in a day, it restores steering precision and protects the axle internals.","description":"The closed-knuckle design wraps the steering joint in a grease-filled spherical housing instead of using exposed U-joints and ball joints. Inside, kingpin bearings (top and bottom) let the knuckle pivot for steering, and felt-and-metal seals keep the grease in and contamination out. Both wear over decades and miles.\n\nWandering or vague steering that an alignment does not fix often traces to worn kingpin bearings \u2014 the knuckle has play it should not have. A clunk over bumps can be the same. Grease seeping from the knuckle seam means the wiper seals have failed, and once they do, water and grit get into the bearings and accelerate wear. Uneven inner-edge tire wear can also point to kingpin slop changing the steering geometry.\n\nThis is a methodical disassembly, not a difficult one, but it has a measured step that matters:\n\n1. **Remove the hub, brakes, and axle shaft** to access the knuckle. Support the axle and work one side at a time so you have a reference for reassembly.\n\n2. **Unbolt and remove the knuckle** from the kingpin assembly. Clean out the old grease and inspect the spherical ball surface for pitting or wear \u2014 a deeply pitted ball will not seal no matter how good the new wipers are.\n\n3. **Replace kingpin bearings, springs, and caps.** The lower kingpin typically uses a bearing and the upper a spring-loaded bushing or bearing depending on configuration. Press in new races and seat new bearings.\n\n4. **Set kingpin preload.** This is the step that defines the job. With the knuckle reassembled, use a spring scale to measure the effort needed to swing the knuckle through its arc. Too loose and the steering wanders; too tight and it binds. Shim or adjust to the spec rolling effort. This is what restores tight steering.\n\n5. **Install new knuckle seals**, fresh wheel bearings, and inner axle seals. Pack the knuckle with the correct semi-fluid grease \u2014 not regular chassis grease, which is too stiff for the spherical joint.\n\nKingpin preload is the make-or-break measurement. Skip it and you have done a lot of work for a front end that still wanders. An inexpensive spring scale and a few minutes of adjustment is what separates a proper rebuild from a grease change.\n\nUse the right grease. The closed knuckle wants a 00 or 0 grade semi-fluid grease so it flows around the bearings and ball. Packing it with stiff wheel-bearing grease leaves the internals dry where it matters.\n\nIf your steering is tight and the knuckles are dry on the outside, you may only need a grease top-up and seal inspection, not a full rebuild. Pull a fill plug and check the grease condition before tearing into it.\n\n- Kingpin rebuild kit: $90\n- Knuckle and inner seal kits: $70\n- Wheel bearings and seals: $60\n- Semi-fluid knuckle grease: $20\n\nBudget $120 for seals and grease if the bearings are still good, up to $400 for a full both-sides rebuild with new bearings and wheel bearings. The labor is yours; the parts are inexpensive for what they restore."},{"id":"bronco-maint-fluids-schedule","title":"Fluids and Service Schedule for the Early Bronco","vehicle":"bronco","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"scheduled service","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":250},"tools_required":["drain pan","socket set","fluid pump (for gear oil)","funnel","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["draining and filling fluids","reading a dipstick","identifying fill/drain plugs"],"build_tiers":["stock_resto","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["maintenance","fluids","service schedule","gear oil","transfer case","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Engine oil + filter (10W-30 or 10W-40)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"80W-90 GL-5 gear oil (axles + transfer case)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"ATF (C4/C6 auto) or manual trans oil","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"Wheel bearing grease + seals","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":30}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco fluid specs and capacities","url":"https://www.fordification.com/tech/"},{"title":"Service intervals for an off-road-used Bronco","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-maint-tune-up","bronco-axle-dana44-front"],"summary":"A Bronco that wheels needs fluids changed on mileage *and* on exposure. The factory said engine oil every 3,000 miles and gear oil yearly, and that's still a fair baseline \u2014 but the moment a water crossing or a deep mud day gets water past a seal, the schedule resets. The single most-skipped service on these trucks is the front axle and transfer case gear oil, and milky, water-contaminated gear oil quietly destroys a Dana 44 over a season. Pull the fill plugs and look.","description":"These are old trucks with old seals, used hard. The honest framing isn't \"follow the original manual\" \u2014 it's \"change fluids on whichever comes first, the interval or the abuse.\" A desert-only Phoenix Bronco can stretch intervals; one that fords creeks needs the differentials and transfer case checked after every wet trip.\n\nConventional oils are correct for these engines and drivetrains \u2014 there's nothing here that requires synthetic, though synthetic gear oil holds up better to heat in a hard-worked axle. Stock the basics: engine oil and a filter, 80W-90 GL-5 gear oil for the axles and transfer case, the right transmission fluid (ATF for a C4/C6 automatic, gear oil for the manual), wheel bearing grease and seals, and brake fluid. A hand pump makes filling the axles and case far less miserable.\n\nEngine oil and filter: every 3,000 miles or annually with 10W-30 or 10W-40 conventional. These engines were built loose by modern standards and run a slightly heavier oil happily, especially in Arizona heat. Capacity is roughly 5 quarts.\n\nAxle gear oil (front and rear Dana 44): change annually or after any water crossing that may have submerged the axle. 80W-90 GL-5 is the spec. When you pull the fill plug, look at what comes out \u2014 milky or tan oil means water got in and you should change it immediately and inspect the seals and vent. A clogged axle breather vent is a common culprit; extend the breathers up high if you wheel water.\n\nTransfer case (Dana 20): same story \u2014 annual or post-water service with gear oil. The Dana 20 shares lubricant character with the axles. Check it whenever you check the diffs.\n\nTransmission: automatic (C4/C6) fluid and filter every 30,000 miles, sooner if you wheel hard and run it hot; manual transmission gear oil annually. A trans temp gauge is cheap insurance on an automatic pulling big tires.\n\nWheel bearings: clean, inspect, and repack the front bearings yearly or after deep water \u2014 water washes grease out of the spindle and bearings rust fast. Replace the grease seals while you're in there.\n\nBrake fluid: flush every two years. Old fluid absorbs water, which lowers the boiling point and rusts the system from the inside. This matters more on a truck that sits.\n\nCooling system: drain, flush, and refill the coolant every two to three years with the correct mix; inspect hoses and the radiator while it's open.\n\nThe killer is water in the gear oil going unnoticed. After any wet trip, pull the diff and transfer case fill plugs and look. Catching milky oil early is a fluid change; ignoring it is a bearing and gear replacement. Extend and route the axle and transfer case breathers high if you cross water regularly.\n\nDon't overfill the axles \u2014 fill to the bottom of the fill hole and no more. Overfilled diffs push lube past the seals.\n\nWatch the engine for oil leaks rather than chasing them all at once. A rear main or valve cover seep on a 50-year-old engine is normal; address them when you're in the area for another reason, and keep the oil topped up in the meantime.\n\nA full fluid service across engine, both axles, transfer case, and transmission runs $80\u2013150 in materials if you do it yourself, more if you add synthetic gear oil or a bearing repack with new seals (push toward $200\u2013250 with parts). Any parts store carries everything; the specs are common. You probably don't need premium synthetics everywhere \u2014 conventional oils meet the original spec \u2014 but synthetic gear oil in a hard-worked, heat-soaked axle is one place the upgrade earns its keep."},{"id":"bronco-maint-tune-up","title":"Ignition and Carburetor Tune-Up","vehicle":"bronco","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"ignition","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":220},"tools_required":["timing light","vacuum gauge","carburetor sync tool","distributor wrench","spark plug socket","feeler gauges"],"skills_required":["basic carb tuning","timing procedure"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["tune-up","ignition","carburetor","points","timing","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Pertronix Ignitor I (points replacement)","part_number":"1281","vendor":"Pertronix","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Motorcraft SP432 plugs (set of 8)","vendor":"Motorcraft","price_estimate_usd":24},{"name":"Distributor cap and rotor set","vendor":"Standard Motor Products","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Autolite 2100 rebuild kit","vendor":"Autolite","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"Gates ignition wires (set of 8)","vendor":"Gates","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco tune-up specs","url":"https://www.fullsizebronco.com/forum/"},{"title":"Pertronix installation guide","url":"https://www.pertronix.com/tech"}],"alternatives":["bronco-diag-vacuum-gauge","bronco-eng-302-v8","bronco-fuel-efi-conversion"],"summary":"The single highest-value maintenance task on a carbureted early Bronco is replacing the points with a Pertronix Ignitor \u2014 $90, about 45 minutes, and you've eliminated the most failure-prone component in the entire ignition system. A complete tune-up adds plugs, cap, rotor, wires, timing, and a carb check.","description":"A tune-up on a carbureted Bronco is two systems working together: the ignition has to deliver a hot, correctly timed spark, and the carburetor has to deliver the right mixture. Fix one and ignore the other and the truck still runs poorly. Work through them in order \u2014 ignition first, because a weak or mistimed spark masks every carb symptom.\n\nSet initial timing with a timing light, engine at operating temperature, and the vacuum advance line disconnected and plugged:\n\n- 302 V8: 10\u201312\u00b0 BTDC initial. Total advance (mechanical + vacuum) should reach 34\u201338\u00b0 at 3,500 RPM.\n- 351W V8: 12\u00b0 BTDC initial. Same total-advance target.\n- 200/170 I6: 6\u00b0 BTDC initial.\n\nIf the engine pings under load, back initial timing down a degree or two; if it's sluggish off idle, the mechanical advance may be sticking. The engine-specific cards cover combustion symptoms in more depth \u2014 see the [302 V8 reference](/db/bronco/bronco-eng-302-v8/).\n\n1. **Plugs:** Gap to 0.035\" for points ignition, 0.044\" for Pertronix or MSD. Use Motorcraft SP432 (302/351W) or an equivalent copper-core plug. Install with anti-seize on the threads.\n2. **Points (if still fitted):** Set gap to 0.017\" with a feeler gauge, or dwell to 26\u201330\u00b0 with a dwell meter. If you're in here doing this, seriously consider the Pertronix swap instead \u2014 it deletes the part that drifts out of adjustment.\n3. **Cap and rotor:** Replace every 30,000 miles, or sooner if carbon tracking is visible on the cap interior.\n4. **Plug wires:** Inspect for cracks, especially at the boots. Resistance-test each wire \u2014 it should read under 10,000 ohms per foot. Gates or MSD wires are reliable aftermarket options.\n5. **Timing:** Set per the specs above with the vacuum advance disconnected and plugged.\n\nThe factory Autolite 2100 is a durable 2-barrel, and most running problems are float level or idle mixture, not a worn-out carb:\n\n- Float level: 7/16\" from the gasket surface to the top of the float on the 2100. A high float floods; a low float leans out and stumbles.\n- Check the fuel bowl for leaks at the float valve seat \u2014 a weeping seat causes hard hot-starts and a fuel smell.\n- Idle mixture screws: start at 1.5 turns out from lightly seated, then adjust each for the highest steady idle vacuum on a gauge.\n\nIf you're tired of seasonal and altitude re-jetting, an EFI conversion is the longer-term answer \u2014 see [Carb-to-EFI Conversion](/db/bronco/bronco-fuel-efi-conversion/). For a stock truck that mostly stays near home elevation, a clean 2100 and a correct tune is hard to beat for the money.\n\nA working vacuum advance retards timing at closed throttle and advances it under cruise load. Disconnect the vacuum line at idle \u2014 if timing jumps 6\u201312\u00b0, the advance canister is working. If the timing mark doesn't move, the canister diaphragm has failed; replace it ($12). Reading manifold vacuum with a gauge while you tune tells you a lot about engine health beyond the advance alone, which is why a [vacuum gauge](/db/bronco/bronco-diag-vacuum-gauge/) belongs in the tune-up kit.\n\nThe two recurring mistakes: setting timing with the vacuum advance still connected (you'll end up with initial timing far off), and chasing a carb problem that's actually a weak spark. Confirm a strong, correctly timed spark before you touch the carburetor. And after a points-to-Pertronix swap, re-check timing \u2014 the trigger point shifts slightly when the points come out.\n\n- Pertronix Ignitor I: $90\n- Plugs, cap, rotor, wires: roughly $95 together\n- Autolite 2100 rebuild kit: $30\n\nA full ignition refresh with the Pertronix swap lands around $185\u2013220 in parts. The labor is DIY-friendly with a timing light and a vacuum gauge \u2014 the value is in doing the steps in order rather than throwing parts at a symptom."},{"id":"bronco-recovery-points","title":"Recovery Points \u2014 Rated Shackle Mounts Front and Rear","vehicle":"bronco","category":"recovery","subcategory":"recovery points","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":450},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","drill (if adding mounts)","thread locker"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","hardware torque"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["recovery","recovery points","shackle","tow hooks","safety","recovery_points"],"parts":[{"name":"Bolt-on rated recovery points (frame-mount, pair)","vendor":"Factor 55 / Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":160},{"name":"Soft shackles (2x, 30,000+ lb)","vendor":"Factor 55 / Bubba Rope","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Grade 8 mounting hardware","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":25}],"citations":[{"title":"Rated recovery point selection","url":"https://www.factor55.com/"},{"title":"EB recovery point mounting","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-armor-rock-rails"],"summary":"SAFETY CRITICAL \u2014 recovery loads are enormous and a failed attachment point turns a steel hook into a projectile that has killed people. Use rated, frame-mounted points and rated shackles. Never recover from a ball hitch, a bumper bracket, or a bent factory tab.","description":"> Before you wheel an early Bronco, fit a rated, frame-mounted recovery point front and rear. The factory has no proper recovery points, and a winch line or kinetic strap snatched off a bumper bracket or trailer ball can rip steel loose at lethal speed. This is cheap insurance that has to be in place before you get stuck, not after.\n\nWhen a stuck vehicle is pulled \u2014 especially with a kinetic (snatch) strap that stores and releases energy \u2014 the forces involved can exceed the vehicle's weight several times over. If the attachment point isn't rated and isn't tied to the frame, it can tear free and launch metal through a windshield. People have died this way. An early Bronco has no factory recovery points worth trusting, so adding rated ones is the first thing to do before any trail.\n\nThe honest framing: this is one of the least glamorous and most important upgrades on the truck. It's also among the cheapest and quickest. There's no reason to skip it.\n\nRated recovery points (not \"tow hooks\" of unknown rating) that bolt to the frame with Grade 8 hardware, front and rear. Pair them with rated soft shackles or screw-pin bow shackles \u2014 soft shackles are lighter, won't become projectiles if something fails, and are easier on the hardware. A rated point will state its working load limit; if it doesn't have a rating, don't trust it for recovery.\n\nMount the recovery points to the frame using the existing frame holes or the kit's specified mounting plate, with Grade 8 bolts and thread locker, torqued to the kit spec. The point must bolt through the frame rail or a plate that sandwiches it \u2014 not to a bumper bracket, not to a body mount. Position the points so a strap or winch line pulls in a straight line without binding against the bumper. Verify a shackle fits and the gate closes fully.\n\nIf you're adding a front bumper or rear bumper with integrated D-ring tabs, confirm the tabs are rated and welded to the bumper's structure, not merely tacked to a decorative face.\n\nThe fatal mistake is recovering from the wrong thing \u2014 a trailer ball (snaps and launches the ball), a thin bumper bracket, or a bent factory tab. Use only rated, frame-tied points.\n\nA screw-pin shackle must have the pin fully threaded then backed off a quarter turn so it doesn't seize under load. A cross-threaded or hand-snug pin can fail.\n\nDon't use a kinetic strap on a point rated only for static winching unless it's rated for the shock load \u2014 kinetic recovery multiplies force. Match the point and shackle ratings to the recovery method.\n\nKeep a damper (blanket or recovery bag) over the strap or winch line during a pull, so if something fails the line drops instead of flying.\n\nBolt-on rated recovery points run $80\u2013250 a pair depending on whether you buy basic shackle mounts or integrated bumper tabs. Rated soft shackles are $40\u201360 each; steel bow shackles $15\u201330. A complete front-and-rear setup with shackles lands at $120\u2013450. Factor 55, Smittybilt, and ARB sell rated points and shackles; the key is a stated load rating and a frame-tied mount. You probably don't need the most expensive billet point \u2014 any properly rated, frame-mounted point does the job. What you can't compromise on is the rating and the mounting."},{"id":"bronco-recovery-winch-install","title":"Winch Install \u2014 Sizing, Mounting, and Wiring","vehicle":"bronco","category":"recovery","subcategory":"winch","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":1400},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","wire crimpers (lug crimper)","wrenches","multimeter","drill"],"skills_required":["high-current wiring","torquing to spec","crimping battery cable lugs"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["recovery","winch","winch mount","wiring","self-recovery","winch","safety_critical"],"safety_critical":true,"parts":[{"name":"8,000-9,500 lb winch with synthetic line","vendor":"Warn / Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":500},{"name":"Winch wiring kit (2/0 cable, solenoid)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Frame-mounted winch bumper / cradle","vendor":"WFO Concepts / Tom's Offroad","price_estimate_usd":450}],"citations":[{"title":"Winch sizing by vehicle weight","url":"https://www.warn.com/"},{"title":"Early Bronco winch mounting","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-armor-front-bumper","bronco-recovery-points","bronco-electrical-alternator-upgrade"],"summary":"Size the winch to at least 1.5x your truck's loaded weight \u2014 for an early Bronco that lands at 8,000\u20139,500 lb pull, and bigger if you run heavy. Mount it only to a frame-tied bumper, wire it with proper 2/0 cable, and treat every pull as a serious load. A winch is a recovery tool and a hazard at the same time: under tension, a failed line or shackle becomes a projectile that can kill.","description":"A winch turns a stuck truck into a self-recovery instead of a long wait or a tow strap from a buddy you didn't bring. For an early Bronco \u2014 light but high and prone to getting a tire in the wrong place \u2014 a winch earns its keep on technical trails and solo trips. The two things that make a winch install right are mounting (frame-tied, never sheet metal) and wiring (heavy cable, clean grounds), and the thing that keeps you alive is technique.\n\nSynthetic line over steel cable is the choice most people land on now: it's lighter, safer when it fails (it drops instead of whipping with stored energy), and easier to handle. It does need a fairlead and care around heat and abrasion.\n\nPull rating comes first. Loaded, an early Bronco with armor and gear runs 4,500\u20136,000 lb; the rule of thumb is winch rating at least 1.5x that, so 8,000\u20139,500 lb covers most builds with margin. Undersizing means the winch labors, overheats, and pulls slowly; the line's mechanical advantage with a snatch block helps but doesn't replace adequate base rating.\n\nYou need a frame-tied mount \u2014 a winch is only as strong as what it's bolted to, and the early Bronco's stock bumper can't take it. A winch-ready bumper that ties into the frame rail is required. Wiring is 2/0 cable from the battery to the winch with a solenoid or contactor, and the charging system should be healthy because a winch under load pulls 200+ amps.\n\nMount the winch to the frame-tied bumper cradle and torque the mounting bolts to spec. Route the heavy positive and negative cables to the battery, keeping them clear of moving and hot parts, and protect every pass-through with a grommet \u2014 chafed 2/0 cable against the frame is a dead short and a fire. Mount the solenoid/contactor per the winch diagram, connect the control, and verify the winch spools in and out under control before trusting it.\n\nSpool the synthetic line on under light tension so it beds evenly \u2014 loose wraps dig into lower layers and jam under load. Confirm the fairlead is mounted and the line runs through it cleanly.\n\nRecovery is where people get hurt. Under tension a winch line stores energy; a failed line, hook, or shackle becomes a projectile. Use a rated line damper (a heavy blanket or bag) draped over the line, keep bystanders out of the line's path on both sides, and never step over a loaded line. Synthetic line is safer than steel here but is not a license to be casual.\n\nMounting to anything but the frame is the fatal shortcut. A winch bolted to sheet metal or a horn-mounted bumper tears free under load.\n\nCharging matters. A winch dragging a weak 50-amp charging system flat will leave you with a dead battery after one long pull. Plan the alternator and a healthy battery alongside the winch.\n\nA quality 9,000\u20139,500 lb winch with synthetic line runs $400\u2013700; premium units run $900\u20131,400. A wiring kit is $80\u2013120. The frame-tied bumper to mount it is a separate $300\u2013650. Total for a complete, done-right install is $800\u20131,400 plus the bumper.\n\nYou probably don't need a 12,000 lb winch on a light early Bronco \u2014 a 9,000\u20139,500 lb unit with a snatch block covers nearly any self-recovery and weighs less on the front end. Spend the saved money on the bumper, the wiring, and a proper recovery kit (damper, soft shackles, snatch block), because the winch without those is half a system."},{"id":"bronco-steering-box-upgrade","title":"Power Steering Conversion and Box Upgrade","vehicle":"bronco","category":"steering","subcategory":"power steering","type":"upgrade","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":14},"cost_usd":{"low":500,"high":1400},"tools_required":["socket set","pitman arm puller","tie rod separator","flare nut wrenches","torque wrench","pulley puller","floor jack"],"skills_required":["steering linkage service","hydraulic line fitting","pulley alignment"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["steering","power steering","steering box","safety","manual steering"],"parts":[{"name":"EB power steering conversion kit (box, pump, brackets)","vendor":"Wild Horses / PSC","price_estimate_usd":850},{"name":"Saginaw or Borgeson steering box","vendor":"Borgeson / Saginaw","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Steering shaft / U-joint (vibration-isolated)","vendor":"Borgeson","price_estimate_usd":140},{"name":"High-pressure and return hoses","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":75}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco power steering conversion","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/"},{"title":"Saginaw box swap guidance","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-susp-soa"],"summary":"SAFETY CRITICAL \u2014 steering is a system where component failure means loss of control. Use a complete, matched conversion kit, torque every linkage fastener to spec, and never reuse a worn pitman arm or tie rod end.","description":"> Converting an early Bronco from manual to power steering \u2014 or upgrading a tired factory power box to a modern Saginaw or Borgeson unit \u2014 transforms how the truck drives, especially with larger tires that make the manual box brutally heavy at low speed. It's a real conversion costing $500\u20131,400, and it's the upgrade that makes a lifted EB pleasant to live with.\n\nManual steering on a stock-tire early Bronco is manageable. Put 33s or 35s on it and the manual box becomes a workout \u2014 parking and low-speed trail maneuvering take both arms. A power steering conversion (or a modern box upgrade on trucks that already have power) restores light, precise steering and reduces the kickback that bigger tires transmit through the wheel. On a lifted, larger-tired EB this is one of the biggest day-to-day quality improvements available.\n\nThe honest verdict: if your Bronco is near-stock with original-size tires and you don't mind the workout, manual steering is reliable and uncomplicated, and you probably don't need this. The moment you go up in tire size or do a lot of slow technical driving, power steering stops being a luxury.\n\nA complete EB power steering conversion kit (Wild Horses, PSC, Borgeson) includes the steering box, pump, mounting brackets, pulley, pitman arm, and hoses matched to the engine. Buying a kit rather than assembling pieces avoids bracket and pulley-alignment headaches, which are the hard part of the job. A modern Borgeson or Saginaw box gives a better ratio and feel than the original Ford power box. Add a vibration-isolated steering shaft and fresh tie rod ends if the originals are worn.\n\nRemove the manual box (or old power box), the pitman arm (use a puller \u2014 never hammer it), and the steering column shaft. Mount the new box to the frame with the kit's brackets, torquing to spec. Install the pump bracket and pump on the engine, align the pulley to the crank and accessory pulleys (misalignment throws belts and whines), and route the high-pressure and return hoses away from the headers. Connect the steering shaft with the supplied U-joints, install the pitman arm and connect the drag link with a new tie rod end, and fill and bleed the system by turning lock to lock with the front wheels off the ground until the air works out.\n\nBleed with the front end raised, turning slowly lock to lock several times and topping off \u2014 air in the system causes a foamy, groaning pump and inconsistent assist.\n\nPulley alignment is the most common conversion mistake. A pump pulley that doesn't line up with the crank pulley throws the belt and screams. Use a straightedge across the pulleys and shim the bracket as needed.\n\nRoute the high-pressure hose clear of the exhaust manifold. A hose resting on a header burns through and dumps fluid \u2014 and a sudden loss of assist mid-corner is dangerous.\n\nDon't reuse a worn pitman arm or tie rod end. Steering linkage is safety-critical; replace anything with play. Torque the pitman nut and all linkage fasteners to spec and use cotter pins or new lock nuts.\n\nA vibration-isolated steering shaft (rag joint or Borgeson U-joint with a damper) keeps engine and road vibration out of the wheel. Skipping it leaves the steering buzzy.\n\nA complete EB power steering conversion kit runs $700\u20131,400 depending on box quality and whether it's engine-matched. A box-only upgrade for trucks that already have power steering is $300\u2013500 (Borgeson). Add hoses ($75) and fresh tie rod ends if needed ($40\u201380). Realistic total: $500\u20131,400. Wild Horses, PSC Motorsports, and James Duff sell complete EB kits with matched brackets and pulleys \u2014 the kit route is worth it here because pulley alignment and bracket fitment are where DIY conversions go wrong."},{"id":"bronco-steering-power-conversion","title":"Manual-to-Power Steering Conversion","vehicle":"bronco","category":"steering","subcategory":"power_steering","type":"kit","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":600,"high":1800},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","pickle fork / tie rod separator","pulley puller","pitman arm puller","hydraulic line wrenches","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["steering geometry","hydraulic line routing","pump bracket fitment","bleeding a steering system"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","daily_driver"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["steering","power steering","conversion","steering box","big tires","safety_critical"],"safety_critical":true,"parts":[{"name":"Power steering conversion kit (box, pump, bracket, lines)","vendor":"PSC / WFO Concepts / Tom's Offroad","price_estimate_usd":900},{"name":"Saginaw power steering box (early Bronco fit)","vendor":"PSC / Borgeson","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Pump, bracket, and pulley for your engine","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":250}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco power steering conversion","url":"https://www.pscmotorsports.com/"},{"title":"Steering box and crossover options","url":"https://www.wfoconcepts.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-steering-box-upgrade"],"summary":"Convert to power steering once you're running 33s or bigger, or any time arm-wrestling the manual box on the trail has worn you out \u2014 it's one of the best quality-of-life upgrades on an early Bronco. Done right it's also a steering-safety upgrade, because a proper conversion replaces tired manual components with a stronger box and crossover linkage. Done wrong, with mismatched parts, it introduces play and bump steer, so buy a matched kit.","description":"The early Bronco came with manual steering, which is fine on stock 31s and exhausting on 35s, especially crawling at low speed where you're muscling the wheel with no momentum to help. A power steering conversion adds a hydraulic assist and, in most good kits, a stronger Saginaw box and upgraded linkage. The result is a truck you can place precisely on a rock without fighting it, and that's worth real money to anyone who wheels regularly.\n\nThe honest framing: this is a quality-of-life and capability upgrade, not a cheap one. Budget $600\u20131,800 depending on whether you do a basic box swap or a full crossover-steering kit. If you're on stock-size tires and rarely crawl, the manual box is livable. If you're on 33s or bigger, this changes the truck.\n\nBuy a matched conversion kit rather than assembling parts from a parts-store shelf. A complete kit includes the power box (usually a Saginaw), the pump and a bracket that bolts to your specific engine (170 I6, 302, or 351W differ), the pulley, the high- and low-pressure lines, and the pitman arm. Mismatched pumps, brackets, and boxes are the usual reason a home-built conversion ends up with leaks, wandering, or interference.\n\nFor trucks on big tires, consider a crossover (high-steer) conversion at the same time. It changes the steering linkage geometry to reduce bump steer with a lift and routes the drag link to clear the axle \u2014 a worthwhile pairing if you're already in there and running a tall lift.\n\nThis is a substantial job touching steering and hydraulics. Remove the manual box and pitman arm (a puller is required \u2014 don't hammer it). Mount the power box to the frame, fit the pump bracket to the engine, and install the pulley. Route and connect the pressure and return lines, keeping them clear of the exhaust and moving parts. Set the pitman arm and drag link, then check steering geometry through full travel for bump steer and binding. Fill with the correct fluid and bleed the system by cycling the steering lock to lock with the front wheels off the ground until the air is out.\n\nSteering is safety-critical: every fastener gets torqued to spec and verified, and the system must be bled fully so it doesn't aerate and lose assist mid-corner.\n\nSteering is a system where a loose or wrong-torqued fastener can fail and take away your control of the truck. Torque the box mounts, the pitman nut, and the tie-rod and drag-link ends to spec, and verify they're tight before driving.\n\nBump steer is the common conversion complaint \u2014 the truck darts over bumps because the drag link and track bar aren't parallel after the change. A crossover/high-steer setup or correct drag-link geometry fixes it; ignoring it makes the truck nervous at speed.\n\nLine routing near the exhaust is a leak-and-fire risk. Keep high-pressure lines clear and supported. And don't skip the bleed: trapped air gives you mushy, fading assist exactly when you're loading the system in a corner.\n\nA basic power steering box-and-pump conversion runs $600\u20131,000. A complete kit with crossover/high-steer linkage runs $1,200\u20131,800. Reputable early Bronco steering sources (PSC, WFO Concepts, Tom's Offroad, Borgeson) sell matched kits cut for the EB frame and your engine.\n\nYou probably don't need the full crossover kit if you're on a mild lift and 33s \u2014 a clean box-and-pump conversion transforms the truck. But if you're on a tall lift and 35s, do the high-steer geometry at the same time so you fix bump steer once instead of chasing it later."},{"id":"bronco-steering-stabilizer-brace","title":"Steering Stabilizer and Box Brace \u2014 Taming the Wander","vehicle":"bronco","category":"steering","subcategory":"stability","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":400},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","drill","thread locker","pry bar"],"skills_required":["steering component R&R","bracket mounting"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","weekend_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["steering stabilizer","steering box brace","steering","stability","wander","safety"],"parts":[{"name":"Steering stabilizer (damper)","vendor":"Bilstein / Fox","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"Steering box brace kit","vendor":"WildHorses / James Duff","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Grade 8 hardware and bushings","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":30}],"safety_critical":true,"citations":[{"title":"Steering Box Brace and Stabilizer \u2014 early Bronco stability","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-steering-box-upgrade","bronco-susp-shackle-reversal"],"summary":"**Safety relevant.** Early Broncos are known for nervous, wandering steering, and worn or flexing steering components make it worse. A steering box brace stops the frame from flexing under steering load, and a stabilizer damps the shimmy that lift and bigger tires introduce. Neither replaces worn parts \u2014 diagnose and fix slop first \u2014 but on a sound front end they make the truck calmer and safer to drive at speed.","description":"The early Bronco's steering box mounts to the frame rail, and under hard steering load \u2014 especially with larger tires \u2014 the frame can flex at the mount. That flex shows up as vague, delayed steering response. Add a lift, which changes geometry and reduces caster, plus heavier tires that resist turning, and the result is a truck that wanders and darts and demands constant correction.\n\nThis is the honest part: a stabilizer and a brace are not a cure for worn steering components. If your steering wanders, first check for play in the tie rod ends, drag link, kingpins, steering box, and pitman arm. A stabilizer that masks a worn tie rod end is hiding a part that can fail completely. Have someone rock the wheel while you watch each joint, and replace anything with visible play before adding bracing.\n\nOnce the components are sound, the brace and stabilizer address the remaining stability problems \u2014 frame flex and bump-induced shimmy.\n\nA box brace ties the steering box to the opposite frame rail or to a reinforcing structure, so steering loads no longer flex the frame at the box mount. The improvement in steering precision is immediate and real on trucks running larger tires. The brace bolts in with grade 8 hardware; some kits require drilling the frame, which must be done carefully and the holes deburred and protected against rust.\n\nThis is the higher-value of the two upgrades for a truck with bigger tires, because frame flex at the box is a root cause, not a symptom.\n\nA steering stabilizer is a damper \u2014 essentially a shock absorber mounted horizontally between the tie rod and the axle. It does not fix wander; it damps oscillation. Its real job is killing the shimmy or \"death wobble\"-style shake that lift and large tires can introduce, and softening kickback from trail impacts so they do not snatch the wheel from your hands.\n\nA stabilizer is a complement to good steering, never a substitute. If your truck needs a heavy-duty stabilizer to feel stable, you have an underlying problem \u2014 worn parts, bad alignment, or insufficient caster \u2014 that the damper is hiding.\n\nDo not use a stabilizer to mask a real defect. A shaking front end on a solid-axle truck can stem from worn joints, loose wheel bearings, or low caster, and those are the things that cause genuinely dangerous instability. Fix the cause; add the damper to refine an already-sound setup.\n\nIf you drilled the frame for the brace, treat the bare metal \u2014 even in dry Arizona, a drilled hole in a frame rail is a place for corrosion to start.\n\nYou probably don't need a high-zoot dual stabilizer on a mild build with stock-size tires and a sound front end. A single quality damper and good components handle that. Save the dual setup for trucks on 35s and up that genuinely shimmy.\n\n- Steering stabilizer (single, quality): $80\n- Steering box brace kit: $180\n- Hardware and bushings: $30\n\nBudget $90 for a stabilizer alone, up to $400 for a brace plus a premium stabilizer. Diagnose worn parts first \u2014 the bracing and damping only pay off on a front end that is already tight."},{"id":"bronco-susp-2in-lift","title":"2\" Mild Lift \u2014 Leveling and Small Tire Clearance","vehicle":"bronco","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","spring compressor","3/4\" socket set","torque wrench","pry bar"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","spring removal"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["lift","2 inch","suspension","springs","leveling"],"parts":[{"name":"Wild Horses / Duff 2.5\" front lift leaf springs (pair)","vendor":"Wild Horses 4x4","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Rear add-a-leaf or 2\" rear lift leaf pack","vendor":"Duff Offroad","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Rancho RS5000X shocks (set of 4)","vendor":"Rancho","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Greaseable shackle and bushing kit","vendor":"Wild Horses 4x4","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Degree caster shims (pair)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":40}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco lift options \u2014 Wild Horses 4x4","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/"},{"title":"Early Bronco suspension tech \u2014 Duff Offroad","url":"https://www.duffoffroad.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-susp-4in-lift","bronco-susp-shocks"],"summary":"A 2\" lift on an early Bronco is about tire clearance and recovering a sagged stance, not articulation. The early Bronco is leaf-sprung front and rear, so a \"2-inch lift\" means taller leaf packs (or an add-a-leaf), longer shocks, and an alignment to put caster back where it belongs. Done right it clears 33\" tires and rides better than worn-out factory springs. It does not transform the truck off-road \u2014 for that, see the [4\" lift](/db/bronco/bronco-susp-4in-lift/).","description":"A 2\" lift gives enough room to clear 33\" tires without rubbing, nudges the departure and approach angles, and levels a truck whose 50-year-old springs have settled. Because both ends of an early Bronco ride on leaf springs, the cleanest way to gain height and restore ride quality at the same time is new leaf packs rather than a spacer or a block.\n\n- Taller front leaf springs (most early-Bronco \"2 inch\" front packs net 2\u20132.5\" over a sagged stock spring) or a front add-a-leaf\n- Rear add-a-leaf or a replacement 2\" rear leaf pack\n- Four extended shocks \u2014 stock-length shocks top out and self-destruct with any lift\n- Greaseable shackles and polyurethane or rubber bushings to refresh worn pivots\n- Caster shims to correct front-axle pinion and steering-axis angle after the lift\n\nWork one corner at a time so the axle never hangs unsupported. With the frame on jack stands and the axle supported on a floor jack:\n\n1. Remove the shock, then the U-bolts holding the axle to the spring. Loosen the spring eye bolt and the shackle bolt.\n2. Drop the old pack out. Fifty-year-old spring eye bolts and shackle bolts are frequently seized \u2014 soak them in penetrant ahead of time and have a torch and a spare hardware kit ready rather than fighting rounded heads on the trail of the job.\n3. Install the new pack, starting the fixed eye bolt first, then the shackle. Fit fresh greaseable shackles and bushings while it is apart \u2014 doing this later means tearing it down twice.\n4. Re-seat the axle on the spring center pin, torque the U-bolts in a cross pattern to roughly 70\u201390 ft-lb (verify against your kit's spec), and install the new shocks.\n5. Repeat per corner, then roll it off the stands and let the suspension settle before measuring ride height.\n\nAny lift changes front-axle caster. Stock caster runs about 1\u20132\u00b0 positive; a 2\" lift rotates the axle and often drops caster below spec, which shows up as lazy steering return and wander. Caster shims between the spring and the axle perch (or a shop setting caster on the rack) restore it. If the truck already wanders before the lift, address the steering box and linkage first \u2014 the [steering stabilizer and box brace guide](/db/bronco/bronco-steering-stabilizer-brace/) covers what a stabilizer can and cannot fix. For shocks, the Rancho RS5000X or Bilstein 5100 in the correct extended length bolt to the stock mounts and handle trail chop well; shock selection is detailed in the [shocks guide](/db/bronco/bronco-susp-shocks/).\n\nA 2\" lift clears 33\" tires in most applications, sometimes with light trimming of the inner front fender lip at full stuff. Confirm clearance on your own truck at full articulation before committing to a tire \u2014 backspacing matters as much as lift height, so cross-check the [tire sizing](/db/bronco/bronco-wt-tire-sizing/) and wheel backspacing guides. Going to 33s also pushes you toward [regearing](/db/bronco/bronco-axle-gear-ratio/) if you are running tall factory gears, since the taller tire drops your effective ratio.\n\nA front and rear leaf upgrade with new shackles and bushings runs $400\u2013$600 in parts; add $220 for a set of four shocks and roughly $40 for caster shims. An alignment at a shop that understands solid-axle Fords is $100\u2013$150 and is not optional. Total out-the-door for a do-it-yourself 2\" leaf lift lands around $450\u2013$700 plus the alignment.","pros":["clears 33\" tires cleanly at low cost","new leaf packs ride better than tired stock springs","bolt-on with no fabrication","minimal geometry change keeps driveline angles tame"],"cons":["does not improve articulation the way a 4\" system with longer springs does","requires an alignment and caster correction immediately","stock-length shocks must be replaced or they fail fast","seized 50-year-old spring hardware can turn a half-day job into a full one"]},{"id":"bronco-susp-4in-lift","title":"4\" Suspension Lift \u2014 Trail Build Baseline","vehicle":"bronco","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":20},"cost_usd":{"low":800,"high":2200},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands (4)","torque wrench","breaker bar","1/2\" impact or long-handle wrenches","angle grinder","penetrating oil","caster/angle gauge","spring spreader or pry bar","line wrenches for brake fittings"],"skills_required":["suspension work","alignment basics","brake line knowledge","bleeding brakes"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["lift","4 inch","suspension","springs","trail","35s"],"parts":[{"name":"Wild Horses / James Duff 4\" complete leaf spring kit, early Bronco","vendor":"Wild Horses 4x4 / James Duff","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"Skyjacker 4\" early Bronco spring kit (budget tier)","vendor":"Skyjacker","price_estimate_usd":950},{"name":"Bilstein 5100 series shocks (set of 4)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Stainless braided extended brake lines (front pair + rear)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Caster correction shims, 2-4 degree tapered pair","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"Greasable shackles and polyurethane bushing set","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Drop pitman arm or crossover steering kit","vendor":"James Duff / WH4x4","price_estimate_usd":180}],"citations":[{"title":"Superlift \u2014 early Bronco lift kits","url":"https://www.superlift.com/"},{"title":"Wild Horses 4x4 \u2014 early Bronco suspension","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/"},{"title":"Lift and caster discussion \u2014 ClassicBroncos forums","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-susp-2in-lift","bronco-susp-soa","bronco-susp-shocks"],"summary":"4\" is the standard trail-build lift for an early Bronco. It clears 35\" tires with the right backspacing, gives a real articulation gain over a 2\" kit, and stays in a height range where quality twin-tube or monotube shocks still work without remote reservoirs. Budget $800 for a DIY spring-and-shock install and closer to $2,200 once caster correction, extended brake lines, steering geometry, and an alignment are included. Those supporting items are what separate a safe 4\" lift from a twitchy one, and skipping them is the reason so many lifted early Broncos feel nervous above 50 mph.","description":"A 4\" suspension lift is the baseline for a capable early Bronco trail build, sitting between the mild [2\" lift](/db/bronco/bronco-susp-2in-lift/) and a full [spring-over-axle conversion](/db/bronco/bronco-susp-soa/). It buys 35\" tire clearance, better approach and departure angles, real articulation, and driveline angles that remain manageable with stock-length shafts. Because the early Bronco is leaf-sprung at both ends with no track bars or control arms, a lift here is a spring swap rather than a geometry rebuild \u2014 which is what keeps the job inside a home garage.\n\n- **35\" tire clearance** with 3.75\"-4.5\" backspacing wheels, usually with modest fender trimming at the front corners\n- **Approach and departure angle gains** that show up immediately on ledges\n- **Genuine articulation improvement** over a 2\" kit, because 4\" packs use longer, softer multi-leaf designs rather than added arch alone\n- **Room for a 2-2.5\" body-to-tire buffer** at full compression once bumpstops are extended\n\nConfirm wheel fitment against the [backspacing guide](/db/bronco/bronco-wt-wheel-backspacing/) before ordering. The wrong offset either rubs the radius of the fender at full stuff or pushes the tire out far enough to load the closed-knuckle Dana 44's wheel bearings harder than the design intends.\n\nNot all 4\" kits are the same spring. The difference between a pleasant truck and a buckboard is leaf count and pack design, not the advertised height.\n\n- **Wild Horses 4x4 and James Duff** build early-Bronco-specific packs with more leaves of thinner stock. They ride softer and flex further, and run roughly $1,000-1,300 for a complete four-corner kit.\n- **Skyjacker and Superlift** kits are the value tier at $900-1,100 complete. They lift correctly and are widely supported, but a stiffer pack rides firmer on pavement.\n- **Tuff Country** sits between the two on both price and ride.\n- **Bargain no-name kits** save $300 and cost it back in a harsh ride and springs that sag within two seasons.\n\nWhatever you pick, buy the kit with greasable shackles and a full polyurethane or rubber bushing set included. Reusing 50-year-old shackle bushings on new springs wastes the new springs.\n\n- **Shocks.** 4\" of lift demands extended-travel shocks. Bilstein 5100s or Fox 2.0 IFP units are the common choices at $80-140 per corner. Stock-length shocks will top out and let the axle pound the bumpstops, which destroys both the shock and the spring's main leaf. Match length to the measured extended and collapsed dimensions at your new ride height \u2014 see the [shock selection guide](/db/bronco/bronco-susp-shocks/).\n- **Brake lines.** Stock lines go taut at full droop with 4\" of lift. Stainless braided extended lines run about $110 for the truck and are a safety requirement rather than an upgrade. A line that stretches at articulation tears at the crimp, and you find out at the worst moment.\n- **Bumpstop extensions.** Adding 4\" of static height without extending bumpstops means the tire finds the fender before the suspension finds its stop. Extend with a stack of urethane pucks or a weld-on tower.\n- **Extended sway bar links** if your truck carries the factory front bar. Stock links are too short at 4\" and will bind or tear their mounts.\n\nThis is the section owners skip and then spend a year regretting.\n\n**Caster.** Arching a leaf spring rotates the axle, and 4\" of lift pulls roughly 2 degrees of caster out of the front end. Low caster reads as vague on-center feel, slow return-to-center, and a truck that wanders in a crosswind. Correct it with tapered degree shims between the spring and the perch \u2014 $30 for a pair \u2014 or by cutting and rotating the perches on a rebuilt front end. Target 4-6 degrees positive at ride height and verify with an angle gauge on the knuckle face, not by feel.\n\n**Steering geometry.** The early Bronco's box-and-bellcrank steering puts the drag link at an angle that changes with lift. At 4\" the drag link and the front spring travel through different arcs, and the resulting bump steer shows as the truck darting when one front tire hits a bump. A drop pitman arm ($120-180) is the cheap correction; a crossover or high-steer conversion is the thorough one. Pair either with the [steering box brace and stabilizer](/db/bronco/bronco-steering-stabilizer-brace/), because a lifted early Bronco puts real load into a frame-mounted box that was never braced from the factory.\n\n**Shackle geometry.** If the truck is road-driven and still feels nervous after caster correction, a [front shackle reversal](/db/bronco/bronco-susp-shackle-reversal/) is the next step. It changes the axle's fore-aft arc and generally improves both ride and steering stability.\n\nAt 4\", front and rear driveshaft angles increase but stay inside U-joint operating range with stock suspension travel, so a slip yoke eliminator is not mandated by the lift alone. Reconsider once you add a locker, chase aggressive articulation, or go taller \u2014 at that point read the [driveshaft upgrade guide](/db/bronco/bronco-dt-driveshaft-upgrade/) and the [SYE and CV driveshaft guide](/db/bronco/bronco-dt-sye-cv-driveshaft/). Inspect front U-joints annually regardless; the steeper operating angle wears them faster than stock.\n\nGearing is the other half of the 35\" decision. Stock 3.50 or 4.11 gears with 35s make the truck dull off idle and hard on the clutch or the C4. Plan a [re-gear to 4.56 or 4.88](/db/bronco/bronco-regear/) into the same budget rather than treating it as a later surprise, and recalibrate the speedometer to match.\n\n1. Break the U-bolt and shackle hardware loose with penetrating oil applied a day ahead. Fifty-year-old shackle bolts seize into their bushings, and a cutoff wheel is often faster than a fight.\n2. Support the frame on stands and the axle on a floor jack. Work one axle at a time so the other end holds the truck located.\n3. Fit the new center pins and set the spring on the perch before torquing U-bolts. New U-bolts come with the kit \u2014 reusing stretched ones is a false economy.\n4. Torque U-bolts in a cross pattern to the kit spec (typically 85-110 ft-lb), then re-torque after the first 100 miles and again at 500. New spring packs settle.\n5. Fit extended brake lines and bleed the system before the truck touches the ground.\n6. Set caster with shims, then get a front-end alignment with toe set to spec.\n\n- **Spring wrap under power.** Taller lifts increase axle wrap on leaf springs, which shows up as a shudder on hard acceleration. Traction bars or a stiffer front spring pack resolve it.\n- **Sagging within a season.** A pack that loses an inch in six months was underbuilt or overloaded. Winch bumpers and full-size spares change the front spring rate you need.\n- **Death-wobble-adjacent symptoms.** A lifted early Bronco that shakes at 45-55 mph nearly always has a worn bellcrank, loose steering box, or uncorrected caster rather than a bad tire balance. Chase the geometry first.\n- **Fender contact at stuff.** Expect to trim the front fender lips for 35s \u2014 the [fender trimming guide](/db/bronco/bronco-body-fender-trim/) covers how far to go without hurting the body line.\n\n| Item | Typical cost |\n|---|---|\n| 4\" complete spring kit (value tier) | $900-1,100 |\n| 4\" complete spring kit (Wild Horses / Duff) | $1,000-1,300 |\n| Shocks, set of four | $320-560 |\n| Extended stainless brake lines | $110 |\n| Caster shims | $30 |\n| Drop pitman arm | $120-180 |\n| Bumpstop extensions | $60 |\n| Alignment | $100-150 |\n| **DIY total** | **$1,700-2,200** |\n\nThe $800 low end assumes springs and shocks only on a truck whose steering and brake lines are already sorted. Plan on $2,000 for a complete, safe 4\" build, and treat the [re-gear](/db/bronco/bronco-regear/) as a separate line item that follows within the same season.","pros":["clears 35\" tires with appropriate wheels and modest trimming","real articulation and departure-angle improvement over a 2\" kit","complete bolt-on kits available from multiple established vendors","no fabrication required beyond brake lines and bumpstop extensions"],"cons":["extended brake lines and caster correction are mandatory and safety-critical","bump steer needs a drop pitman arm or crossover steering to fully resolve","front driveshaft angles increase \u2014 inspect U-joints annually","costs three to four times a 2\" kit once supporting parts are counted","35s without a re-gear make the truck noticeably slower and harder on the drivetrain"]},{"id":"bronco-susp-shackle-reversal","title":"Front Shackle Reversal \u2014 Ride and Caster Fix","vehicle":"bronco","category":"suspension","subcategory":"geometry","type":"upgrade","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":14},"cost_usd":{"low":300,"high":800},"tools_required":["MIG welder","angle grinder","floor jack","jack stands (4)","torque wrench","drill and step bits","measuring tape","caster/angle gauge","plumb bob or laser level","spring clamps"],"skills_required":["welding","suspension geometry","frame bracket fabrication","measuring and squaring a chassis"],"build_tiers":["weekend_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["shackle reversal","suspension","caster","ride quality","geometry"],"parts":[{"name":"Shackle reversal kit (weld-on, early Bronco)","vendor":"Wild Horses 4x4 / James Duff","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Greasable shackles and polyurethane bushing set","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Caster correction shims, tapered pair","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"Extended stainless front brake lines (pair)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"Drop pitman arm (if bump steer persists)","vendor":"James Duff","price_estimate_usd":150}],"citations":[{"title":"Shackle reversal explained \u2014 ClassicBroncos forums","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"},{"title":"Wild Horses 4x4 \u2014 early Bronco shackle reversal kits","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-susp-2in-lift","bronco-susp-soa","bronco-steering-stabilizer-brace"],"summary":"A shackle reversal moves the front spring's fixed mount to the front of the spring and the shackle to the rear, changing how the front axle reacts to bumps and to braking. The payoff is a noticeably calmer highway ride and steering that stops darting. It is real fabrication work with welding on the frame, and it earns its keep on trucks that see significant road miles or have developed the wandering, nervous steering these Broncos are known for. Budget $300 if you weld a kit in yourself and $800 with shop welding plus an alignment. If your truck is trail-only and already steers acceptably, spend the money on a [steering box brace](/db/bronco/bronco-steering-stabilizer-brace/) and worn-component replacement first.","description":"The factory 1966-1977 Bronco front suspension mounts the spring shackle at the front and the fixed eye at the rear. When the front tire strikes a bump, the axle's arc carries it up and slightly rearward, into the impact rather than away from it. Reversing the arrangement \u2014 fixed eye forward, shackle at the rear \u2014 flips that arc so the axle moves up and rearward away from the obstacle, absorbing the hit instead of transmitting it into the frame and the steering.\n\nA leaf spring pivots around whichever eye is fixed. The shackle end is the one allowed to move fore and aft as the spring flattens and lengthens under load.\n\n- **Factory layout (shackle forward):** the fixed pivot is at the rear, so the axle swings on an arc centered behind it. A bump pushes the axle up and *toward* the obstacle. Under braking, torque reaction tries to wind the spring in the same direction it is already loaded, which contributes to nose dive and to a front end that feels busy.\n- **Reversed layout (shackle rearward):** the fixed pivot is at the front. The axle now swings up and slightly *away* from the impact, and brake torque reacts against the fixed eye. The result is anti-dive behavior under braking and a front end that stops fighting the steering over washboard.\n\nMost reversal kits also relocate the axle roughly 1 to 1.5 inches forward. That extra clearance between the tire and the firewall is a side benefit when running 33s or 35s, and it improves approach angle slightly.\n\nThe honest benefits are ride quality and steering stability, not capability. A shackle reversal adds no lift and no articulation. What it does:\n\n- **Highway composure.** The most-reported result is that the truck stops requiring constant small corrections. Owners describe the factory arrangement as \"you cannot take your hands off the wheel\"; a good reversal is the fix people credit most often.\n- **Better bump absorption** on pavement seams, washboard, and gravel roads.\n- **Anti-dive under braking**, which matters more once you have added a [front disc conversion](/db/bronco/bronco-brakes-front-disc-swap/) that actually stops the truck.\n- **Caster improvement.** Lift reduces caster, and low caster is what kills self-centering feel. The reversal, combined with correction shims where needed, helps put caster back in the 4-6 degree range where the steering settles.\n\nWhat it does not do: it will not fix a worn bellcrank, a loose steering box, sloppy kingpins, or bad tire balance. If the truck shakes at 45-55 mph, diagnose those first \u2014 a reversal welded onto worn components produces an expensive truck that still shakes.\n\nThis is weld-on work on the frame. A reversal kit from Wild Horses, James Duff, or a comparable vendor supplies new front spring hangers, rear shackle hangers, and the hardware.\n\n1. **Measure and record everything first.** Wheelbase, spring eye centers, and ride height at all four corners, with the truck on level ground. You are going to change this geometry deliberately, so you need the before numbers.\n2. **Support the frame on stands** and the axle on a floor jack. Remove the front springs and the factory hangers \u2014 the factory brackets are riveted or welded, so plan on a grinder and a chisel.\n3. **Position the new front (fixed) hanger** per the kit's measurement from a reference point on the frame. Measure side to side and diagonally. A hanger 1/4\" out of square puts the axle out of square with the chassis and produces a truck that dog-tracks.\n4. **Weld both hangers in** with clean, fully penetrated welds. Frame brackets carry the entire front suspension load \u2014 this is not a place for tacks and hope. If your welding is not up to structural work, have this portion done by a shop; it is a two-hour job for someone competent.\n5. **Reinstall springs** with greasable shackles and fresh polyurethane or rubber bushings. Reusing 50-year-old bushings on new geometry wastes the work.\n6. **Set ride height, then measure caster** with an angle gauge on the knuckle face and correct with tapered shims as needed.\n7. **Fit extended brake lines** if the axle's new position or travel puts the stock lines under tension at droop.\n8. **Align the front end** and confirm toe and steering geometry on the ground under the truck's own weight.\n\nPrecise bracket placement is the entire job. Get the spring angle or the wheelbase wrong and you introduce bump steer or undo the caster the kit was designed to establish.\n\nA shackle reversal pairs naturally with a [4\" lift](/db/bronco/bronco-susp-4in-lift/), and the two are often installed in the same weekend since the springs are out either way. Doing both at once also means one alignment instead of two.\n\nWatch the steering after the change. Moving the axle forward alters the drag link angle relative to the spring's arc, so a truck that had acceptable bump steer before may need a drop pitman arm afterward \u2014 or, on taller builds, a crossover steering conversion. If you are already planning a [power steering conversion](/db/bronco/bronco-steering-power-conversion/), sequence it after the reversal so the geometry is settled before you set up the box and linkage.\n\nFront bumper and winch clearance changes too. With the axle 1-1.5\" forward, verify your [front bumper](/db/bronco/bronco-armor-front-bumper/) and any winch fairlead still clear the tire at full lock and full compression.\n\n- **Squaring errors.** The single most common failure is a hanger welded out of square, producing dog-tracking or uneven tire wear that no alignment can fix. Measure three times.\n- **Frame condition.** Welding new brackets to a rusty frame rail is a structural problem, not a suspension one. Probe the rail thoroughly and repair or plate it before mounting anything.\n- **Caster drift.** Re-check caster after 500 miles. New bushings and springs settle, and the number you set on install day moves.\n- **Overselling the result.** A reversal on a truck with worn steering components changes very little. Sort the wear items first, then decide whether you still want the geometry change.\n\n| Item | Typical cost |\n|---|---|\n| Weld-on shackle reversal kit | $300-400 |\n| Greasable shackles and bushing set | $90 |\n| Caster correction shims | $30 |\n| Extended front brake lines | $80 |\n| Alignment | $100-150 |\n| Shop welding (if farmed out), 2-3 hrs | $150-300 |\n| **DIY total** | **$300-500** |\n| **With shop welding and alignment** | **$650-800** |\n\nThe parts are modest. The value sits entirely in careful bracket placement and a proper alignment afterward \u2014 which is why a $350 kit installed carelessly is worse than no kit at all.","pros":["meaningful highway ride and steering-stability improvement on road-driven trucks","anti-dive behavior under braking","helps restore caster lost to a lift","adds 1-1.5\" of tire-to-firewall clearance as a side benefit"],"cons":["requires structural welding on the frame","adds no lift and no articulation \u2014 this is a ride and handling change only","bracket placement errors cause dog-tracking that alignment cannot correct","can expose bump steer that needs a drop pitman arm to resolve","poor value on a trail-only truck with worn steering components"]},{"id":"bronco-susp-shocks","title":"Shocks for the Early Bronco \u2014 Choosing and Installing","vehicle":"bronco","category":"suspension","subcategory":"shocks","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","wrench set","torque wrench","penetrating oil"],"skills_required":["measuring travel","torquing to spec","reading a shock chart"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["suspension","shocks","ride quality","damping","lift"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein 5100 monotube shock (lift-matched)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"Rancho RS5000X twin-tube shock","vendor":"Rancho","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"Fox 2.0 IFP smooth-body shock","vendor":"Fox","price_estimate_usd":140},{"name":"Extended-travel shock relocation hardware","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":60}],"citations":[{"title":"Bilstein 5100 application guide","url":"https://www.bilstein.com/"},{"title":"Early Bronco shock length and travel","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"},{"title":"Matching shock travel to lift height","url":"https://www.fox.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-susp-2in-lift","bronco-susp-4in-lift","bronco-susp-soa"],"amazon_links":null,"summary":"Match the shock to the lift, not the lift to the shock. The single most common early Bronco shock mistake is bolting on a shock that's too short for the lift \u2014 it runs out of down-travel and limits articulation, or it's too long and bottoms out internally on compression. Measure your extended and compressed lengths at the mounts before you buy anything.","description":"A shock controls how fast the spring moves. It does not hold the truck up and it does not set ride height \u2014 that's the spring's job. What the shock does is damp the spring's oscillation so the tire stays planted instead of bouncing down the trail or porpoising on the highway. On a leaf-sprung early Bronco, the right shock is the difference between a truck that tracks straight at 60 mph and one that wanders and wallows.\n\nThe honest verdict for most builds: a quality twin-tube or entry monotube shock matched to your lift height covers the vast majority of early Bronco use. You probably don't need a remote-reservoir race shock unless you're running fast in the desert. Spend the money on getting the length right first, then on damping quality.\n\nTwo numbers decide everything: the extended length (shock fully drooped, at the mounting eyes) and the compressed length (suspension fully stuffed). Cycle the suspension through its full travel with the spring disconnected or the axle on a jack, and measure mount-to-mount at both extremes. The shock's extended length should be slightly longer than your full-droop measurement so a limit strap \u2014 not the shock \u2014 stops down-travel, and its compressed length must be longer than your full-stuff measurement so it never bottoms internally.\n\nFor a stock-height or 2-inch lift, factory-length replacement shocks usually work. At 3.5\u20134 inches you'll need longer shocks and often a relocation bracket up top to recover droop. At SOA height you're almost always relocating mounts and may run a dual-shock or longer-body setup.\n\nShock types, plainly: twin-tube shocks (Rancho RS5000X, Bilstein B6) are inexpensive, ride well on the street, and fade under sustained hard use as the oil heats. Monotube shocks (Bilstein 5100, Fox 2.0) run cooler, resist fade, and give more consistent damping \u2014 the better choice for any truck that sees real trail miles or desert running. Remote-reservoir shocks add oil capacity for sustained high-speed work and cost the most; reserve them for fast desert builds.\n\nSupport the frame on jack stands and let the axle hang, or support the axle to take the load off the shock mounts. Soak the lower shock bolt with penetrant \u2014 early Bronco shock studs corrode and snap. Remove the old shock top and bottom.\n\nCompress the new shock by hand and seat the bushings and washers in the correct order (washer, bushing, mount, bushing, washer, nut). Snug the lower mount first, then the upper. Torque the stud nuts only until the rubber bushing bulges slightly past the washer edge \u2014 over-torquing crushes the bushing and kills its isolation. Cycle the suspension and confirm the shock has clearance to the spring, frame, and tire at full stuff and full droop.\n\nA shock too short for the lift is the classic failure: it becomes the droop limiter, yanking on its mounts and tearing them loose over time, and it caps your articulation. If the shock goes tight before the spring or limit strap does, it's wrong.\n\nMixing shock lengths front to rear, or a worn shock paired with a new one, gives uneven damping and a truck that handles differently at each corner. Replace in pairs at minimum, all four if budget allows.\n\nBushing order and torque matter more than people expect. Crushed or missing washers let the bushing walk off the stud, and a clunk over bumps is almost always a loose or worn shock bushing, not a failed shock body.\n\nTwin-tube shocks run $45\u201370 each (Rancho RS5000X, Bilstein B6) \u2014 a full set of four is $180\u2013280. Monotube shocks run $75\u2013150 each (Bilstein 5100, Fox 2.0 IFP), so $300\u2013600 for four. Relocation brackets add $40\u201380 if your lift needs them. Remote-reservoir shocks start around $250 each and climb fast \u2014 overkill for a trail-only rig.\n\nBuy from the shock maker's application chart or an early Bronco supplier (Wild Horses, Tom's Offroad, James Duff) that lists shocks by lift height, so the length is right out of the box. The Bilstein 5100 in the correct length is the value pick for most early Bronco builds: monotube fade resistance at a twin-tube price."},{"id":"bronco-susp-soa","title":"Spring Over Axle (SOA) \u2014 Maximum Lift","vehicle":"bronco","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift","type":"task","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":40,"high":100},"cost_usd":{"low":2500,"high":6000},"tools_required":["welder (MIG or TIG)","plasma cutter or angle grinder","lathe access","floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","spring compressor","driveline angle gauge"],"skills_required":["fabrication","welding","driveline geometry","brake line fabrication"],"build_tiers":["rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["SOA","spring over","lift","fabrication","rock crawler","extreme"],"parts":[{"name":"Custom spring perch plates (fabricated)","vendor":"Fabricated","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"Tom Woods custom front driveshaft","vendor":"Tom Woods","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"SYE (slip yoke eliminator) for Dana 20","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Long travel shocks (extended/bypass)","vendor":"King / Fox","price_estimate_usd":900},{"name":"Extended brake lines (full set)","vendor":"Custom","price_estimate_usd":150},{"name":"Extended bump stops","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":60}],"citations":[{"title":"SOA on early Bronco \u2014 fullsizebronco.com build thread","url":"https://www.fullsizebronco.com/forum/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-susp-4in-lift","bronco-dt-sye-cv-driveshaft","bronco-steering-box-upgrade"],"summary":"SOA on an early Bronco is a serious fabrication project \u2014 not a bolt-on lift. Done right it yields 8\u201310\" of lift and 37\u201340\" tire clearance with excellent articulation, but it touches springs, driveshafts, steering, and brakes, and it demands welding skills and a real shop. The result is a capable dedicated trail rig, not a comfortable street truck.","description":"Spring over axle moves the leaf springs from below the axle housing to above it, using the full spring arch as lift rather than losing it under the axle. On an early Bronco with roughly 4.5\" of spring arch, SOA yields about 8\u201310\" of lift depending on spring selection. That's a lot of height from re-mounting parts you already own \u2014 but every system the suspension touches has to be corrected to make it safe and drivable. If 8\u201310\" is more than you need, a bolt-in [4\" lift](/db/bronco/bronco-susp-4in-lift/) gets you 35s with far less work.\n\nThe factory spring perches sit under the axle. SOA relocates them to the top of the housing, which means cutting off the old perches and welding new perch pads to the top of each axle tube. This is precision work \u2014 the perch centerline must align with the axle centerline, and the pinion angle is set here, so getting it wrong cascades into driveline vibration. This is the weld that the whole build rides on; if you're not confident in structural welding, this is the step to hand to a fab shop.\n\nSOA changes the front shackle geometry. Some builders run a shackle reversal kit; others extend or re-locate the factory mount. Each approach has handling tradeoffs, and the goal is to keep the spring operating angle and caster sane so the truck tracks straight. Plan the front-end geometry as a package with the steering correction below, not as an afterthought.\n\nSOA dramatically increases driveshaft angles. A custom front driveshaft (Tom Woods, Dynatrac) is mandatory. At the rear, the transfer case output angle changes enough to require a slip yoke eliminator on the Dana 20 plus a CV-style rear driveshaft \u2014 the same combination covered in [Slip Yoke Eliminator and CV Driveshaft](/db/bronco/bronco-dt-sye-cv-driveshaft/), here treated as non-optional rather than a lift-dependent upgrade. Measure pinion angle at ride height on the ground before ordering shafts.\n\nWith the axle dropped well below the frame, the drag link and tie rod now sit at a steep angle, which causes severe bump steer if left uncorrected. A dropped pitman arm or a high-steer kit restores the steering geometry. Pair it with a stout steering box and brace \u2014 see [Steering Box Upgrade](/db/bronco/bronco-steering-box-upgrade/) \u2014 because the leverage from 37s magnifies any slop in a tired factory box.\n\nAll brake lines must be custom-fabricated or extended to reach the dropped axle through its full travel. This is safety-critical \u2014 a brake line pulled tight at full droop will tear, so there are no shortcuts here. Factory shock mounts also end up at the wrong height and angle and have to be cut off and re-welded for long-travel shocks. Extended bump stops are required to keep the tire out of the body and the shock off its internal stops at full compression.\n\nThe recurring SOA failures are all geometry: pinion angle set wrong at the perch weld (chronic vibration), steering left uncorrected (dangerous bump steer), and brake lines built to static length instead of full-droop length. Mock up the full suspension cycle \u2014 stuff and droop each corner \u2014 before final-welding anything. And be honest about use: an 8\u201310\" SOA Bronco on 37s is a trail machine that drives differently on the highway, with a higher center of gravity and slower steering. If the truck still needs to be a daily driver, the milder lift is the better call.\n\n- Custom perches and welding (if shop-built): $400\u2013900\n- Front driveshaft + SYE + CV rear: $700\u20131,100\n- Long-travel shocks: $900\n- Extended brake lines, bump stops, steering correction: $400\u2013700\n\nBudget $2,500 if you fabricate and weld it yourself with quality components, $6,000+ for a shop-built SOA with premium shocks and high-steer. The hardware isn't the expensive part \u2014 the fabrication time and the correction of every connected system is."},{"id":"bronco-diag-compression-test","title":"Compression and Leak-Down Testing an Early Bronco Engine","vehicle":"bronco","category":"tools_diagnostic","subcategory":"engine diagnosis","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":90},"tools_required":["compression tester","leak-down tester (optional)","shop air compressor (for leak-down)","spark plug socket","ratchet and extension","remote starter switch (optional)"],"skills_required":["removing spark plugs","reading gauge values","interpreting cylinder-to-cylinder variance"],"build_tiers":["stock_resto","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["tools_diagnostic","compression test","leak-down","engine health","diagnosis","buying"],"parts":[{"name":"Screw-in compression tester kit","vendor":"OTC / Actron","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Cylinder leak-down tester","vendor":"OTC / Harbor Freight","price_estimate_usd":50}],"citations":[{"title":"Compression test procedure and specs","url":"https://www.fordification.com/tech/"},{"title":"Leak-down vs compression interpretation","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-diag-vacuum-gauge","bronco-eng-302-v8"],"summary":"A compression test is the truth-teller before you buy an early Bronco or before you decide whether a tired engine is worth keeping. It measures how well each cylinder seals; a leak-down test then tells you *where* the leak is. Run both before spending money on a rebuild \u2014 plenty of \"blown\" early Bronco engines only need a valve job, and plenty of \"good runners\" hide a dead cylinder the seller never mentioned.","description":"The carbureted small-blocks and sixes in these trucks were built for roughly 130\u2013160 psi of cranking compression when new, depending on the engine and altitude. The absolute number matters less than the spread between cylinders: you want every cylinder within about 10\u201315% of the highest one. A single cylinder 25% low is a problem you can see before it becomes a problem you can hear.\n\nThe direct answer on buying: no compression numbers, no deal. If a seller won't let you pull plugs and crank it, assume the worst and price accordingly.\n\nA screw-in compression tester (the threaded-hose kind, not the rubber-tip press-in type \u2014 the press-in style leaks and lies on a warm engine). For diagnosis of *where* a low cylinder is losing its seal, add a leak-down tester, which needs shop air at 90\u2013120 psi. A spark plug socket, a ratchet with a long extension, and a helper or a remote starter switch round it out.\n\nFor the compression test, warm the engine, then shut it off. Disable the ignition (pull the coil wire and ground it) and block the throttle wide open so the engine breathes freely. Pull all the spark plugs \u2014 testing with the other plugs still in skews the numbers. Thread the tester into the first cylinder, crank the engine through four to six compression strokes, and record the reading. Repeat for every cylinder, cranking the same number of strokes each time so the comparison is fair.\n\nRead the spread. All cylinders within 10\u201315% of each other and in the 130\u2013160 range (lower at altitude \u2014 Phoenix-area trucks read roughly 10\u201315 psi lower) is healthy. To separate a ring problem from a valve problem on a low cylinder, squirt a teaspoon of oil into that cylinder and retest: if the number jumps up, the rings are worn (the oil sealed them); if it stays low, the valves or head gasket are leaking, because oil can't seal those.\n\nThe leak-down test confirms it. Bring the cylinder to top dead center on the compression stroke, apply regulated air through the spark plug hole, and read the percentage of leakage. Under 10% is excellent, 10\u201320% is serviceable, over 25% needs attention. Then listen: air hissing out the carb means an intake valve, air out the exhaust pipe means an exhaust valve, air out the oil filler or dipstick tube means rings, and bubbles in the radiator mean a head gasket or cracked head.\n\nThe most common error is testing cold or with the other plugs installed \u2014 both drag the numbers down and hide real differences. Warm it up and pull every plug.\n\nDon't read a single low number as a death sentence. The oil-squirt step and a leak-down test together tell you whether you're looking at a $200 valve job, a top-end refresh, or a full rebuild. Those are very different decisions.\n\nWatch the threads. The aluminum-head swaps and the original cast-iron heads both have spark plug threads that strip if you cross-thread a hot tester fitting. Start every fitting by hand.\n\nA quality screw-in compression kit runs $35\u201350; a leak-down tester is another $40\u201360, and the Harbor Freight unit is adequate for occasional use. You probably don't need the professional twin-gauge leak-down sets unless you're testing engines regularly \u2014 the single-gauge tester reads accurately enough to make the buy/rebuild call. Spending $90 on both tools before a $4,000 engine decision is the cheapest insurance in the garage."},{"id":"bronco-diag-vacuum-gauge","title":"Reading a Vacuum Gauge to Diagnose a Carbureted Early Bronco","vehicle":"bronco","category":"tools_diagnostic","subcategory":"engine diagnosis","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":15,"high":40},"tools_required":["vacuum gauge","assorted vacuum tee/hose","tachometer (or dwell-tach)"],"skills_required":["locating a manifold vacuum port","reading a gauge against a baseline"],"build_tiers":["stock_resto","mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["tools_diagnostic","vacuum gauge","carburetor","tuning","diagnosis","engine"],"parts":[{"name":"Engine vacuum gauge (0-30 in-Hg)","vendor":"Actron / OTC / Innova","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"Vacuum hose + brass tee assortment","vendor":"Dorman","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"Vacuum gauge diagnosis chart","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"},{"title":"Manifold vacuum tuning basics","url":"https://www.fordification.com/tech/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-diag-compression-test","bronco-maint-tune-up"],"summary":"A $25 vacuum gauge tells you more about a tired early Bronco engine than almost any other tool, and it does it without pulling a single part. Tee it into manifold vacuum, watch the needle, and the way it sits and twitches points straight at the problem \u2014 vacuum leaks, burnt valves, worn rings, late timing, a clogged exhaust. Learn to read it before you start throwing parts at a rough idle.","description":"The carbureted 170/200 six and the 289/302/351 small-blocks in an early Bronco all breathe through a manifold that pulls a steady vacuum at idle. A healthy engine at sea level idles around 17\u201322 in-Hg with the needle holding nearly steady. How the needle deviates from that baseline is the diagnosis \u2014 the pattern matters more than the number.\n\nThe honest verdict: a vacuum gauge won't fix anything, but it will stop you from guessing. Most \"needs a carb rebuild\" complaints on these trucks turn out to be a cracked vacuum line, a leaking intake gasket, or timing that drifted. The gauge finds those in five minutes.\n\nA standard 0\u201330 in-Hg engine vacuum gauge, a length of vacuum hose, and a brass tee. You want a *manifold* vacuum source \u2014 a port on the intake manifold base or the carburetor body below the throttle plates \u2014 not a ported (timed) source above the throttle plates, which reads near zero at idle. The PCV port or the power-brake booster line are reliable manifold sources on most early Bronco intakes.\n\nA tachometer helps because some readings change with rpm. Phoenix and other high-altitude areas read lower across the board: subtract roughly 1 in-Hg per 1,000 feet of elevation from the sea-level baseline, so a healthy engine in Phoenix (~1,100 ft) idles closer to 16\u201321 in-Hg.\n\nWarm the engine to operating temperature and set a stable idle. Connect the gauge to a manifold vacuum port with the tee, or directly if you have a spare port. Read the needle at idle, then snap the throttle open and let it close, and watch the recovery.\n\nSteady needle at 17\u201322 (sea level): healthy. A low but steady reading, say 12\u201315, points to retarded ignition timing or a general loss of compression. A needle that drops 4\u20136 in-Hg at a regular, rhythmic interval is almost always a burnt or leaking valve \u2014 the drop happens every time that cylinder fires. A rapid, fluttering needle of 2\u20134 in-Hg at idle that steadies as rpm climbs points to worn valve guides. A floating or drifting needle that won't sit still usually means a vacuum leak at the intake gasket or a carb base gasket, or an idle mixture set too lean.\n\nSnap the throttle and release: the needle should drop near zero, then jump to 22\u201325 before settling. If it barely recovers past the idle reading, the rings or exhaust are restricting flow. To separate the two, hold a steady 2,000 rpm \u2014 if vacuum slowly falls off, the exhaust is clogged (collapsed pipe or a packed muffler/cat where one was added).\n\nThe single most common mistake is reading a ported vacuum source and panicking at the near-zero idle reading. Confirm you're on a manifold port that reads high at idle and drops when you open the throttle.\n\nDon't chase a fluttering needle into a carburetor rebuild before checking for vacuum leaks. Spray a little carb cleaner around the intake-to-head and carb-to-intake gaskets with the engine running \u2014 if idle changes, you found an unmetered air leak. On a 50-year-old truck, brittle vacuum hoses and a warped intake are far more likely than a bad carb.\n\nRemember the altitude correction. A Phoenix Bronco reading 16 in-Hg is not sick; it's normal for the elevation.\n\nA serviceable vacuum gauge from Actron, OTC, or Innova runs $20\u201330; combination compression/vacuum kits run $35\u201340. The hose and tee assortment is about $12. You probably don't need a premium gauge \u2014 the cheap ones read accurately enough to spot the patterns, since you're watching needle behavior against a baseline, not certifying an absolute number. Print a vacuum diagnosis chart and tape it inside the toolbox lid; the patterns are quick to forget between uses."},{"id":"bronco-tires-at","title":"All-Terrain Tires \u2014 The Street-and-Trail Default","vehicle":"bronco","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tires_at","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":800,"high":1500},"tools_required":["torque wrench","tire shop mount/balance","tape measure","tire pressure gauge"],"skills_required":["none"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["tires","all-terrain","wheels_tires"],"parts":[{"name":"All-terrain tires, set of 5 (31x10.50R15, Load Range C)","vendor":"BFGoodrich KO2/KO3 / Falken Wildpeak AT3W / Cooper Discoverer AT3 XLT","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"All-terrain tires, set of 5 (33x12.50R15, Load Range C)","vendor":"BFGoodrich KO2/KO3 / Falken Wildpeak AT3W / Toyo Open Country AT3","price_estimate_usd":1450},{"name":"Mount, balance, and new valve stems (per tire)","vendor":"local tire shop","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Dana 20 speedometer drive gear (recalibration)","vendor":"Wild Horses 4x4 / Bronco Graveyard","price_estimate_usd":30}],"citations":[{"title":"Wild Horses 4x4 \u2014 early Bronco tire & wheel guide","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/early-bronco-tire-and-wheel-guide"},{"title":"Sexton Offroad \u2014 ultimate early Bronco parts guide","url":"https://www.sextonoffroad.com/blogs/4x4-parts-accessories/the-ultimate-early-ford-bronco-parts-guide-build-your-dream-1966-1977-rig"},{"title":"Kincer Chassis \u2014 understanding the axles on an early Ford Bronco","url":"https://kincerchassis.com/axles-on-an-early-ford-bronco/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-wt-tire-sizing","bronco-tires-mt","bronco-wt-wheel-backspacing","bronco-axle-gear-ratio"],"summary":"A 31x10.50R15 or 33x12.50R15 all-terrain in Load Range C is the right tire for the overwhelming majority of early Broncos. It has real dirt and wet-road grip, it stays quiet enough to drive four hours to the trailhead, and \u2014 the part most buyers miss \u2014 it is light and soft enough that a 4,000-pound truck with a small V8, a manual steering box, and possibly front drum brakes can still stop, steer, and accelerate. The 33 is the sweet spot. Go bigger only when the terrain genuinely demands it.","description":"The early Bronco is 92 inches of wheelbase and roughly 3,600 to 4,200 pounds depending on hardtop, engine, and how much steel armor is bolted to it. That combination is why the truck wheels so far above its tire size \u2014 the approach and breakover angles are enormous, and there is very little mass to push through a mud hole. It does not need a giant tire to be capable, and every inch of diameter you add costs you somewhere else.\n\nThe costs on this chassis are steeper than on a modern truck, because the early Bronco has no power assist for most of what a big tire loads up:\n\n**Steering.** A large share of these trucks still wear the factory manual box, and many \"power\" conversions are a Saginaw box with modest assist. A 33 in a soft compound is noticeably heavier at parking speeds than a 31; a 35 is a different vehicle entirely. If your Bronco is manual-steering, treat the 33 as the practical ceiling until you address the box \u2014 see the [power steering conversion guide](/db/bronco/bronco-steering-power-conversion/).\n\n**Brakes.** Early Broncos left the factory with front drums through 1975. Drums plus a taller, heavier tire is a measurable stopping-distance penalty, not a theoretical one. If you are running 33s on four-wheel drums, the [front disc conversion](/db/bronco/bronco-brakes-front-disc-swap/) is a higher-priority purchase than the tires.\n\n**Gearing.** Factory ratios are 3.50, 4.11, or 4.57. A 31 on 3.50s is livable; a 33 on 3.50s is lazy and hunts on grades; a 33 on 4.11s is well-matched. Read the [gear ratio guide](/db/bronco/bronco-axle-gear-ratio/) before you buy, not after.\n\nAn all-terrain sidesteps most of this because it is the lightest and lowest-rolling-resistance tire that still carries usable trail tread.\n\nThese numbers match the dedicated [tire sizing reference](/db/bronco/bronco-wt-tire-sizing/) \u2014 use that guide for the full matrix and this card for the buying decision.\n\n- **31x10.50R15** \u2014 stock height to 2 inches of lift, no trimming to minimal, 3.50 gears acceptable and 4.11 better.\n- **32x11.50R15** \u2014 about 2 inches of lift, light fender lip trim, 4.11 gears.\n- **33x12.50R15** \u2014 2.5 to 3 inches of lift, fender lip trim or a cut-and-fold at the rear of the front opening, 4.11 minimum and 4.56 strong.\n\nBackspacing matters as much as lift and gets ignored more often. The early Bronco runs a 5x5.5 inch (5x139.7) bolt pattern, and the target for a 15x8 wheel is about 3.75 inches of backspacing. The factory wheel is deeper than that and tucks a wide modern tire straight into the radius arm, where it grinds the sidewall at flex \u2014 a contact point out of sight from the driver's seat and unfixable by fender trimming. The [backspacing guide](/db/bronco/bronco-wt-wheel-backspacing/) has the full explanation; the short version is that you settle the wheel before you order rubber.\n\nBefore committing to a size, mount one tire and cycle the truck: jack the axle to full bump with the tire on, turn the wheel to full lock both directions, and look for contact at the rear of the front fender opening, the radius arm, and the frame rail. One tire proves the fit. Then order five.\n\nThis is the most common mistake on a classic 4x4. Tire shops default to Load Range E because it is what half-ton and three-quarter-ton trucks take, and E-rated tires are marketed as tougher. On an early Bronco, E is the wrong call.\n\nAn E-rated 33 carries a 10-ply-equivalent casing designed to hold up a 6,000-plus-pound truck. A 4,000-pound Bronco cannot deflect that sidewall, so the tire rides harsh on the road and, more important, refuses to air down usefully on the trail. At 15 psi an E-rated tire under a light truck barely bulges, which throws away the single biggest free traction gain available to you.\n\nE casings also weigh roughly 8 to 12 pounds more per tire than the C version of the same size. Across five tires that is 40 to 60 pounds of unsprung and rotating mass added to a truck with modest brakes and a small engine.\n\nLoad Range C in a 33x12.50R15 is rated in the neighborhood of 2,000 pounds per tire. Four of those carry an early Bronco with a wide margin, even loaded for a week out. Buy C, and spend the difference on a fifth matching tire so the spare still works behind a locker.\n\nReference weights are worth internalizing: a 31x10.50R15 all-terrain runs roughly 44 to 48 pounds, a 33x12.50R15 around 55 to 60, and a 35-inch mud-terrain 68 to 75. That spread is why a 33 all-terrain and a 35 mud-terrain feel like different trucks from behind the wheel.\n\n**BFGoodrich All-Terrain T/A** \u2014 the long-standing default. The KO2 is being superseded by the KO3, and both are offered in the classic 15-inch sizes early Broncos want. Tough three-ply sidewall, long tread life, predictable in wet. The most expensive of the mainstream options.\n\n**Falken Wildpeak AT3W** \u2014 the value pick and hard to argue against. Close to BFG capability for meaningfully less money, quieter on the highway, strong in snow. The tire many builders land on after pricing the KO2.\n\n**Cooper Discoverer AT3 XLT** \u2014 quiet, long-wearing, good on-road manners. The better choice for a Bronco that spends most of its life on pavement and graded fire roads.\n\n**Toyo Open Country AT3** \u2014 a solid middle option with strong wet and winter performance and a reputation for even wear.\n\nAll four are offered in 31x10.50R15 and 33x12.50R15, which is the real constraint worth checking. The 15-inch light-truck market is smaller than it was, so confirm current availability in your exact size before you settle on a wheel diameter.\n\n**Tires age out before they wear out on this truck.** A Bronco that covers 2,000 miles a year will reach ten years on a set with most of the tread intact. Rubber does not care about tread depth. Read the DOT date code on the sidewall \u2014 the last four digits are week and year \u2014 and plan to replace on age somewhere between six and ten years regardless of how the tread looks. A 50-year-old truck on 12-year-old tires is a highway blowout waiting for its moment.\n\n**The spare is a real problem to solve.** A 33 will not stow the way the factory spare did, and it is heavy enough that a tailgate-hung carrier stresses the hinges over time. Decide the carrier before the tires land \u2014 see the [rear bumper and tire carrier guide](/db/bronco/bronco-armor-rear-bumper/).\n\n**The speedometer will read slow.** The early Bronco's speedometer is cable-driven off the Dana 20 transfer case, so the correction is a physical drive gear swap, not an electronic recalibration. Moving from a factory-diameter tire to a 33 leaves you roughly 10 percent optimistic on both speed and odometer. The gear is inexpensive and the fix keeps the mileage on the truck honest.\n\n**Air down and mean it.** The whole reason to run Load Range C is that it will deform. On a light truck, 15 to 18 psi transforms the footprint on rock and sand. Carry a gauge and a way to air back up \u2014 that is the cheapest capability upgrade in this entire guide.\n\n- 31x10.50R15 all-terrain, set of five: **$950\u20131,300** depending on brand\n- 33x12.50R15 all-terrain, set of five: **$1,250\u20131,700**\n- Mount, balance, new valve stems: **$25\u201340 per tire**, so $125\u2013200 for five\n- Dana 20 speedometer drive gear: **$25\u201340**\n- Fender lip trimming, DIY consumables (cutoff wheel, primer, paint): **about $20**\n\nA realistic all-in for a set of 33s on an early Bronco is **$1,450 to $1,950** before wheels. If the budget forces a choice between 33s now and the front disc conversion, take the 31s and the brakes \u2014 the truck will be better in every measurable way. Step up to a [mud-terrain](/db/bronco/bronco-tires-mt/) only when your terrain is genuinely rock and deep mud, and go in knowing that tire drags lift, fender work, and a regear behind it."},{"id":"bronco-tires-mt","title":"Mud-Terrain Tires \u2014 For the Serious Trail Bronco","vehicle":"bronco","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tires_mt","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":1100,"high":2000},"tools_required":["torque wrench","tire shop mount/balance","tape measure","tire pressure gauge"],"skills_required":["none"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["tires","mud-terrain","wheels_tires"],"parts":[{"name":"Mud-terrain tires, set of 5 (33x12.50R15)","vendor":"BFGoodrich KM3 / Nitto Trail Grappler / Cooper Discoverer STT Pro","price_estimate_usd":1500},{"name":"Mud-terrain tires, set of 5 (35x12.50R15)","vendor":"BFGoodrich KM3 / Nitto Trail Grappler / Maxxis Trepador","price_estimate_usd":1750},{"name":"Mount, balance, and new valve stems (per tire)","vendor":"local tire shop","price_estimate_usd":40},{"name":"Chromoly rear axle shafts, 31-spline, large-bearing conversion","vendor":"Currie / Yukon / Moser","price_estimate_usd":650}],"citations":[{"title":"Wild Horses 4x4 \u2014 early Bronco tire & wheel guide","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/early-bronco-tire-and-wheel-guide"},{"title":"Kincer Chassis \u2014 understanding the axles on an early Ford Bronco","url":"https://kincerchassis.com/axles-on-an-early-ford-bronco/"},{"title":"Sexton Offroad \u2014 ultimate early Bronco parts guide","url":"https://www.sextonoffroad.com/blogs/4x4-parts-accessories/the-ultimate-early-ford-bronco-parts-guide-build-your-dream-1966-1977-rig"}],"alternatives":["bronco-tires-at","bronco-regear","bronco-wt-tire-sizing","bronco-axle-dana44-front"],"summary":"A mud-terrain is the correct tire for an early Bronco built to wheel hard rock and deep mud, and on this short, light chassis it is startlingly effective. It is also the tire that writes checks the rest of the truck has to cash: 3.5 to 4 inches of lift, cut fenders, a mandatory regear, likely stronger axle shafts, and a wet-pavement grip penalty on a vehicle that has neither ABS nor much brake. If your terrain is mixed, the [all-terrain](/db/bronco/bronco-tires-at/) is the smarter tire. If your terrain is rock and mud, read the whole bill below before you order.","description":"The difference between an all-terrain and a mud-terrain is void ratio \u2014 the proportion of the tread that is open space. An MT runs large, widely spaced lugs so that mud packs in and then throws clear as the tire rotates, instead of filling the tread and turning the tire into a slick. That self-cleaning behavior is the whole point, and no all-terrain replicates it.\n\nOn rock, the gain is different and comes from two things: aggressive shoulder and sidewall lugs that grab when the tire is aired down and rolled onto its edge, and a heavier three-ply casing that survives sharp granite. On a 4,000-pound early Bronco aired down to 12 to 15 psi, a mud-terrain wraps a rock in a way that is genuinely hard to believe until you have driven it.\n\nThe Bronco's light weight is an advantage here that heavier rigs do not share. It floats over ground that bogs a full-size truck, and the short 92-inch wheelbase lets the aggressive tread find purchase on ledges that a longer rig has to attack at an angle.\n\n**Lift and body work.** A 33-inch mud-terrain generally measures slightly taller and wider than a 33 all-terrain of the same nominal size, and its stiffer sidewall bulges less under compression. Plan on 3 to 3.5 inches for a 33 MT, and 3.5 to 4 inches plus aggressive trimming for a 35 \u2014 often with a body lift or a radius-arm drop strategy to buy the rest. The [tire sizing reference](/db/bronco/bronco-wt-tire-sizing/) has the full matrix.\n\n**Regear, and treat it as mandatory.** Factory ratios on these trucks are 3.50, 4.11, or 4.57. A 35 on 3.50 gears makes a 302 feel gutless, runs hot, and abuses an automatic's torque converter. The documented early Bronco baseline is 4.56 with 35s; a 33 lands at 4.11 to 4.56; a heavy 35 behind a small V8 sometimes wants 4.88. Both axles get the same ratio, or the Dana 20 binds. See the [regear guide](/db/bronco/bronco-regear/).\n\n**Axle shafts \u2014 and a correction worth reading.** Every early Bronco from 1966 through 1977 came from the factory with a **Ford 9-inch rear axle**, not a Dana 44. The Dana in these trucks is the front: a closed-knuckle Dana 30 through 1971, then a closed-knuckle Dana 44 from mid-1971 to 1977. This matters practically, because it changes what you should spend money on:\n\n- The 9-inch **housing and third member are not the weak link** \u2014 that design is strong enough that people put it under race cars. The weak link is the shafts. Most early Broncos use 28-spline small-bearing axles, and those are what twist and snap behind a locker on 35s. The upgrade is 31-spline chromoly shafts with a large-bearing conversion (Currie, Yukon, Moser), roughly $550 to $800 a pair installed \u2014 not a housing swap.\n- On a semi-float 9-inch, a shaft that breaks outboard of the bearing can let the wheel leave the truck. This is the reason to take shaft strength seriously rather than treating it as a durability nicety.\n- The **front axle is usually the real limit on 35s.** A closed-knuckle Dana 30 (1966\u201371) is the one that breaks under a 35 and a front locker; the common fix is the 1971\u201377 Dana 44 front, then chromoly inner shafts beyond that. See the [Dana 44 front axle guide](/db/bronco/bronco-axle-dana44-front/).\n\n**Steering and brakes.** A 35-inch mud-terrain weighs 68 to 75 pounds and has enormous rolling resistance at parking speeds. On a manual box this is genuinely unpleasant; budget the [power steering conversion](/db/bronco/bronco-steering-power-conversion/). If the truck still has front drums, the [disc conversion](/db/bronco/bronco-brakes-front-disc-swap/) stops being optional.\n\n**BFGoodrich Mud-Terrain T/A KM3** \u2014 the current benchmark for rock. Excellent sidewall durability, strong aired-down behavior, and better street manners than a mud-terrain has any right to have. Expensive.\n\n**Nitto Trail Grappler** \u2014 the quiet one. Noticeably less road drone than most MTs, which matters if the Bronco drives to the trail. Slightly less aggressive in deep mud than the KM3.\n\n**Cooper Discoverer STT Pro** \u2014 strong in mud and soft ground, good value, a bit louder.\n\n**Maxxis Trepador** \u2014 a dedicated rock tire in the classic sizes. Sticky, aggressive, and short-lived on pavement. For a trailered or trail-primary Bronco.\n\n**Falken Wildpeak M/T** \u2014 the value entry, and a reasonable choice if the truck sees moderate use rather than hard rock.\n\n**Wet pavement is the real safety tradeoff, not noise.** Large tread blocks mean less rubber contacting the road and channels that evacuate water poorly at speed. A mud-terrain on a wet highway, on a truck with drum rear brakes, no ABS, and a 92-inch wheelbase, is a meaningfully longer stopping distance and a twitchier truck. Slow down in the rain, and treat this as the reason a street-driven Bronco should stay on all-terrains.\n\n**Snow and ice are worse still** unless the tire carries the three-peak mountain snowflake rating. Most classic mud-terrains do not. Large voids pack with snow and the compound stiffens in cold.\n\n**Wear and balance.** Expect 30,000 to 40,000 miles, versus 50,000-plus from a good all-terrain. Aggressive tread also invites cupping if alignment or shocks are tired, so rotate on a schedule and get a road-force balance \u2014 on a light truck with a solid front axle, an out-of-balance 35 will find a wheel-hop resonance you cannot ignore.\n\n**Run five, not four.** Behind a locker, a mismatched spare loads the differential and the driveline. Buy the fifth tire with the set, and settle the carrier before the tires arrive \u2014 see the [rear bumper and tire carrier guide](/db/bronco/bronco-armor-rear-bumper/).\n\n**Air down further than you think.** These tires are designed to work deformed. On a light Bronco, 12 to 15 psi on rock is where the sidewall lugs start earning their money. Carry a way to air back up.\n\n- 33x12.50R15 mud-terrain, set of five: **$1,300\u20131,750**\n- 35x12.50R15 mud-terrain, set of five: **$1,550\u20132,100**\n- Mount, balance, valve stems: **$30\u201345 per tire**, so $150\u2013225 for five\n- Regear, both axles, gears plus master kits plus setup labor: **$1,200\u20131,800**\n- 31-spline chromoly rear shafts with large-bearing conversion: **$550\u2013800** a pair\n- Fender cutting and flare work: **$100\u2013400** depending on whether you buy flares\n\nThe honest total for putting 35-inch mud-terrains on an early Bronco correctly \u2014 tires, gears, and shafts \u2014 lands between **$3,300 and $4,700**, before the lift and before any steering or brake work. That number is why so many well-sorted early Broncos end up on 33s. Built around properly, though, the mud-terrain is what makes a short-wheelbase Bronco a genuine rock rig, and there is no substitute for it in mud."},{"id":"bronco-wheels-upgrade","title":"Wheels \u2014 Sizing, Backspacing, and Material","vehicle":"bronco","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"wheels","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":500,"high":1400},"tools_required":["torque wrench","tire shop mount/balance","straightedge","tape measure"],"skills_required":["measuring backspacing","torquing to spec"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["wheels","wheels_tires"],"parts":[{"name":"15x8 steel wheel, 5x5.5, 3.75 in backspacing (set of 5)","vendor":"US Wheel / Vision / Wild Horses 4x4","price_estimate_usd":500},{"name":"15x8 cast aluminum wheel, 5x5.5, 3.75 in backspacing (set of 5)","vendor":"Method / Pro Comp / Trail-Gear","price_estimate_usd":950},{"name":"Lug nuts, 1/2-20 conical 60-degree seat (set of 20)","vendor":"Dorman / Gorilla / McGard","price_estimate_usd":40},{"name":"Hub-centric rings (only if the wheel bore is oversize)","vendor":"Coyote / Motorsport Tech","price_estimate_usd":25}],"citations":[{"title":"Wild Horses 4x4 \u2014 early Bronco tire & wheel guide","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/early-bronco-tire-and-wheel-guide"},{"title":"Sexton Offroad \u2014 ultimate early Bronco parts guide","url":"https://www.sextonoffroad.com/blogs/4x4-parts-accessories/the-ultimate-early-ford-bronco-parts-guide-build-your-dream-1966-1977-rig"},{"title":"Kincer Chassis \u2014 understanding the axles on an early Ford Bronco","url":"https://kincerchassis.com/axles-on-an-early-ford-bronco/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-wt-wheel-backspacing","bronco-wt-beadlocks","bronco-wt-tire-sizing","bronco-wt-wheel-spacers-adapters"],"summary":"Buy a 15x8 in the factory 5x5.5 inch pattern with roughly 3.75 inches of backspacing, in steel or a load-rated cast aluminum, and you will fit almost any early Bronco running 31s through 35s. The tire gets the attention, but the wheel underneath it decides whether that tire clears the radius arms at full stuff, how heavy the steering feels, and how hard the front wheel bearings work. Settle the wheel before you order rubber \u2014 the wrong backspacing turns a correctly sized tire into a rubbing, bearing-eating problem that no amount of fender trimming fixes.","description":"The early Bronco runs a **5 on 5.5 inch (5x139.7) bolt pattern** on **1/2-20 studs with conical, 60-degree seat lug nuts**. That pattern is shared with a great many Jeeps, period Ford half-tons, and Dodges, which is why the aftermarket for these trucks is as deep as it is.\n\nTwo things to confirm before you buy:\n\n**Verify the pattern on the truck, not from the year.** These are 50-year-old vehicles and a previous owner's axle swap can have changed the pattern without changing anything visible. Measure across the studs on a five-lug pattern by going from the center of one stud to the *far edge* of the stud two positions over \u2014 on a 5x5.5 that measurement is 5.5 inches.\n\n**Check the center bore.** The wheel's center hole has to clear the axle hub register or the wheel will not seat flat, and no amount of lug torque will fix that. Many aftermarket 5x5.5 wheels are made with a generously oversize bore and are lug-centric \u2014 the conical lug seats do the centering. That works, but it makes clean seat contact and a proper torque sequence more important, not less. If a wheel is meaningfully oversize and you want it hub-centric, a $25 set of rings solves it.\n\nBackspacing is the distance from the wheel's mounting face to the back lip of the rim. Measure it by laying a straightedge across the back of the wheel and running a tape from the straightedge down to the mounting pad.\n\nMore backspacing tucks the tire inboard toward the suspension. Less backspacing pushes it outboard toward the fender. The factory early Bronco wheel is deeper than modern wide tires want, which is why nearly every lifted build moves to a shallower wheel.\n\nThe target for a lifted early Bronco on 33s or 35s is **about 3.75 inches on a 15x8**. Both directions off that number cost you something specific:\n\n- **Deeper than about 4 inches** and the tire's inner shoulder contacts the radius arm and frame at articulation. That is the worst kind of rub \u2014 it happens off-road where you cannot see it, it chews the sidewall, and fender work does nothing about it.\n- **Shallower than about 3.5 inches** and the tire pokes past the flare. That throws mud down the side of the truck, and more importantly it increases the scrub radius, which levers the front wheel bearings and kingpins harder, adds steering effort on a truck that may not have power assist, and accelerates wear in the [closed-knuckle front end](/db/bronco/bronco-maint-closed-knuckle-service/).\n\nIf you want to translate backspacing to the offset number a wheel is often advertised with, the arithmetic is: **offset = backspacing \u2212 (rim width + 1 inch) / 2**. The extra inch accounts for the two bead flanges, which are not included in the stated rim width. For a 15x8 at 3.75 inches of backspacing that works out to roughly \u22120.75 inch, or about **\u221219 mm** \u2014 a mildly negative offset. The [dedicated backspacing guide](/db/bronco/bronco-wt-wheel-backspacing/) walks through the fitment consequences in more detail.\n\nWhatever number you settle on, **buy one wheel and one tire first.** Mount them, jack the axle to full bump, turn the steering to full lock in both directions, and look for contact at the radius arm, the frame rail, and the rear of the front fender opening. One corner proves the fit. Then order the other four.\n\nA **15-inch wheel** keeps a tall, flexible sidewall that airs down beautifully and absorbs rock strikes that would dent a rim. On a light truck like this, sidewall is capability, and the 15 is the better wheeling choice as well as the correct period look.\n\nA **17-inch** buys you two things and costs you one. It buys a wider modern tire selection \u2014 the 15-inch light-truck market has been shrinking \u2014 and it buys room for large brake calipers. It costs you sidewall.\n\nThat second point is a real trap worth planning around: **some front disc brake conversion kits will not clear a 15-inch wheel**, and a few will not clear a 16. If a [front disc conversion](/db/bronco/bronco-brakes-front-disc-swap/) is anywhere in your plan, confirm the kit's minimum wheel diameter and its caliper clearance profile before you spend money on wheels. Buying wheels and then discovering the brakes will not fit behind them is an expensive sequence to run in that order.\n\nFor width, match the rim to the tire's recommended range. A 33x12.50 or 35x12.50 wants an 8 to 10 inch rim, and the 8 is the versatile middle: it supports the tread without stretching the bead or pinching it. A 12.50-section tire on a narrow 7-inch rim balloons the sidewall outward and makes fender contact worse, not better.\n\n**Steel** is cheap, strong, and forgiving. Its defining trail advantage is that it bends rather than cracks: a wheel that takes a hard rock hit will usually deform and still hold air, and can often be hammered close enough to true to drive home. Steel is heavier, rusts where the coating is chipped, and offers a narrower range of backspacings off the shelf. For a working trail Bronco it remains the honest value choice.\n\n**Cast aluminum** saves roughly 5 to 8 pounds per wheel, looks sharp, and comes in the widest range of widths and backspacings. The tradeoff is failure mode: cast aluminum cracks rather than bends, and a cracked wheel loses air immediately and cannot be field-repaired. Check the load rating stamped on the wheel \u2014 some lightweight street-oriented designs are not rated for a heavy trail tire and repeated impact.\n\n**Forged aluminum** gets you light weight with a much better impact tolerance, at two to three times the price. It is worth the money on a competition rig and difficult to justify on a weekend truck.\n\n**True beadlocks** clamp the outer bead mechanically so the tire stays seated at very low pressure. On a light Bronco that airs down to 12 psi on rock, they work. They are also not DOT-approved, are illegal for street use in many jurisdictions, require periodic ring-bolt re-torquing, and complicate every tire change. Most builds do not need them \u2014 the [beadlock guide](/db/bronco/bronco-wt-beadlocks/) walks through when they are worth it.\n\nClean the hub face and the wheel mounting face before the wheel goes on. Fifty years of rust scale under a wheel is a real cause of lugs loosening and of vibration that no balance job cures.\n\nTorque **1/2-20 lugs to 85 to 100 lb-ft** in a star pattern, in two or three passes, with the wheel off the ground for the first pass and on the ground for the final one. Do not use an impact gun for the final torque, and do not put anti-seize or oil on the studs \u2014 a lubricated thread reaches far higher clamping load than the same torque reading on a dry thread, which is how studs get stretched and snapped.\n\n**Re-torque after the first 50 to 100 miles**, and again after the first trail run. New wheels seat. If you are running spacers or adapters, that re-torque schedule stops being optional \u2014 see the [spacers and adapters guide](/db/bronco/bronco-wt-wheel-spacers-adapters/) for whether you need them at all. The better answer is almost always a wheel with the correct backspacing from the start.\n\n- 15x8 steel wheels, 5x5.5: **$80\u2013115 each**, so **$400\u2013575** for a set of five\n- 15x8 cast aluminum: **$150\u2013250 each**, or **$750\u20131,250** for five\n- Forged aluminum: **$300\u2013450 each**\n- True beadlocks: **$250\u2013400 each**, plus periodic re-torque labor\n- Lug nuts, 1/2-20 conical, set of 20: **$30\u201350**\n- Hub-centric rings, if needed: **$20\u201330** a set\n- Mount and balance onto new wheels: **$25\u201340 per tire**\n\nA realistic set of five steel 15x8s with correct backspacing, new lug nuts, and mounting lands around **$600 to $775** \u2014 the cheapest correct decision on this page. Put the savings into tires, where it changes how the truck behaves."},{"id":"bronco-wt-beadlocks","title":"Beadlock Wheels on an Early Bronco \u2014 Do You Need Them?","vehicle":"bronco","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"wheels","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":800,"high":2400},"tools_required":["torque wrench (in-lb and ft-lb)","tire machine or manual mount/dismount tools","wire brush","thread locker"],"skills_required":["mounting tires","torquing a ring in sequence","periodic re-torque discipline"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["wheels_tires","wheels","beadlock","low pressure","rock crawling","wheels"],"parts":[{"name":"Single-beadlock wheel, 15x8 or 15x10, 5x5.5","vendor":"Trail-Gear / Method","price_estimate_usd":260},{"name":"Replacement beadlock ring bolt set","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Bead sealant","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"Beadlock basics and maintenance","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"},{"title":"DOT vs non-DOT beadlock legality","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-wt-wheel-backspacing","bronco-wt-tire-sizing"],"summary":"Most early Bronco owners do not need beadlocks. They exist to let you air a tire down to single-digit pressures for serious rock crawling without the tire bead unseating and burping off the rim. If your wheeling tops out at trails, forest roads, and the occasional rocky climb at 12\u201318 psi, a standard wheel holds the bead fine and you keep your money. If you're crawling technical rock at 6\u201310 psi and spinning tires against ledges, beadlocks earn their cost. Be honest about which one you are.","description":"A beadlock wheel clamps the outer tire bead between the rim and a bolt-on ring, so the tire physically cannot slip on the rim or unseat at very low pressure. That's the entire benefit: it lets you run the low pressures that put the most rubber on the rock for traction, without the tire walking off the wheel. Everything else about a beadlock \u2014 the cost, the maintenance, the legality questions \u2014 is a tradeoff you accept to get that one capability.\n\nBeadlock wheels in the 5x5.5 early Bronco pattern, a torque wrench that reads the ring-bolt range (usually in-lb to low ft-lb), and the discipline to re-torque the rings on a schedule. Single-beadlock wheels (outer bead clamped) are the standard; double-beadlocks exist but are overkill for a light truck like a Bronco. Match width and backspacing to your tire and lift the same way you would a standard wheel.\n\nMounting a tire on a beadlock is a process, not a quick job. Clean the ring and wheel mating surfaces, set the tire bead into the clamp area, lay the ring on evenly, and start every bolt by hand. Torque the ring bolts in a star/cross pattern in two or three passes to the wheel maker's spec \u2014 typically 12\u201320 ft-lb, and never all at once on one side, or you'll warp the ring and create a leak. Many builders add a thread locker and a thin bead of sealant.\n\nThe inner bead on a single-beadlock still seats with air pressure like a normal tire, so you air it up to seat the inner bead, then the outer bead is held mechanically and needs no high pressure to stay put.\n\nRe-torque the ring bolts after the first drive and then on a schedule \u2014 before every serious trip is the safe habit. Bolts back off as the tire and ring settle, and a loose ring leaks or, worse, lets the bead go.\n\nThe real cost of beadlocks is maintenance, not only the purchase. Dozens of ring bolts per wheel, all of which must be checked, is a recurring chore. Skipping the re-torque is how people lose a bead on the trail despite running beadlocks. If you won't keep up with the bolts, you're better off on standard wheels.\n\nLegality is a gray area. Many beadlock wheels are not DOT-approved for street use, and some states treat them as not street-legal. In practice enforcement varies, but know your local rules before you run them on a daily-driven Bronco. DOT-stamped beadlocks exist and cost more.\n\nWatch balance. Beadlocks are heavier and harder to balance than standard wheels; the rings add rotating mass. On a street-driven early Bronco that can show up as vibration. For a dedicated trail rig it's a non-issue.\n\nBeadlock wheels run roughly $200\u2013320 each, so a set lands between $800 and $1,600, and DOT-approved versions push toward $2,000\u20132,400 for a set. Standard steel or alloy wheels do everything most early Bronco owners need for a quarter of that. The honest recommendation: skip beadlocks unless you've actually unseated a bead at low pressure or you're building a dedicated rock rig. The money is better spent on tires, gearing, or a locker for the average trail Bronco."},{"id":"bronco-wt-tire-sizing","title":"Tire Sizing for the Early Bronco \u2014 What Fits With What Lift","vehicle":"bronco","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"fitment","type":"reference","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":0},"cost_usd":{"low":800,"high":2000},"tools_required":["tape measure","trim/cutoff tool (if trimming fenders)"],"skills_required":["measuring clearance","understanding gearing tradeoffs"],"build_tiers":["stock_resto","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["wheels_tires","tires","tire size","fitment","lift","gearing","tires"],"parts":[{"name":"31x10.50R15 all-terrain (stock-ish fit)","vendor":"BFG / Cooper / Falken","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"33x12.50R15 all-terrain (most popular EB size)","vendor":"BFG / Cooper / Toyo","price_estimate_usd":270},{"name":"35x12.50R15 mud-terrain","vendor":"BFG / Nitto","price_estimate_usd":330}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco tire fitment guide","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"},{"title":"Tire size vs lift and gearing","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-wt-wheel-backspacing","bronco-axle-gear-ratio","bronco-susp-2in-lift"],"summary":"The short answer for an early Bronco: 33s are the sweet spot. A 33x12.50R15 clears with a 2.5\u20133\" lift and modest trimming, fits the stock Dana 44s without re-gearing being mandatory, and keeps the truck driving like a truck instead of a tractor. 35s look great and crawl better but cost you gearing, steering effort, and fender real estate. 31s bolt under a near-stock truck. Pick the tire to match the lift and the gears you're willing to run, not the other way around.","description":"The early Bronco is small and light, which works in your favor \u2014 it doesn't take a tall lift to clear a big tire the way a full-size truck does. But the short wheelbase and the stock 3.50 or 4.11 gearing mean a tall tire changes the driving character fast. Every inch of tire diameter is a step away from the gearing the engine was tuned for.\n\nKnow three things before you buy: your lift height, your wheel backspacing, and your axle gear ratio. Tire fitment is the interaction of all three. A 33 that clears on a 3.75\" backspacing wheel will rub on a 4.5\" wheel because it sits further inboard into the radius arm and frame. See the companion guide on backspacing \u2014 it matters as much as lift for fitment.\n\nMatch the tire to the build:\n\n**31x10.50R15** fits a stock-height or 1\u20132\" lifted early Bronco with stock backspacing, with little or no trimming. This is the choice for a stock restoration or a mild trail truck that still has to drive nicely on the highway with stock 3.50 gears. Minimal speedometer error, no steering penalty.\n\n**33x12.50R15** is the most popular early Bronco size and the one most builds settle on. It needs a 2.5\u20133\" suspension lift, a wheel in the 3.75\u20134.0\" backspacing range, and usually some fender lip trimming or a cut-and-fold at full stuff. With 3.50 gears a 33 is livable but lazy; with 4.11s it's well-matched; with 4.56s it's strong. This size balances clearance, ride, and street manners better than anything else on these trucks.\n\n**35x12.50R15** needs a 3.5\u20134\" lift (or a 2.5\u20133\" lift plus a body lift and aggressive trimming), and re-gearing is effectively mandatory \u2014 plan on 4.56 or 4.88 gears to keep the small-block out of the doldrums. Steering effort climbs noticeably; a power steering conversion or a hydraulic assist is worth budgeting. This is rock-crawler territory.\n\nFor each size, measure before you commit: stuff the suspension to full bump with the tire mounted and the wheel at full lock both directions, and look for contact at the rear of the front fender, the radius arm, and the frame. Trimming the fender lip and a tucked inner fender buys the clearance most builds need.\n\nThe biggest mistake is buying tall tires and skipping the gear change. A 35 on 3.50 gears makes the truck feel gutless, hot, and slow, and it hammers the automatic transmission's torque converter. Tire and gear decisions are one decision. Read the gear ratio guide alongside this one.\n\nWatch wheel backspacing \u2014 a deep-backspaced wheel tucks the tire into the radius arm and frame and causes inner rub that no amount of fender trimming fixes. Width also matters: a 12.50 section on a narrow rim balloons the sidewall outward and worsens fender contact.\n\nDon't forget the speedometer. Going from 31s to 33s or 35s makes the speedometer read slow; a recalibrated gear or an electronic correction keeps you legal and keeps the odometer honest.\n\nA set of five 31s runs roughly $1,000\u20131,250; 33s run $1,300\u20131,700; 35s run $1,600\u20132,000, with mud-terrains at the top of each range. Add gearing ($1,200\u20131,800 installed for both axles) if you go to 35s. You probably don't need 35s for the vast majority of trail driving \u2014 a 33 clears the same obstacles with less penalty everywhere else, and most early Broncos look and wheel their best on 33s."},{"id":"bronco-wt-wheel-backspacing","title":"Wheel Backspacing and Bolt Pattern on the Early Bronco","vehicle":"bronco","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"wheels","type":"reference","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":0},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":1400},"tools_required":["tape measure","straightedge"],"skills_required":["measuring backspacing","checking bolt pattern and bore"],"build_tiers":["stock_resto","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["wheels_tires","wheels","backspacing","bolt pattern","offset","wheels"],"parts":[{"name":"15x8 steel wheel, 5x5.5, 3.75 backspacing","vendor":"US Wheel / Vision","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"15x8 or 15x10 alloy wheel, 5x5.5","vendor":"Method / Pro Comp","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Wheel spacer / adapter (only if necessary)","vendor":"Spidertrax","price_estimate_usd":180}],"citations":[{"title":"Early Bronco wheel fitment and backspacing","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"},{"title":"5x5.5 bolt pattern reference","url":"https://www.wildhorses4x4.com/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-wt-tire-sizing","bronco-wt-beadlocks"],"summary":"The early Bronco uses a 5x5.5\" (5x139.7) bolt pattern, and the backspacing number you want for most builds is about 3.75\". That figure pushes the tire out far enough to clear the radius arms and frame at full stuff without throwing the tire so far outboard that it sticks past the fenders and loads the wheel bearings. Get backspacing wrong and a correctly sized tire will still rub \u2014 it matters as much as lift.","description":"Backspacing is the distance from the wheel's mounting face to the back lip of the rim. More backspacing tucks the tire inboard toward the suspension; less backspacing pushes it outboard toward the fender. The stock early Bronco wheel ran a deep backspacing that tucks modern wide tires right into the radius arms, which is why nearly every lifted build moves to a shallower-backspaced wheel.\n\nThe direct answer: a 15x8 wheel with 3.75\" backspacing in the factory 5x5.5 pattern fits the widest range of early Bronco builds running 31s through 35s. Start there unless you have a specific reason not to.\n\nConfirm the bolt pattern first. Early Broncos are 5x5.5\" \u2014 the same pattern as many Jeeps, full-size Fords of the era, and Dodges, which gives you a wide aftermarket. Measure or confirm before buying; a previous owner's axle swap can change the pattern. Check the center bore too: the wheel's center hole must clear the axle hub register, or the wheel won't seat flat no matter how you torque the lugs.\n\nPick rim width to match the tire. A 33x12.50 or 35x12.50 wants an 8\" to 10\" rim; an 8\" rim is the versatile middle ground that supports the tire without overstretching or pinching the bead. Narrower rims balloon the sidewall outward and worsen fender rub.\n\nSet backspacing for clearance. On a typical lifted early Bronco running 33s or 35s, 3.75\" backspacing on a 15x8 (offset = backspacing minus half the overall rim width, so 3.75 - 4.5 = -0.75 inch, or about -19mm) clears the radius arms at full compression and full lock. Going shallower than about 3.5\" pushes the tire out past the fender flares and increases the leverage on the front wheel bearings and the closed-knuckle kingpins, which wears them faster and adds steering effort. Going deeper than 4\" risks the tire rubbing the radius arm and frame on the inner edge \u2014 a contact point that fender trimming cannot fix.\n\nTest-fit before you buy five. Mount one wheel and tire, cycle the suspension to full bump and the steering to full lock both ways, and check inner and outer clearance. Order the set once one wheel proves out.\n\nThe most common and most frustrating mistake is buying a wheel with too much backspacing because it \"looks tucked and clean,\" then finding the tire grinds the radius arm at flex. That contact happens off-road where you can't see it and chews the sidewall. Err toward the standard 3.75\" rather than going deep.\n\nAvoid solving a backspacing problem with thick wheel spacers. Spacers can correct a too-deep wheel, but they add stress to studs and bearings and need careful torque and periodic re-checks. A wheel with the right backspacing from the start is the cleaner answer. If you do run spacers or adapters, buy hub-centric units from a known maker and re-torque them after the first 50 miles.\n\nWatch the load rating on alloy wheels. Some lightweight street wheels aren't rated for a heavy tire and trail abuse. Steel wheels are cheap, strong, and bend rather than crack \u2014 a real advantage on the trail.\n\nSteel wheels in 5x5.5 run $80\u2013110 each; alloy and bead-capable wheels run $150\u2013250 each. A set of four to five wheels lands between $400 and $1,250 depending on material. Spacers or adapters, if genuinely needed, run $150\u2013220 a pair from a reputable maker like Spidertrax \u2014 and you probably don't need them if you buy the right backspacing the first time. Steel is the honest value choice for a trail truck; spend the wheel budget on tires instead."},{"id":"bronco-wt-wheel-spacers-adapters","title":"Wheel Spacers and Adapters \u2014 When They Help and When to Skip","vehicle":"bronco","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"fitment","type":"reference","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":300},"tools_required":["torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands","wire brush","thread locker"],"skills_required":["wheel torque procedure","hub cleaning"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","weekend_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1966-1977"],"tags":["wheel spacers","adapters","wheels_tires","backspacing","fitment","bolt pattern"],"parts":[{"name":"Hub-centric wheel spacers (5x5.5, pair)","vendor":"Spidertrax / Various","price_estimate_usd":160},{"name":"Bolt pattern adapters (e.g. 5x5.5 to 5x4.5)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"Extended wheel studs (if needed)","vendor":"ARP / Various","price_estimate_usd":40}],"safety_critical":true,"citations":[{"title":"Wheel Spacer Safety and Selection \u2014 fitment basics","url":"https://www.classicbroncos.com/forums/"}],"alternatives":["bronco-wt-wheel-backspacing","bronco-wt-tire-sizing"],"summary":"**Safety note.** Wheel spacers and adapters carry your truck's full weight and steering loads through extra bolted joints. Used correctly \u2014 hub-centric, properly torqued, with adequate stud engagement \u2014 they are a legitimate fitment tool. Used carelessly, they cause vibration, loosening, and in the worst case a wheel coming off. This guide covers when a spacer is the right answer and when a different wheel is the better one.","description":"Spacers push the wheel outward from the hub. Adapters do the same but also change the bolt pattern, letting a wheel with a different lug pattern fit your axle. Both solve real fitment problems, and both add a bolted joint that has to be done right.\n\nBackspacing measures from the wheel's mounting pad to the back inside lip; offset measures from the wheel's true centerline to the mounting pad, expressed in millimeters, positive toward the outside of the truck. A spacer doesn't change a wheel's stamped offset -- it changes the effective offset by pushing the whole wheel-and-tire assembly outward by the spacer's thickness. A 1.5\" (38mm) spacer on a wheel with +12mm offset gives you an effective offset around -26mm, worth doing the math on before you stack a spacer on a wheel that already runs aggressive negative offset, since the combination can push the tire well past the fender edge.\n\nTwo legitimate uses:\n\n**Clearing a wider tire or suspension component.** A lifted Bronco with a wider tire may rub the inner fender or a steering component at full lock. A modest spacer moves the tire outboard for clearance.\n\n**Widening track for stability.** Lift raises the center of gravity. Pushing the wheels outward widens the stance and improves stability on side slopes. This is a real benefit on a tall build.\n\nIf you are choosing wheels anyway, the better answer is almost always a wheel with the correct backspacing rather than a stock-backspace wheel plus a spacer. A properly backspaced wheel achieves the same outboard position with no added joint, no extra unsprung weight, and nothing to torque-check. Spacers are the right tool when you are keeping wheels you already own; new wheels make them unnecessary.\n\nAdapters that change bolt pattern are a compromise to make a wheel fit that was never designed for the axle. They work, but a wheel in the correct pattern is the cleaner build. Use adapters when the wheels you want only come in the wrong pattern, not as a default.\n\nIf you run spacers, the safety rules are non-negotiable:\n\n1. **Hub-centric, not lug-centric.** A hub-centric spacer locates on the axle hub and provides a new hub register for the wheel, so the wheel is centered by the hub, not by the lug nuts. This prevents vibration and uneven loading.\n\n2. **Adequate stud engagement.** The spacer's studs must thread into the wheel with full engagement. As a rule of thumb, full engagement means roughly 1.5x the stud diameter's worth of thread contact -- for a common 1/2\"-20 stud, that's about 3/4\" of thread engaged in the nut. Thin spacers that bolt over the existing studs must leave enough stud protruding to fully seat the wheel's lug nuts at that depth.\n\n3. **Torque in stages, then re-check.** Torque the spacer's hub studs to 65-70 ft-lb, mount the wheel, torque the wheel's lug nuts to 90-100 ft-lb in a star pattern (verify both against your hub's factory spec, since early Bronco values vary by axle), and re-check both after the first 50\u2013100 miles. Bolted joints settle; a re-torque catches loosening before it becomes dangerous.\n\n4. **Inspect periodically.** Make the spacer bolts part of your pre-trip check. A loose spacer announces itself as vibration or a clunk \u2014 do not ignore it.\n\nCheap lug-centric spacers are the failure mode to avoid. They rely on the lug nuts alone to center the wheel and tend to vibrate and loosen. Spend on hub-centric units from a known maker.\n\nStacking spacers, or running very thick spacers on factory studs without full engagement, is where wheels come off. If you need a lot of offset change, get the right wheel instead.\n\n- Hub-centric spacers (pair): $160\n- Bolt-pattern adapters (pair): $200\n- Extended studs if required: $40\n\nBudget $80\u2013$300 depending on type and quality. Buy hub-centric, torque correctly, and re-check \u2014 or sidestep the whole question with a correctly backspaced wheel."}];